window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["kubernetes"]=[{"id":"kubernetes-f0001","card":"q-kubernetes-001-what-kubernetes-is-and-what-it-solves","sec":"kubernetes-s002","q":"What is Kubernetes, and why is it used?","a":"<p>Kubernetes (K8s) is an open-source platform for automating deployment, scaling, and management of containerized apps. It is used for scalability, resilience, and portability, handling load balancing, self-healing, and resource optimization across any environment.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0002","card":"q-kubernetes-001-what-kubernetes-is-and-what-it-solves","sec":"kubernetes-s002","q":"What problems does Kubernetes solve in container orchestration?","a":"<p>It automates deployment, scaling, load balancing, and self-healing. It manages resources, service discovery, and configs, and coordinates containers across multiple hosts for high availability.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0003","card":"q-kubernetes-001-what-kubernetes-is-and-what-it-solves","sec":"kubernetes-s002","q":"Explain the difference between Docker and Kubernetes.","a":"<p>Docker builds and runs containers on one host. Kubernetes orchestrates many containers across a cluster, adding scaling, scheduling, and networking.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0004","card":"q-kubernetes-001-what-kubernetes-is-and-what-it-solves","sec":"kubernetes-s002","q":"How does Kubernetes differ from traditional virtualization?","a":"<p>Containers share the host OS, which makes them lighter and faster than VMs that run full guest OSes. Kubernetes optimizes this for scale.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0005","card":"q-kubernetes-001-what-kubernetes-is-and-what-it-solves","sec":"kubernetes-s002","q":"In container orchestration, what problem does Kubernetes solve that running containers with plain Docker on a server does not?","a":"<p>Docker builds and runs containers on a single host. Once you have many containers on many machines you need something to decide where each one runs, restart it when it dies, scale it, route traffic to it and roll out new versions. Kubernetes does this: scheduling, self-healing, scaling, service discovery, load balancing and rolling updates across a cluster of machines.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0006","card":"q-kubernetes-001-what-kubernetes-is-and-what-it-solves","sec":"kubernetes-s002","q":"What does \"declarative desired state\" mean in Kubernetes, and why does it matter?","a":"<p>You describe what you want (for example 3 replicas of an image) and Kubernetes controllers continuously compare actual state to desired state and fix the difference. If a Pod or node dies, the controller creates a replacement without anyone running a command. It makes systems self-healing and lets you keep the desired state in Git.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0007","card":"q-kubernetes-001-what-kubernetes-is-and-what-it-solves","sec":"kubernetes-s002","q":"How do containers differ from virtual machines, and why does that suit Kubernetes?","a":"<p>Containers share the host kernel and isolate processes with namespaces and cgroups. A VM runs a full guest OS on a hypervisor. Containers start in seconds, use less memory and pack more densely on a node, which makes fast scaling and rescheduling practical. The trade-off is weaker isolation than a VM, since the kernel is shared.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0008","card":"q-kubernetes-001-what-kubernetes-is-and-what-it-solves","sec":"kubernetes-s002","q":"When is Kubernetes the wrong choice?","a":"<p>For a small app with a few containers on one host, Kubernetes adds operational cost (cluster upgrades, networking, RBAC, monitoring) with little gain. Docker Compose, a single VM, or a managed service such as AWS ECS/Fargate or a serverless platform is simpler. Kubernetes pays off with many services, several teams, a need for autoscaling or portability across environments.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0009","card":"q-kubernetes-001-what-kubernetes-is-and-what-it-solves","sec":"kubernetes-s002","q":"Name the main capabilities Kubernetes gives you out of the box.","a":"<p>Scheduling Pods onto nodes, self-healing (restart, reschedule), horizontal scaling, rolling updates and rollbacks, service discovery and load balancing, configuration and secret management, persistent storage orchestration, and role-based access control.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0010","card":"q-kubernetes-002-containers-clusters-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s003","q":"What is a container, and how does Kubernetes manage containers?","a":"<p>A container is a lightweight, isolated app package with its dependencies. Kubernetes groups containers into Pods, schedules them on nodes, manages their lifecycle, and handles networking and scaling.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0011","card":"q-kubernetes-002-containers-clusters-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s003","q":"What is the role of a Kubernetes cluster?","a":"<p>A group of nodes running containers, managed as one system for deployment, scaling, and fault tolerance.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0012","card":"q-kubernetes-002-containers-clusters-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s003","q":"What is the difference between a node and a cluster?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Node</strong>: a single machine (master or worker).</li>\n  <li><strong>Cluster</strong>: all nodes working together under a control plane.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0013","card":"q-kubernetes-002-containers-clusters-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s003","q":"What is a Pod in Kubernetes?","a":"<p>The smallest unit. It runs one or more containers that share network (IP) and storage. Pods are ephemeral and managed by controllers.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0014","card":"q-kubernetes-002-containers-clusters-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s003","q":"What is a Namespace, and how is it used?","a":"<p>A logical partition that isolates resources, scopes access, and limits quotas for teams or projects.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0015","card":"q-kubernetes-002-containers-clusters-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s003","q":"What is the difference between a node and a cluster in Kubernetes?","a":"<p>A node is one machine (physical or virtual) that is part of Kubernetes. A cluster is the whole set of nodes, a control plane plus worker nodes, managed as one system. You schedule workloads onto the cluster and Kubernetes picks the node.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0016","card":"q-kubernetes-002-containers-clusters-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s003","q":"Why is the Pod, not the container, the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes?","a":"<p>A Pod wraps one or more tightly coupled containers that must run together on the same node. They share a network namespace (one IP, localhost) and can share volumes. Kubernetes schedules, scales and restarts Pods, and the container runtime handles the individual containers inside.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0017","card":"q-kubernetes-002-containers-clusters-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s003","q":"Pods are described as ephemeral. What follows from that for how you design an application?","a":"<p>A Pod can be killed, evicted or rescheduled at any time and gets a new IP when recreated. So you must not rely on a Pod's IP or local disk. Use a Service for stable access, a PersistentVolume for data that must survive, and a controller such as a Deployment to recreate Pods.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0018","card":"q-kubernetes-002-containers-clusters-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s003","q":"What are Namespaces used for, and what do they not give you?","a":"<p>Namespaces partition one cluster into logical groups for teams, environments or projects. They scope names, RBAC permissions and ResourceQuotas. They are not a hard security boundary: by default Pods in different namespaces can still talk to each other unless NetworkPolicies restrict it, and they share the same nodes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0019","card":"q-kubernetes-002-containers-clusters-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s003","q":"You need to give a team its own space in a shared cluster with a cap on CPU and memory. What do you set up?","a":"<p>Create a Namespace for the team, a ResourceQuota in it to cap total requests and limits, and optionally a LimitRange for per-container defaults. Then bind the team to a Role in that namespace with a RoleBinding so they can only manage their own namespace.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0020","card":"q-kubernetes-002-containers-clusters-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s003","q":"How do the containers in one Kubernetes Pod share an IP address, and what is the pause container?","a":"<p>Each Pod gets one network namespace. A small infrastructure container (the pause container) starts first and holds that namespace, and the app containers join it. So all containers in the Pod share one IP and port space and reach each other on <code>localhost</code>. Two containers in one Pod cannot listen on the same port. If an app container restarts, the namespace and IP stay because the pause container keeps running.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0021","card":"q-kubernetes-003-control-plane-and-worker-node-components","sec":"kubernetes-s004","q":"What are the key components of Kubernetes architecture?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Control Plane</strong>: kube-apiserver, etcd, kube-scheduler, kube-controller-manager.</li>\n  <li><strong>Worker Nodes</strong>: kubelet, kube-proxy, container runtime (for example Docker).</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0022","card":"q-kubernetes-003-control-plane-and-worker-node-components","sec":"kubernetes-s004","q":"What is a control plane, and what does it consist of?","a":"<p>The brain of the cluster. It manages state and scheduling and includes kube-apiserver, etcd, kube-scheduler, and kube-controller-manager.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0023","card":"q-kubernetes-003-control-plane-and-worker-node-components","sec":"kubernetes-s004","q":"Explain the difference between master nodes and worker nodes.","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Master</strong>: runs the control plane (manages the cluster).</li>\n  <li><strong>Worker</strong>: runs Pods (executes workloads).</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0024","card":"q-kubernetes-003-control-plane-and-worker-node-components","sec":"kubernetes-s004","q":"Which components make up the Kubernetes control plane and which run on every worker node?","a":"<p>Control plane: kube-apiserver, etcd, kube-scheduler, kube-controller-manager (plus cloud-controller-manager on cloud clusters). Worker node: kubelet, kube-proxy and a container runtime such as containerd or CRI-O.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0025","card":"q-kubernetes-003-control-plane-and-worker-node-components","sec":"kubernetes-s004","q":"In Kubernetes, why do the control plane and the worker nodes run different components?","a":"<p>Separation of duties. The control plane makes decisions and stores state (what should run and where). Worker nodes carry out those decisions by running Pods. If a worker fails, the cluster state is safe in the control plane. If the control plane is briefly down, existing Pods on workers keep running, but nothing new is scheduled or changed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0026","card":"q-kubernetes-003-control-plane-and-worker-node-components","sec":"kubernetes-s004","q":"Docker was commonly named as a Kubernetes container runtime. What is the current situation?","a":"<p>Since Kubernetes 1.24 the built-in dockershim was removed, so the kubelet talks to runtimes through the Container Runtime Interface (CRI), typically containerd or CRI-O. Images built with Docker still run, because they follow the OCI image format.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0027","card":"q-kubernetes-004-what-each-component-does","sec":"kubernetes-s005","q":"Explain the role of the kube-apiserver.","a":"<p>The central hub. It exposes the API, handles requests, authenticates and authorizes them, and updates etcd.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0028","card":"q-kubernetes-004-what-each-component-does","sec":"kubernetes-s005","q":"What does the kube-scheduler do?","a":"<p>It places Pods on nodes based on resources, policies, and constraints.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0029","card":"q-kubernetes-004-what-each-component-does","sec":"kubernetes-s005","q":"What is the purpose of the kube-controller-manager?","a":"<p>It runs controllers that keep the cluster state (for example replicas and nodes) as desired.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0030","card":"q-kubernetes-004-what-each-component-does","sec":"kubernetes-s005","q":"What is etcd, and why is it important?","a":"<p>A distributed store for cluster state. It is critical for consistency and control plane operations.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0031","card":"q-kubernetes-004-what-each-component-does","sec":"kubernetes-s005","q":"How does the kubelet work on a worker node?","a":"<p>It is the node agent. It starts and stops containers, ensures Pods run, and reports to the control plane.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0032","card":"q-kubernetes-004-what-each-component-does","sec":"kubernetes-s005","q":"What is the role of kube-proxy?","a":"<p>It manages networking. It sets up rules for Service IPs and load balances traffic to Pods, using iptables or IPVS.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0033","card":"q-kubernetes-004-what-each-component-does","sec":"kubernetes-s005","q":"What is the Cloud Controller Manager, and when is it used?","a":"<p>It links Kubernetes to cloud APIs for nodes, load balancers, and storage. It is used in cloud-hosted clusters.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0034","card":"q-kubernetes-004-what-each-component-does","sec":"kubernetes-s005","q":"How does Kubernetes ensure high availability of the control plane?","a":"<p>Multiple master nodes, replicated etcd, leader election, and load-balanced API servers.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0035","card":"q-kubernetes-004-what-each-component-does","sec":"kubernetes-s005","q":"In Kubernetes, why do all other components talk to etcd only through the kube-apiserver?","a":"<p>The API server is the single entry point. It authenticates and authorizes every request, validates objects, applies admission control and is the only component that reads and writes etcd. This keeps etcd protected and the access rules consistent. Scheduler, controllers and kubelets watch the API server for changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0036","card":"q-kubernetes-004-what-each-component-does","sec":"kubernetes-s005","q":"What does etcd store in a Kubernetes cluster and what happens if you lose it?","a":"<p>etcd is a consistent, distributed key-value store that holds all cluster state: objects such as Pods, Deployments, Secrets and ConfigMaps. Running Pods keep running if etcd is lost, but you can no longer change anything and the cluster state is gone unless restored from a backup. Regular <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code> backups are therefore essential.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0037","card":"q-kubernetes-004-what-each-component-does","sec":"kubernetes-s005","q":"How does the kube-scheduler pick a node for a Pod?","a":"<p>In two phases. Filtering removes nodes that cannot run the Pod (not enough resources, taints it does not tolerate, node selector or affinity mismatch). Scoring ranks the remaining nodes (for example least loaded) and the best one wins. The scheduler only writes the chosen node name on the Pod. The kubelet on that node starts it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0038","card":"q-kubernetes-004-what-each-component-does","sec":"kubernetes-s005","q":"What is the job of the kube-controller-manager, and how does a controller work?","a":"<p>It runs the built-in controllers (ReplicaSet, Node, Deployment, Job and others) as one process. Each controller runs a reconcile loop: watch the current state through the API server, compare with the desired state, and act to close the gap, for example create a missing Pod.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0039","card":"q-kubernetes-004-what-each-component-does","sec":"kubernetes-s005","q":"What does the kubelet do on a node, and what does it report?","a":"<p>The kubelet is the node agent. It watches the API server for Pods assigned to its node, asks the container runtime to start and stop containers, runs the liveness, readiness and startup probes, mounts volumes, and reports Pod and node status back to the API server.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0040","card":"q-kubernetes-004-what-each-component-does","sec":"kubernetes-s005","q":"What does kube-proxy do and what are its modes?","a":"<p>kube-proxy programs rules on each node so traffic to a Service's virtual IP is load balanced across the backing Pod IPs. Its modes are iptables (default on Linux) and IPVS (better for large numbers of Services). It does not proxy packets in userspace in normal operation, it only maintains the rules.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0041","card":"q-kubernetes-004-what-each-component-does","sec":"kubernetes-s005","q":"When do you need the cloud-controller-manager?","a":"<p>In clusters running on a cloud provider. It contains the cloud-specific control loops: creating a cloud load balancer when you create a Service of type LoadBalancer, managing node lifecycle against the cloud's instances, and setting up routes. On bare metal you usually do not have it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0042","card":"q-kubernetes-004-what-each-component-does","sec":"kubernetes-s005","q":"How is the control plane made highly available?","a":"<p>Run several control plane nodes with a load balancer in front of the API servers. The API servers are stateless and all active. etcd runs as an odd-numbered cluster (3 or 5) using Raft for consensus, which tolerates the loss of a minority. The scheduler and controller manager use leader election so only one instance is active at a time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0043","card":"q-kubernetes-004-what-each-component-does","sec":"kubernetes-s005","q":"Why should an etcd cluster have an odd number of members?","a":"<p>etcd needs a majority (quorum) to commit writes. 3 members tolerate 1 failure, 5 tolerate 2. A 4th member does not add fault tolerance over 3 because quorum becomes 3 of 4, yet it adds replication cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0044","card":"q-kubernetes-004-what-each-component-does","sec":"kubernetes-s005","q":"Walk through what happens when you run <code>kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml</code>.","a":"<p>kubectl sends the object to the API server, which authenticates, authorizes, runs admission, and stores it in etcd. The Deployment controller creates a ReplicaSet, and the ReplicaSet controller creates Pod objects. The scheduler assigns each Pod to a node. The kubelet on that node sees the Pod and tells the container runtime to pull the image and start the containers, then reports status back.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0045","card":"q-kubernetes-005-creating-a-pod","sec":"kubernetes-s006","q":"How do you create a Pod in Kubernetes?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: nginx-pod\nspec:\n  containers:\n  - name: nginx\n    image: nginx:latest\n    ports:\n    - containerPort: 80</code></pre></div>\n<p>Apply it with <code>kubectl apply -f pod.yaml</code>, or run it directly with <code>kubectl run my-pod --image=nginx --restart=Never</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0046","card":"q-kubernetes-005-creating-a-pod","sec":"kubernetes-s006","q":"In Kubernetes, what are the required top-level fields of a Pod manifest and what does each do?","a":"<p><code>apiVersion</code> and <code>kind</code> tell the API server which object type this is. <code>metadata.name</code> identifies it (labels and namespace also go in <code>metadata</code>). <code>spec</code> is the desired state: here a list of containers, each with a name and an image. <code>containerPort</code> is informational documentation of the port the container listens on. It does not publish or open the port.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: nginx-pod\nspec:\n  containers:\n  - name: nginx\n    image: nginx:latest\n    ports:\n    - containerPort: 80</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0047","card":"q-kubernetes-005-creating-a-pod","sec":"kubernetes-s006","q":"What is the pitfall of using <code>image: nginx:latest</code> in a Pod spec?","a":"<p><code>latest</code> is a moving tag, so different nodes or restarts can pull different versions, which makes deployments non-reproducible and rollbacks unclear. Also the default <code>imagePullPolicy</code> becomes <code>Always</code> for <code>latest</code>. Pin a specific version tag, or better a digest, in production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0048","card":"q-kubernetes-005-creating-a-pod","sec":"kubernetes-s006","q":"In Kubernetes, how do <code>kubectl apply -f pod.yaml</code> and <code>kubectl run my-pod --image=nginx --restart=Never</code> differ?","a":"<p><code>apply -f</code> is declarative: it creates or updates the object from a manifest that you can keep in Git. <code>kubectl run ... --restart=Never</code> is imperative and creates a bare Pod directly from flags, fine for quick tests. Imperative objects are harder to track and reproduce.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0049","card":"q-kubernetes-005-creating-a-pod","sec":"kubernetes-s006","q":"Why is running a bare Pod in production a bad idea?","a":"<p>A bare Pod is not managed by a controller. If its node fails or it is deleted, nothing recreates it, and it cannot be scaled or rolled out. Use a Deployment (or StatefulSet, Job) so the controller keeps the desired number of Pods running.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0050","card":"q-kubernetes-005-creating-a-pod","sec":"kubernetes-s006","q":"In a Kubernetes container spec, what are the values of <code>imagePullPolicy</code> and which default applies?","a":"<p><code>Always</code> pulls the image (checking the digest) on every start, <code>IfNotPresent</code> pulls only if the node does not have it, and <code>Never</code> never pulls. If you do not set it, the default is <code>Always</code> when the tag is <code>latest</code> or missing, and <code>IfNotPresent</code> for any other tag. A fixed tag with <code>IfNotPresent</code> is fast but a re-pushed tag will not be picked up on nodes that cached it, so use immutable tags or digests.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0051","card":"q-kubernetes-006-pod-replicaset-and-deployment","sec":"kubernetes-s007","q":"What is the difference between a Pod and a Deployment?","a":"<p>A Pod is a single instance of containers. A Deployment manages Pods with replicas, updates, and self-healing.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0052","card":"q-kubernetes-006-pod-replicaset-and-deployment","sec":"kubernetes-s007","q":"What is a ReplicaSet, and how does it relate to a Deployment?","a":"<p>A ReplicaSet ensures a set number of Pods run. A Deployment creates and updates ReplicaSets for rollouts.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0053","card":"q-kubernetes-006-pod-replicaset-and-deployment","sec":"kubernetes-s007","q":"Explain the lifecycle of a Pod.","a":"<p><code>Pending → Running → Succeeded/Failed → (Unknown if lost)</code>. It is managed through init containers and probes.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0054","card":"q-kubernetes-006-pod-replicaset-and-deployment","sec":"kubernetes-s007","q":"What are labels and selectors, and how are they used?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Labels</strong>: key-value tags (for example <code>app=nginx</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Selectors</strong>: filter objects by labels for routing or management.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0055","card":"q-kubernetes-006-pod-replicaset-and-deployment","sec":"kubernetes-s007","q":"What is the difference between a Pod, a ReplicaSet and a Deployment?","a":"<p>A Pod runs the containers. A ReplicaSet keeps a specified number of identical Pods running. A Deployment manages ReplicaSets to give declarative updates: rolling updates, rollbacks and scaling. You normally create Deployments and let them manage the ReplicaSets and Pods.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0056","card":"q-kubernetes-006-pod-replicaset-and-deployment","sec":"kubernetes-s007","q":"In Kubernetes, why does a Deployment create a new ReplicaSet on every image change instead of editing the existing one?","a":"<p>Each Pod template change creates a new ReplicaSet with a different pod-template hash. During a rolling update the Deployment scales the new ReplicaSet up and the old one down. The old ReplicaSet is kept (scaled to 0) so <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> can scale it back up. <code>revisionHistoryLimit</code> controls how many old ones are kept.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0057","card":"q-kubernetes-006-pod-replicaset-and-deployment","sec":"kubernetes-s007","q":"What are the phases in a Pod lifecycle?","a":"<p>Pending (accepted but not yet running: scheduling, image pull), Running (at least one container running), Succeeded (all containers exited with 0 and will not restart), Failed (all terminated, at least one with an error), Unknown (node cannot be reached, so state is not known).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0058","card":"q-kubernetes-006-pod-replicaset-and-deployment","sec":"kubernetes-s007","q":"What is the difference between a Pod's phase and a container state like CrashLoopBackOff?","a":"<p>Phase is a coarse summary of the Pod. Container states are Waiting, Running and Terminated, with a reason. CrashLoopBackOff is a container Waiting reason shown by <code>kubectl get pods</code> in the STATUS column: the container keeps crashing and the kubelet is delaying restarts with exponential backoff (up to 5 minutes). The Pod phase is still Running.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0059","card":"q-kubernetes-006-pod-replicaset-and-deployment","sec":"kubernetes-s007","q":"What are init containers and how do they differ from normal containers in a Pod?","a":"<p>Init containers run to completion one at a time, in order, before the app containers start. If one fails, the Pod restarts it according to <code>restartPolicy</code> and the app containers do not start. Use them for setup such as waiting for a dependency, running migrations or fetching config. They can use a different image and have tools that the app image should not contain.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0060","card":"q-kubernetes-006-pod-replicaset-and-deployment","sec":"kubernetes-s007","q":"How do labels and selectors tie a Deployment to its Pods and a Service to its Pods?","a":"<p>Labels are key-value tags on objects. A Deployment's <code>spec.selector.matchLabels</code> must match the labels in its Pod template, which is how the ReplicaSet knows which Pods it owns. A Service's <code>selector</code> matches Pod labels to build its list of endpoints. If labels and selector differ, the Service has no endpoints and traffic goes nowhere.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0061","card":"q-kubernetes-006-pod-replicaset-and-deployment","sec":"kubernetes-s007","q":"In Kubernetes, what is the difference between labels and annotations?","a":"<p>Labels are identifying metadata that selectors can query, used for grouping and selection. Annotations are non-identifying metadata that selectors cannot use: build info, tool configuration, URLs, and settings that controllers or ingress controllers read.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0062","card":"q-kubernetes-007-multi-container-pods-and-restarts","sec":"kubernetes-s008","q":"What is a multi-container Pod, and when would you use one?","a":"<p>Multiple containers in one Pod, used for sidecars (for example logging) or tightly coupled apps.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0063","card":"q-kubernetes-007-multi-container-pods-and-restarts","sec":"kubernetes-s008","q":"How do you restart a Pod without deleting it?","a":"<ul>\n  <li>For a Deployment: <code>kubectl rollout restart deployment &lt;name&gt;</code></li>\n  <li>Direct: edit the spec (for example add a label) or let probes fail.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0064","card":"q-kubernetes-007-multi-container-pods-and-restarts","sec":"kubernetes-s008","q":"When would you put two containers in one Pod instead of two separate Pods?","a":"<p>When they are tightly coupled and must share a lifecycle, network or files: a sidecar for log shipping or a proxy, an adapter that reformats metrics, or an ambassador. Containers in a Pod share one IP and can share volumes. If they can scale or fail independently, use separate Pods.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0065","card":"q-kubernetes-007-multi-container-pods-and-restarts","sec":"kubernetes-s008","q":"How do containers in the same Pod communicate with each other?","a":"<p>Over <code>localhost</code>, because they share one network namespace, so they must use different ports. They can also exchange files through a shared volume such as an <code>emptyDir</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0066","card":"q-kubernetes-007-multi-container-pods-and-restarts","sec":"kubernetes-s008","q":"How do you restart the Pods of a Deployment without changing the image?","a":"<p>Run <code>kubectl rollout restart deployment &lt;name&gt;</code>. It changes an annotation in the Pod template, which triggers a normal rolling update, so there is no downtime if readiness probes are set. Deleting Pods by hand also works, but it is not controlled.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0067","card":"q-kubernetes-007-multi-container-pods-and-restarts","sec":"kubernetes-s008","q":"What does a Pod's <code>restartPolicy</code> control?","a":"<p>It decides what the kubelet does when a container exits: <code>Always</code> (default, used by Deployments), <code>OnFailure</code> or <code>Never</code> (used by Jobs and run-to-completion Pods). It applies to all containers in the Pod, and restarts happen on the same node with exponential backoff.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0068","card":"q-kubernetes-008-statefulset-daemonset-job-and-cronjob","sec":"kubernetes-s009","q":"What is a StatefulSet, and how does it differ from a Deployment?","a":"<p>It manages stateful apps with stable names and storage. Unlike a Deployment, it is ordered and persistent.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0069","card":"q-kubernetes-008-statefulset-daemonset-job-and-cronjob","sec":"kubernetes-s009","q":"What is a DaemonSet, and when is it useful?","a":"<p>It runs one Pod per node, which suits monitoring, logging, or network agents.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0070","card":"q-kubernetes-008-statefulset-daemonset-job-and-cronjob","sec":"kubernetes-s009","q":"What is a Job and a CronJob?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Job</strong>: a one-time task that runs to completion.</li>\n  <li><strong>CronJob</strong>: scheduled Jobs (for example backups).</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0071","card":"q-kubernetes-008-statefulset-daemonset-job-and-cronjob","sec":"kubernetes-s009","q":"In Kubernetes, how does a StatefulSet differ from a Deployment?","a":"<p>A StatefulSet gives each Pod a stable identity: a predictable name (<code>web-0</code>, <code>web-1</code>), a stable DNS name through a headless Service, and its own PersistentVolumeClaim from <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> that stays attached across restarts. Pods are created and updated in order. Deployment Pods are interchangeable with random names and share volumes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0072","card":"q-kubernetes-008-statefulset-daemonset-job-and-cronjob","sec":"kubernetes-s009","q":"Which workloads need a StatefulSet?","a":"<p>Workloads where each replica has its own identity or data: databases, message brokers and clustered stores such as Kafka, ZooKeeper or Elasticsearch. Stateless web apps should use Deployments. For production databases, many teams prefer a managed service or an operator rather than raw StatefulSets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0073","card":"q-kubernetes-008-statefulset-daemonset-job-and-cronjob","sec":"kubernetes-s009","q":"Why does a StatefulSet need a headless Service?","a":"<p>A headless Service (<code>clusterIP: None</code>) does not load balance. It makes DNS return the individual Pod addresses, so each Pod gets a stable name like <code>web-0.&lt;service&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.svc.cluster.local</code>. Clients and peers can then address a specific replica, for example a database primary.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0074","card":"q-kubernetes-008-statefulset-daemonset-job-and-cronjob","sec":"kubernetes-s009","q":"What happens to the PVCs when you delete or scale down a StatefulSet?","a":"<p>By default the PVCs are kept, so data is not lost and a later scale-up reattaches the same volumes. In newer versions (<code>persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy</code>, beta from 1.27, GA in 1.32) you can choose to delete them. Clean up PVCs manually if you want the storage released.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0075","card":"q-kubernetes-008-statefulset-daemonset-job-and-cronjob","sec":"kubernetes-s009","q":"What is a DaemonSet and give three typical uses.","a":"<p>A DaemonSet runs one Pod on every node (or on selected nodes), and adds one automatically when a node joins. Typical uses are log collectors (Fluent Bit), node monitoring agents (node-exporter) and networking or storage agents (CNI plugin, CSI node driver).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0076","card":"q-kubernetes-008-statefulset-daemonset-job-and-cronjob","sec":"kubernetes-s009","q":"A DaemonSet Pod does not appear on your control plane nodes. Why, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>Control plane nodes are usually tainted (for example <code>node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule</code>). Add a matching toleration to the DaemonSet's Pod template if the agent must run there.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0077","card":"q-kubernetes-008-statefulset-daemonset-job-and-cronjob","sec":"kubernetes-s009","q":"What is the difference between a Job and a CronJob?","a":"<p>A Job runs Pods until a set number complete successfully, retrying on failure up to <code>backoffLimit</code>. A CronJob creates Jobs on a cron schedule, for example nightly backups. Use <code>restartPolicy: OnFailure</code> or <code>Never</code> in the Pod template, because <code>Always</code> is not allowed for Jobs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0078","card":"q-kubernetes-008-statefulset-daemonset-job-and-cronjob","sec":"kubernetes-s009","q":"What does <code>concurrencyPolicy</code> on a CronJob control?","a":"<p>What happens if a new run is due while the previous one is still running: <code>Allow</code> (default, runs both), <code>Forbid</code> (skips the new run) or <code>Replace</code> (cancels the old and starts the new). For jobs like backups, <code>Forbid</code> prevents overlapping runs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0079","card":"q-kubernetes-009-how-pods-and-services-communicate","sec":"kubernetes-s010","q":"How does networking work in Kubernetes?","a":"<p>Pods get unique IPs, communicate over a flat network (CNI), and use Services for stable access.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0080","card":"q-kubernetes-009-how-pods-and-services-communicate","sec":"kubernetes-s010","q":"How do Pods communicate with each other?","a":"<p>By direct IP or through a Service ClusterIP, enabled by CNI and DNS.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0081","card":"q-kubernetes-009-how-pods-and-services-communicate","sec":"kubernetes-s010","q":"What is a CNI (Container Network Interface), and why is it important?","a":"<p>The standard for Pod networking. It assigns IPs and routes traffic (for example Flannel, Calico).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0082","card":"q-kubernetes-009-how-pods-and-services-communicate","sec":"kubernetes-s010","q":"How does Kubernetes handle DNS resolution within a cluster?","a":"<p>CoreDNS assigns names like <code>&lt;service&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.svc.cluster.local</code> for Pod and Service discovery.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0083","card":"q-kubernetes-009-how-pods-and-services-communicate","sec":"kubernetes-s010","q":"What are the basic networking rules every Kubernetes cluster must satisfy?","a":"<p>Every Pod gets its own IP. Pods can reach all other Pods across nodes without NAT, and nodes can reach Pods. This flat network model is implemented by the CNI plugin.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0084","card":"q-kubernetes-009-how-pods-and-services-communicate","sec":"kubernetes-s010","q":"Why do Pods talk to each other through a Service instead of direct Pod IPs?","a":"<p>Pod IPs change whenever a Pod is recreated. A Service gives a stable virtual IP and DNS name and load balances across the healthy Pods that match its selector, so clients do not have to track individual Pods.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0085","card":"q-kubernetes-009-how-pods-and-services-communicate","sec":"kubernetes-s010","q":"What does a CNI plugin do, and how do Calico, Flannel and Cilium differ?","a":"<p>A Container Network Interface plugin assigns Pod IPs and sets up routing between nodes. Flannel is a simple overlay network and does not support NetworkPolicy. Calico supports routing (BGP) or overlay and enforces NetworkPolicies. Cilium uses eBPF for networking, observability and policy enforcement. Choose by policy needs and performance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0086","card":"q-kubernetes-009-how-pods-and-services-communicate","sec":"kubernetes-s010","q":"What DNS name does a Service get in Kubernetes, and how do Pods in other namespaces reach it?","a":"<p>CoreDNS gives it <code>&lt;service&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.svc.cluster.local</code>. Within the same namespace the short name <code>&lt;service&gt;</code> works. From another namespace use <code>&lt;service&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0087","card":"q-kubernetes-009-how-pods-and-services-communicate","sec":"kubernetes-s010","q":"A Pod cannot resolve a Service name. How do you troubleshoot?","a":"<p>Check that the CoreDNS Pods in <code>kube-system</code> are running and look at their logs. From the failing Pod run <code>nslookup &lt;service&gt;</code> and check <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> for the cluster DNS address. Verify the Service exists in that namespace, and check NetworkPolicies are not blocking UDP/TCP 53 to CoreDNS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0088","card":"q-kubernetes-010-service-types","sec":"kubernetes-s011","q":"What is a ClusterIP service, and how does it work?","a":"<p>The default Service type. It gives a virtual IP for internal Pod access, load balanced by kube-proxy.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0089","card":"q-kubernetes-010-service-types","sec":"kubernetes-s011","q":"Explain the difference between ClusterIP, NodePort, and LoadBalancer.","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>ClusterIP</strong>: internal only.</li>\n  <li><strong>NodePort</strong>: exposes on node ports (30000-32767).</li>\n  <li><strong>LoadBalancer</strong>: external IP from the cloud.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0090","card":"q-kubernetes-010-service-types","sec":"kubernetes-s011","q":"How does Kubernetes handle external traffic to services?","a":"<p>Through NodePort (node IP:port), LoadBalancer (cloud IP), or Ingress (HTTP routing).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0091","card":"q-kubernetes-010-service-types","sec":"kubernetes-s011","q":"What is an Ingress controller, and why is it used?","a":"<p>It manages HTTP traffic. It routes by host and path, handles TLS, and cuts LoadBalancer costs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0092","card":"q-kubernetes-010-service-types","sec":"kubernetes-s011","q":"What is the purpose of kube-proxy in networking?","a":"<p>It sets up Service IPs and load balances traffic to Pods using iptables or IPVS.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0093","card":"q-kubernetes-010-service-types","sec":"kubernetes-s011","q":"In Kubernetes, how do the Service types ClusterIP, NodePort and LoadBalancer differ?","a":"<p>ClusterIP (default) is a virtual IP reachable only inside the cluster. NodePort builds on it and opens the same port (30000-32767 by default) on every node. LoadBalancer builds on NodePort and asks the cloud provider for an external load balancer. Each type includes the previous one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0094","card":"q-kubernetes-010-service-types","sec":"kubernetes-s011","q":"Why is a Service of type LoadBalancer for every app expensive, and what is the alternative?","a":"<p>Each LoadBalancer Service provisions a separate cloud load balancer with its own cost and IP. An Ingress controller puts many HTTP(S) apps behind a single load balancer and routes by host and path, and terminates TLS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0095","card":"q-kubernetes-010-service-types","sec":"kubernetes-s011","q":"What is the difference between an Ingress and an Ingress controller?","a":"<p>An Ingress is only a set of routing rules (host, path, backend Service, TLS) stored in the API. An Ingress controller (NGINX, Traefik, AWS Load Balancer Controller) is the running component that watches Ingress objects and configures a proxy or load balancer. Without a controller, Ingress objects do nothing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0096","card":"q-kubernetes-010-service-types","sec":"kubernetes-s011","q":"What is the Gateway API and how does it relate to Ingress?","a":"<p>Gateway API is the newer, more expressive successor to Ingress (its core resources GatewayClass, Gateway and HTTPRoute are GA, v1, since Gateway API v1.0 in October 2023; it is installed as CRDs and versioned separately from Kubernetes). It separates roles with GatewayClass, Gateway and HTTPRoute, and supports more protocols and traffic features such as header matching and weights. Ingress remains widely used.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0097","card":"q-kubernetes-010-service-types","sec":"kubernetes-s011","q":"In a Service manifest, what is the difference between <code>port</code>, <code>targetPort</code> and <code>nodePort</code>?","a":"<p><code>port</code> is the port the Service exposes inside the cluster. <code>targetPort</code> is the port on the Pod's container that traffic is forwarded to. <code>nodePort</code> is the port opened on every node for NodePort and LoadBalancer types, and by default it is picked from 30000-32767.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0098","card":"q-kubernetes-010-service-types","sec":"kubernetes-s011","q":"What is a headless Service, and when do you use one?","a":"<p>A Service with <code>clusterIP: None</code>. It gets no virtual IP, and DNS returns the Pod IPs directly. Use it for StatefulSets and for clients that do their own load balancing or need to find each peer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0099","card":"q-kubernetes-010-service-types","sec":"kubernetes-s011","q":"How do you reach an external database from Pods using a Kubernetes-style name?","a":"<p>Create a Service of type <code>ExternalName</code>, which returns a CNAME to the external DNS name. Applications use the in-cluster Service name, and you can change the target later without changing the apps. It does not proxy traffic and does not work with IP addresses.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0100","card":"q-kubernetes-010-service-types","sec":"kubernetes-s011","q":"In a Kubernetes Service, what does <code>externalTrafficPolicy: Local</code> change for NodePort and LoadBalancer traffic?","a":"<p>With the default <code>Cluster</code>, a node may forward traffic to a Pod on another node, which adds a hop and replaces the client source IP with the node IP (SNAT). With <code>Local</code>, a node sends traffic only to Pods on that same node. This keeps the client source IP and avoids the extra hop, but nodes with no local Pod drop the traffic, so the load balancer must health-check nodes, and load can be uneven.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0101","card":"q-kubernetes-010-service-types","sec":"kubernetes-s011","q":"What does <code>sessionAffinity: ClientIP</code> do on a Kubernetes Service, and what is its limit?","a":"<p>It sends requests from the same client IP to the same backend Pod (default timeout 10800 seconds), instead of random balancing. It is a basic stickiness at the Service level. It breaks down behind NAT or proxies where many users share one IP, and it does not survive the Pod being replaced. For cookie-based stickiness use an Ingress controller feature.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0102","card":"q-kubernetes-011-network-policy","sec":"kubernetes-s012","q":"What is a Network Policy, and how can you enforce it?","a":"<p>Rules that control Pod traffic. They are enforced by CNI plugins such as Calico.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0103","card":"q-kubernetes-011-network-policy","sec":"kubernetes-s012","q":"In Kubernetes, what does a NetworkPolicy do, and what is the default behavior when none exists?","a":"<p>A NetworkPolicy restricts which Pods can send traffic to or receive traffic from a set of Pods, based on Pod labels, namespaces and IP blocks. With no policy, all Pods can talk to all Pods. Once a policy selects a Pod for a direction, only the traffic that policy allows is permitted for that Pod and direction.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0104","card":"q-kubernetes-011-network-policy","sec":"kubernetes-s012","q":"You apply a NetworkPolicy and nothing changes. What is a likely cause?","a":"<p>The cluster's CNI plugin does not enforce NetworkPolicies. The API accepts the object but nothing implements it. Flannel by itself does not enforce policies. Calico, Cilium and others do.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0105","card":"q-kubernetes-011-network-policy","sec":"kubernetes-s012","q":"How would you make a namespace default deny for all incoming traffic, and then allow only what is needed?","a":"<p>Apply a policy with an empty <code>podSelector: {}</code> and <code>policyTypes: [Ingress]</code> with no ingress rules. This selects all Pods and allows nothing in. Then add policies that allow specific senders, ports or namespaces. NetworkPolicies are additive, so allowed traffic is the union of all policies selecting the Pod.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0106","card":"q-kubernetes-012-pv-pvc-and-storageclass","sec":"kubernetes-s013","q":"What is a Persistent Volume (PV)?","a":"<p>A cluster storage resource that abstracts disks (for example NFS or cloud disks) for Pods.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0107","card":"q-kubernetes-012-pv-pvc-and-storageclass","sec":"kubernetes-s013","q":"What is a Persistent Volume Claim (PVC), and how does it relate to a PV?","a":"<p>A user request for storage. It binds to a matching PV for Pod use.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0108","card":"q-kubernetes-012-pv-pvc-and-storageclass","sec":"kubernetes-s013","q":"Explain static vs dynamic provisioning.","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Static</strong>: an admin pre-creates PVs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Dynamic</strong>: PVs are created on demand through a StorageClass.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0109","card":"q-kubernetes-012-pv-pvc-and-storageclass","sec":"kubernetes-s013","q":"What is a StorageClass, and how does it work?","a":"<p>It defines storage types (for example SSD) for dynamic PV provisioning.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0110","card":"q-kubernetes-012-pv-pvc-and-storageclass","sec":"kubernetes-s013","q":"In Kubernetes, why are PersistentVolume and PersistentVolumeClaim separate objects?","a":"<p>To separate who provides storage from who uses it. An admin (or a dynamic provisioner) provides PVs as cluster resources. A developer asks for storage with a PVC, stating size, access mode and class, without knowing the backing disk. Kubernetes binds a PVC to a matching PV, so manifests stay portable across clouds.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0111","card":"q-kubernetes-012-pv-pvc-and-storageclass","sec":"kubernetes-s013","q":"What problem does dynamic provisioning solve compared to static provisioning?","a":"<p>With static provisioning an admin has to create PVs ahead of time and guess sizes. With dynamic provisioning a PVC that names a StorageClass makes the provisioner create the disk and PV on demand. This removes manual work and wasted capacity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0112","card":"q-kubernetes-012-pv-pvc-and-storageclass","sec":"kubernetes-s013","q":"What does a StorageClass define?","a":"<p>The provisioner (CSI driver), its parameters (disk type such as SSD, IOPS, encryption), the reclaim policy for created PVs, <code>volumeBindingMode</code> and whether expansion is allowed. A PVC picks a class with <code>storageClassName</code>, and one class can be marked default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0113","card":"q-kubernetes-012-pv-pvc-and-storageclass","sec":"kubernetes-s013","q":"Why is <code>volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer</code> often used in a StorageClass?","a":"<p>With the default <code>Immediate</code> mode the volume is created as soon as the PVC is created, possibly in a zone where the Pod cannot be scheduled. <code>WaitForFirstConsumer</code> delays provisioning until a Pod uses the PVC, so the volume is created in the zone where the scheduler places the Pod.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0114","card":"q-kubernetes-012-pv-pvc-and-storageclass","sec":"kubernetes-s013","q":"A PVC stays in Pending. What do you check?","a":"<p>Run <code>kubectl describe pvc</code> and read the events. Common causes: no StorageClass named or no default class, the provisioner or CSI driver is not installed or has no permission, no PV matches the requested size or access mode (static), or <code>WaitForFirstConsumer</code> is waiting for a Pod to use it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0115","card":"q-kubernetes-012-pv-pvc-and-storageclass","sec":"kubernetes-s013","q":"Is a PersistentVolume namespaced? And a PVC?","a":"<p>A PV is cluster-scoped. A PVC is namespaced and can only be used by Pods in its own namespace.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0116","card":"q-kubernetes-013-access-modes-csi-and-reclaim-policy","sec":"kubernetes-s014","q":"What are the access modes for Persistent Volumes?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>RWO</strong>: read-write, one node.</li>\n  <li><strong>ROX</strong>: read-only, many nodes.</li>\n  <li><strong>RWX</strong>: read-write, many nodes.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0117","card":"q-kubernetes-013-access-modes-csi-and-reclaim-policy","sec":"kubernetes-s014","q":"How does Kubernetes handle storage in a multi-node cluster?","a":"<p>It uses PV/PVC with node affinity (RWO) or shared systems (RWX) through CSI.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0118","card":"q-kubernetes-013-access-modes-csi-and-reclaim-policy","sec":"kubernetes-s014","q":"What is the role of CSI (Container Storage Interface)?","a":"<p>It standardizes storage drivers and enables dynamic provisioning and advanced features.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0119","card":"q-kubernetes-013-access-modes-csi-and-reclaim-policy","sec":"kubernetes-s014","q":"What happens to a PV when a PVC is deleted?","a":"<p>It depends on the reclaim policy: Retain (keeps the PV), Delete (removes the PV), or Recycle (wipes the data).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0120","card":"q-kubernetes-013-access-modes-csi-and-reclaim-policy","sec":"kubernetes-s014","q":"What do the access modes ReadWriteOnce, ReadOnlyMany and ReadWriteMany mean?","a":"<p>RWO: mounted read-write by a single node (several Pods on that node can still use it). ROX: mounted read-only by many nodes. RWX: mounted read-write by many nodes. RWX needs a shared file system such as NFS, EFS or CephFS. Block disks like EBS are RWO. There is also ReadWriteOncePod (stable since 1.29), which limits it to one Pod.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0121","card":"q-kubernetes-013-access-modes-csi-and-reclaim-policy","sec":"kubernetes-s014","q":"Why can't a Deployment with 3 replicas sharing one RWO PVC scale across nodes?","a":"<p>A RWO volume can attach to only one node, so replicas scheduled on other nodes cannot mount it and stay in ContainerCreating. Use an RWX-capable storage class, or a StatefulSet with <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> so each replica gets its own volume.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0122","card":"q-kubernetes-013-access-modes-csi-and-reclaim-policy","sec":"kubernetes-s014","q":"What is CSI and why was it introduced?","a":"<p>The Container Storage Interface is a standard API between Kubernetes and storage vendors. Storage drivers run as Pods outside the Kubernetes core, replacing the old in-tree volume plugins. Vendors can ship and update drivers independently, and CSI adds features such as snapshots, cloning and volume expansion.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0123","card":"q-kubernetes-013-access-modes-csi-and-reclaim-policy","sec":"kubernetes-s014","q":"What happens to a PV when its PVC is deleted, and which reclaim policy would you choose for production data?","a":"<p>It depends on <code>persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy</code>. Retain keeps the PV and data for manual cleanup. Delete removes the PV and the underlying disk (the default for dynamically provisioned volumes). Recycle (basic scrub) is deprecated. For important data choose Retain so a deleted claim cannot destroy the disk.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0124","card":"q-kubernetes-013-access-modes-csi-and-reclaim-policy","sec":"kubernetes-s014","q":"How can you resize a PVC?","a":"<p>Use a StorageClass with <code>allowVolumeExpansion: true</code>, then increase <code>spec.resources.requests.storage</code> on the PVC. The CSI driver expands the volume, and the file system is grown online or on the next Pod mount depending on the driver. You can grow a volume but not shrink it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0125","card":"q-kubernetes-014-attaching-storage-and-troubleshooting","sec":"kubernetes-s015","q":"How do you attach storage to a Pod?","a":"<p>Reference the PVC under <code>volumes</code> and mount it in the container.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>volumes:\n- name: my-volume\n  persistentVolumeClaim:\n    claimName: my-pvc</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0126","card":"q-kubernetes-014-attaching-storage-and-troubleshooting","sec":"kubernetes-s015","q":"How do you troubleshoot storage issues?","a":"<p>Check Pod events (<code>kubectl describe</code>), PVC/PV binding, and StorageClass config.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0127","card":"q-kubernetes-014-attaching-storage-and-troubleshooting","sec":"kubernetes-s015","q":"A Pod is stuck in ContainerCreating with a volume error. What do you check?","a":"<p>Run <code>kubectl describe pod</code> and read the events (FailedMount, FailedAttachVolume). Then check the PVC is Bound and the PV exists, the access mode fits (an RWO volume still attached to another node blocks a new mount), the StorageClass and CSI driver are healthy, and the Pod is in the same zone as the volume.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0128","card":"q-kubernetes-014-attaching-storage-and-troubleshooting","sec":"kubernetes-s015","q":"What is the difference between an <code>emptyDir</code> volume and a PVC-backed volume?","a":"<p><code>emptyDir</code> is created when the Pod starts on a node and deleted when the Pod is removed from that node. It survives container restarts and is useful for scratch space or sharing files between containers. A PVC-backed volume lives independently of the Pod and keeps data after the Pod is deleted.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0129","card":"q-kubernetes-015-configmaps-and-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s016","q":"What is a ConfigMap, and how is it used?","a":"<p>It stores non-sensitive config data, used as env vars or files in Pods.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0130","card":"q-kubernetes-015-configmaps-and-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s016","q":"What is a Secret, and how does it differ from a ConfigMap?","a":"<p>A Secret stores sensitive data (for example passwords) and is base64-encoded. A ConfigMap holds plain text.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0131","card":"q-kubernetes-015-configmaps-and-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s016","q":"What is the difference between a base64-encoded Secret and a plain-text ConfigMap?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Secret</strong>: encoded for obscurity.</li>\n  <li><strong>ConfigMap</strong>: raw text for configs.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0132","card":"q-kubernetes-015-configmaps-and-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s016","q":"How do you create and manage Secrets?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl create secret generic my-secret --from-literal=key=value</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0133","card":"q-kubernetes-015-configmaps-and-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s016","q":"Why should configuration be kept in ConfigMaps rather than baked into the container image?","a":"<p>One image can then run in dev, staging and prod with different settings, with no rebuild. Config changes do not need a new image, and configuration stays separate from code (twelve-factor app).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0134","card":"q-kubernetes-015-configmaps-and-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s016","q":"Is a Kubernetes Secret encrypted? What is its real protection?","a":"<p>Not by default. Values are only base64-encoded, which is an encoding, not encryption, and anyone who can read the Secret can decode it. Protection comes from RBAC limiting who can read Secrets, encryption at rest configured in the API server (or a KMS provider), and keeping Secrets out of Git.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0135","card":"q-kubernetes-015-configmaps-and-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s016","q":"When would you choose a Secret over a ConfigMap?","a":"<p>Use a Secret for credentials, tokens and keys. It can be restricted with its own RBAC rules, is not printed by <code>kubectl describe</code>, can be encrypted at rest, and is kept in <code>tmpfs</code> when mounted on a node. Use a ConfigMap for non-sensitive settings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0136","card":"q-kubernetes-015-configmaps-and-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s016","q":"What are the size limits of a ConfigMap or Secret?","a":"<p>Each object is limited to 1 MiB because they are stored in etcd. For larger files, use a volume or object storage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0137","card":"q-kubernetes-015-configmaps-and-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s016","q":"How would you create a Secret with <code>kubectl</code> from the command line without writing YAML?","a":"<p><code>generic</code> is the type for arbitrary key-value data (other types are <code>docker-registry</code> and <code>tls</code>). <code>--from-literal</code> adds a key and value, and <code>--from-file</code> reads values from files. The value is stored base64-encoded. Running this puts the secret in your shell history, so prefer <code>--from-file</code> or a secrets manager.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl create secret generic my-secret --from-literal=key=value</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0138","card":"q-kubernetes-015-configmaps-and-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s016","q":"What is an immutable ConfigMap or Secret and why use it?","a":"<p>Setting <code>immutable: true</code> (stable since Kubernetes 1.21) makes the data read-only after creation. It prevents accidental changes and reduces load on the API server because the kubelet stops watching it. To change it, create a new object and update the Pods.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0139","card":"q-kubernetes-016-injecting-values-into-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s017","q":"How do you inject environment variables into a Pod?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>env:\n- name: ENV_VAR\n  value: &quot;value&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0140","card":"q-kubernetes-016-injecting-values-into-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s017","q":"How do you pass sensitive data to an application?","a":"<p>Use Secrets as env vars or mounted files.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0141","card":"q-kubernetes-016-injecting-values-into-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s017","q":"How can you mount a ConfigMap or Secret as a volume?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>volumes:\n- name: config-vol\n  configMap:\n    name: my-config</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0142","card":"q-kubernetes-016-injecting-values-into-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s017","q":"How do you update a ConfigMap or Secret without restarting a Pod?","a":"<p>Mount it as a volume. The Pod sees updates automatically, with some delay.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0143","card":"q-kubernetes-016-injecting-values-into-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s017","q":"In a Pod spec, what is the difference between setting an env var with <code>value</code> and using <code>valueFrom</code>?","a":"<p><code>value</code> hard-codes the string in the manifest. <code>valueFrom</code> reads it from somewhere else: a <code>configMapKeyRef</code>, a <code>secretKeyRef</code>, a <code>fieldRef</code> (such as the Pod name or IP) or a <code>resourceFieldRef</code>. Use <code>valueFrom</code> to keep config and secrets out of the Pod manifest.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>env:\n- name: ENV_VAR\n  value: &quot;value&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0144","card":"q-kubernetes-016-injecting-values-into-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s017","q":"How do you load all keys of a ConfigMap as environment variables at once?","a":"<p>Use <code>envFrom</code> with a <code>configMapRef</code> (or <code>secretRef</code>) in the container spec. Each key becomes an environment variable. Keys that are not valid variable names are skipped. Use <code>env</code> with <code>valueFrom</code> to choose single keys or rename them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0145","card":"q-kubernetes-016-injecting-values-into-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s017","q":"When would you mount a Secret as a volume instead of exposing it as an environment variable?","a":"<p>Files are safer and more flexible. Env vars can leak through process listings, crash dumps, child processes and logs, and cannot be updated while the Pod runs. A mounted Secret is a file with restrictive permissions, is kept in memory-backed storage and is updated by the kubelet. Use it for certificates and keys, and for apps that re-read files.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0146","card":"q-kubernetes-016-injecting-values-into-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s017","q":"In Kubernetes, how do you mount a ConfigMap as files into a container?","a":"<p>The <code>volumes</code> entry declares the ConfigMap volume. The container also needs <code>volumeMounts</code> with the volume name and a <code>mountPath</code>. Each key becomes a file in that directory, with the value as its content. Use <code>items</code> to select keys and <code>subPath</code> to mount a single file.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>volumes:\n- name: config-vol\n  configMap:\n    name: my-config</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0147","card":"q-kubernetes-016-injecting-values-into-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s017","q":"A ConfigMap was updated but the application still sees the old values. Why?","a":"<p>Environment variables are set when the container starts and never refresh, so a restart is needed (<code>kubectl rollout restart</code>). Mounted volumes are updated by the kubelet after a delay (up to about a minute), but not when mounted with <code>subPath</code>. The application must also re-read the file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0148","card":"q-kubernetes-016-injecting-values-into-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s017","q":"How can you make a Deployment roll out automatically when its ConfigMap changes?","a":"<p>Put a hash of the ConfigMap content in a Pod template annotation. In Helm that is <code>checksum/config: {{ include (print $.Template.BasePath \"/configmap.yaml\") . | sha256sum }}</code>. A change alters the Pod template and triggers a rolling update. Alternatively use a tool like Reloader, or give ConfigMaps versioned names with Kustomize.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0149","card":"q-kubernetes-017-securing-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s018","q":"What are the security best practices for managing Secrets?","a":"<p>Encrypt at rest, use RBAC, avoid logging, and rotate regularly.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0150","card":"q-kubernetes-017-securing-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s018","q":"What is the role of RBAC in securing ConfigMaps and Secrets?","a":"<p>It restricts who can read or write them via Roles and Bindings.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0151","card":"q-kubernetes-017-securing-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s018","q":"What are the best practices for protecting Secrets in Kubernetes?","a":"<p>Enable encryption at rest (EncryptionConfiguration or a KMS provider), restrict <code>get</code>, <code>list</code> and <code>watch</code> on Secrets with RBAC (<code>list</code> also reveals values), avoid putting values in logs, env vars or Git, rotate credentials, and prefer an external manager such as HashiCorp Vault or AWS Secrets Manager through the Secrets Store CSI driver or External Secrets Operator.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0152","card":"q-kubernetes-017-securing-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s018","q":"How can you store Secret manifests in Git safely for GitOps?","a":"<p>Never commit plain Secrets. Encrypt them first, for example with Sealed Secrets (only the cluster can decrypt), SOPS (with KMS or age keys), or reference an external secret store with External Secrets Operator so Git contains only a reference.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0153","card":"q-kubernetes-017-securing-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s018","q":"Why is giving someone <code>list</code> permission on Secrets in RBAC dangerous?","a":"<p><code>list</code> and <code>watch</code> return the full contents of the Secrets, including data, not only names. Anyone with them can read every Secret in scope, so grant <code>get</code> on specific named resources, scoped to a namespace.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0154","card":"q-kubernetes-017-securing-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s018","q":"Sealed Secrets, SOPS or External Secrets Operator: how do you choose for Kubernetes GitOps?","a":"<p>Sealed Secrets: a controller in the cluster holds the private key, you commit an encrypted SealedSecret, and only that cluster can decrypt it. It is simple, but the key is per cluster. SOPS: encrypts values in the file with KMS, age or PGP keys, works with any tool such as Flux, and is not tied to one cluster. External Secrets Operator: Git holds only a reference, and the real value stays in a store such as Vault or AWS Secrets Manager, which gives central rotation and audit. Pick Sealed Secrets or SOPS for small setups with no secret manager, and External Secrets when a central manager already exists.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0155","card":"q-kubernetes-018-how-the-scheduler-places-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s019","q":"How does the scheduler decide where to place a Pod?","a":"<p>It filters nodes (resources, taints), then scores them based on policies (for example least usage).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0156","card":"q-kubernetes-018-how-the-scheduler-places-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s019","q":"What is a node selector, and how does it work?","a":"<p>It matches Pods to nodes by labels (for example <code>nodeSelector: disk=ssd</code>).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0157","card":"q-kubernetes-018-how-the-scheduler-places-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s019","q":"What is a taint and toleration, and how are they used?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Taint</strong>: repels Pods from nodes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Toleration</strong>: allows Pods to ignore taints.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Taint a node and tolerate it in a Pod:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl taint nodes &lt;node&gt; app=backend:NoSchedule</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>tolerations:\n- key: &quot;app&quot;\n  operator: &quot;Equal&quot;\n  value: &quot;backend&quot;\n  effect: &quot;NoSchedule&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0158","card":"q-kubernetes-018-how-the-scheduler-places-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s019","q":"What are the two phases of the Kubernetes scheduler, and what happens in each?","a":"<p>Filtering removes nodes that cannot host the Pod: insufficient requested resources, unmatched node selector or affinity, taints not tolerated, volume constraints. Scoring ranks the remaining nodes with plugins (spread, least requested, affinity preferences), and the highest score is chosen. If no node passes filtering, the Pod stays Pending.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0159","card":"q-kubernetes-018-how-the-scheduler-places-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s019","q":"In a Pod spec, how does <code>nodeSelector</code> work and what is its limit?","a":"<p><code>nodeSelector</code> is a map of node labels that must all match for the Pod to be scheduled on a node, for example <code>disk: ssd</code>. It is a hard requirement and only supports exact matches. For OR logic, operators, or soft preferences, use node affinity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0160","card":"q-kubernetes-018-how-the-scheduler-places-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s019","q":"What is the difference between node affinity, pod affinity and pod anti-affinity?","a":"<p>Node affinity attracts Pods to nodes by node labels (required or preferred). Pod affinity places Pods near other Pods, for example on the same node or zone as a cache. Pod anti-affinity keeps Pods apart, for example spreading replicas across nodes or zones for availability. Both pod types use a <code>topologyKey</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0161","card":"q-kubernetes-018-how-the-scheduler-places-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s019","q":"How do taints and tolerations differ from node selectors and affinity?","a":"<p>Selectors and affinity are set on the Pod to attract it to nodes. A taint is set on the node to repel Pods unless they tolerate it. A toleration only permits scheduling on the tainted node. It does not force it, so to dedicate nodes you combine a taint with a node selector or affinity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0162","card":"q-kubernetes-018-how-the-scheduler-places-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s019","q":"What are the three taint effects?","a":"<p><code>NoSchedule</code>: new Pods without a toleration are not scheduled. <code>PreferNoSchedule</code>: the scheduler tries to avoid the node but may use it. <code>NoExecute</code>: new Pods are not scheduled and existing Pods without a toleration are evicted.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0163","card":"q-kubernetes-018-how-the-scheduler-places-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s019","q":"How do you dedicate a set of nodes to one workload?","a":"<p>Taint the nodes (as shown) so other Pods are repelled. Add a toleration with <code>key: \"app\"</code>, <code>operator: \"Equal\"</code>, <code>value: \"backend\"</code>, <code>effect: \"NoSchedule\"</code> to the workload's Pods. Also label the nodes and use a <code>nodeSelector</code> or node affinity, because the toleration alone does not force the Pods onto them.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl taint nodes &lt;node&gt; app=backend:NoSchedule</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0164","card":"q-kubernetes-018-how-the-scheduler-places-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s019","q":"How do you spread replicas evenly across zones?","a":"<p>Use <code>topologySpreadConstraints</code> with <code>topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone</code>, a <code>maxSkew</code> and <code>whenUnsatisfiable</code>. Pod anti-affinity can do something similar, but topology spread constraints control the distribution more precisely.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0165","card":"q-kubernetes-019-requests-and-limits","sec":"kubernetes-s020","q":"What are resource requests and limits?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Requests</strong>: the minimum needed.</li>\n  <li><strong>Limits</strong>: the maximum allowed (CPU/memory).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>spec:\n  containers:\n  - name: nginx\n    image: nginx\n    resources:\n      requests:\n        cpu: &quot;250m&quot;\n        memory: &quot;64Mi&quot;\n      limits:\n        cpu: &quot;500m&quot;\n        memory: &quot;128Mi&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0166","card":"q-kubernetes-019-requests-and-limits","sec":"kubernetes-s020","q":"What happens when a Pod exceeds its resource limits?","a":"<p>CPU is throttled and memory causes an OOM kill.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0167","card":"q-kubernetes-019-requests-and-limits","sec":"kubernetes-s020","q":"What is a Quality of Service (QoS) class, and how is it assigned?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Guaranteed</strong>: limits = requests.</li>\n  <li><strong>Burstable</strong>: requests &lt; limits.</li>\n  <li><strong>BestEffort</strong>: none set. Assigned automatically.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0168","card":"q-kubernetes-019-requests-and-limits","sec":"kubernetes-s020","q":"In Kubernetes, what is the difference between a resource request and a limit?","a":"<p>The request is what the scheduler reserves when placing the Pod, so a node needs that much unreserved capacity. The limit is the maximum the container may use at runtime. <code>250m</code> is a quarter of a CPU core and <code>Mi</code> is mebibytes.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resources:\n  requests:\n    cpu: &quot;250m&quot;\n    memory: &quot;64Mi&quot;\n  limits:\n    cpu: &quot;500m&quot;\n    memory: &quot;128Mi&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0169","card":"q-kubernetes-019-requests-and-limits","sec":"kubernetes-s020","q":"What happens when a container exceeds its CPU limit versus its memory limit?","a":"<p>CPU is compressible: the container is throttled by the CFS quota and keeps running more slowly. Memory is not compressible: the container is OOM-killed (exit code 137, reason OOMKilled) and restarted according to <code>restartPolicy</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0170","card":"q-kubernetes-019-requests-and-limits","sec":"kubernetes-s020","q":"How are Kubernetes QoS classes assigned, and why do they matter?","a":"<p>Guaranteed: every container has CPU and memory requests equal to limits. Burstable: at least one request or limit is set but not Guaranteed. BestEffort: nothing set. Under node memory pressure the kubelet evicts BestEffort first, then Burstable using more than requests, and Guaranteed last.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0171","card":"q-kubernetes-019-requests-and-limits","sec":"kubernetes-s020","q":"Why is it risky to set no requests and limits on production Pods?","a":"<p>The Pod is BestEffort. The scheduler thinks it needs nothing, so nodes get overpacked, and it is first to be evicted. A runaway container can starve neighbours. Set requests from observed usage and limits to bound the worst case.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0172","card":"q-kubernetes-019-requests-and-limits","sec":"kubernetes-s020","q":"What is a common criticism of setting CPU limits, and what is the alternative view?","a":"<p>CPU limits can throttle latency-sensitive apps even when the node has idle CPU, due to CFS quota periods. Some teams set CPU requests but no CPU limits, and always set memory limits equal to requests. Others keep CPU limits for strict multi-tenant fairness. State the trade-off.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0173","card":"q-kubernetes-019-requests-and-limits","sec":"kubernetes-s020","q":"What do ResourceQuota and LimitRange do?","a":"<p>A ResourceQuota caps total resources (CPU, memory, object counts) used in a namespace. A LimitRange sets default requests and limits and min/max per container or Pod in a namespace. Together they stop one team or an unconfigured Pod from consuming the cluster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0174","card":"q-kubernetes-020-priority-and-eviction","sec":"kubernetes-s021","q":"How do you prioritize Pods, and what is a PriorityClass?","a":"<p>Use a PriorityClass. Higher priority Pods get scheduled first.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: scheduling.k8s.io/v1\nkind: PriorityClass\nmetadata:\n  name: high-priority\nvalue: 1000</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0175","card":"q-kubernetes-020-priority-and-eviction","sec":"kubernetes-s021","q":"How does Kubernetes handle Pod eviction?","a":"<p>It evicts low-priority or over-limit Pods when resources are tight.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0176","card":"q-kubernetes-020-priority-and-eviction","sec":"kubernetes-s021","q":"How do you troubleshoot a Pod stuck in Pending?","a":"<p>Check events (<code>kubectl describe</code>), resources, taints, and PVCs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0177","card":"q-kubernetes-020-priority-and-eviction","sec":"kubernetes-s021","q":"In Kubernetes, how does a PriorityClass affect scheduling?","a":"<p>A Pod refers to the class with <code>priorityClassName: high-priority</code>. Higher <code>value</code> Pods are placed first in the scheduling queue. If the cluster is full, the scheduler can preempt (evict) lower priority Pods to make room for a higher priority Pending Pod. The built-in <code>system-cluster-critical</code> and <code>system-node-critical</code> classes protect system Pods.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: scheduling.k8s.io/v1\nkind: PriorityClass\nmetadata:\n  name: high-priority\nvalue: 1000</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0178","card":"q-kubernetes-020-priority-and-eviction","sec":"kubernetes-s021","q":"What is the difference between preemption and node-pressure eviction?","a":"<p>Preemption is done by the scheduler: it removes lower-priority Pods so a higher-priority Pod can be scheduled. Node-pressure eviction is done by the kubelet when the node runs short of memory, disk or PIDs. It terminates Pods to reclaim resources, ranking by QoS class, usage over requests and priority.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0179","card":"q-kubernetes-021-autoscaling","sec":"kubernetes-s022","q":"What is Horizontal Pod Autoscaling (HPA), and how does it work?","a":"<p>It scales Pod replicas based on CPU or memory and needs the metrics server.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0180","card":"q-kubernetes-021-autoscaling","sec":"kubernetes-s022","q":"How do you configure HPA based on custom metrics?","a":"<p>Use Prometheus Adapter and set the custom metric target in the HPA spec.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0181","card":"q-kubernetes-021-autoscaling","sec":"kubernetes-s022","q":"What is Vertical Pod Autoscaling (VPA), and when is it useful?","a":"<p>It adjusts Pod resource limits, which helps optimize fixed workloads.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0182","card":"q-kubernetes-021-autoscaling","sec":"kubernetes-s022","q":"How do you scale an application for sudden traffic spikes?","a":"<p>Use HPA or scale manually with <code>kubectl scale</code>, plus the cluster autoscaler.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0183","card":"q-kubernetes-021-autoscaling","sec":"kubernetes-s022","q":"How does the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler work in Kubernetes?","a":"<p>The HPA controller periodically (every 15 seconds by default) reads metrics, computes <code>desiredReplicas = ceil(currentReplicas * currentValue / targetValue)</code> and updates the replica count of a Deployment or StatefulSet within <code>minReplicas</code> and <code>maxReplicas</code>. For CPU and memory it needs the Metrics Server, and utilization is measured as a percentage of the Pod's resource requests.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0184","card":"q-kubernetes-021-autoscaling","sec":"kubernetes-s022","q":"Why does the HPA not scale a Deployment whose containers have no CPU request?","a":"<p>CPU utilization is calculated as usage divided by the request. Without a request there is no denominator, so the HPA reports that it cannot compute utilization and does nothing. Set <code>resources.requests.cpu</code> on every container.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0185","card":"q-kubernetes-021-autoscaling","sec":"kubernetes-s022","q":"How do you make the HPA scale on a custom metric such as requests per second or queue length?","a":"<p>Use the <code>autoscaling/v2</code> API with <code>Pods</code>, <code>Object</code> or <code>External</code> metric types. A metrics adapter (such as Prometheus Adapter) exposes the metric through the custom or external metrics API. Alternatively KEDA scales on event systems such as queues, and can scale to zero.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0186","card":"q-kubernetes-021-autoscaling","sec":"kubernetes-s022","q":"When would you use VPA instead of HPA?","a":"<p>The Vertical Pod Autoscaler adjusts a container's resource requests (and limits proportionally) based on observed usage. It suits workloads that cannot add replicas, like single-instance or stateful apps, and helps right-size requests. In <code>Auto</code> mode it restarts Pods to apply changes, and you should not drive HPA and VPA from the same CPU or memory metric.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0187","card":"q-kubernetes-021-autoscaling","sec":"kubernetes-s022","q":"What is the Cluster Autoscaler and how does it relate to the HPA?","a":"<p>HPA adds Pods. If new Pods cannot be scheduled for lack of capacity, the Cluster Autoscaler adds nodes to the node group. It removes underused nodes. You need both for a spike: HPA raises replicas, and the Cluster Autoscaler provides nodes for them. Karpenter is an alternative on AWS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0188","card":"q-kubernetes-021-autoscaling","sec":"kubernetes-s022","q":"How do you handle a sudden traffic spike without dropping requests?","a":"<p>Set sensible <code>minReplicas</code> with headroom, use HPA with a fast scale-up <code>behavior</code> policy, use readiness probes so new Pods only get traffic when ready, keep Cluster Autoscaler or Karpenter enabled, and consider pre-scaling for known events. Pod startup time and node provisioning time are the limits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0189","card":"q-kubernetes-022-updates-and-failover","sec":"kubernetes-s023","q":"How does Kubernetes ensure high availability of applications?","a":"<p>Through replicas, multi-node scheduling, and self-healing via controllers.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0190","card":"q-kubernetes-022-updates-and-failover","sec":"kubernetes-s023","q":"What is a rolling update, and how do you perform one?","a":"<p>Gradual Pod replacement: <code>kubectl set image deployment/&lt;name&gt; container=new-image</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0191","card":"q-kubernetes-022-updates-and-failover","sec":"kubernetes-s023","q":"What is the difference between a rolling update and a recreate strategy?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Rolling</strong>: zero downtime, gradual.</li>\n  <li><strong>Recreate</strong>: kills all Pods, then restarts them.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0192","card":"q-kubernetes-022-updates-and-failover","sec":"kubernetes-s023","q":"How does Kubernetes handle failover for Pods?","a":"<p>Controllers (for example a Deployment) reschedule failed Pods to healthy nodes.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0193","card":"q-kubernetes-022-updates-and-failover","sec":"kubernetes-s023","q":"What are the two Deployment strategies, and when would you pick each?","a":"<p><code>RollingUpdate</code> (default) replaces Pods gradually, so the app stays available. It needs the old and new versions to work side by side. <code>Recreate</code> kills all old Pods first, then starts new ones, which causes downtime but is needed when two versions cannot run at once, for example a breaking schema change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0194","card":"q-kubernetes-022-updates-and-failover","sec":"kubernetes-s023","q":"How do <code>maxSurge</code> and <code>maxUnavailable</code> control a rolling update?","a":"<p><code>maxSurge</code> is how many extra Pods above the desired count may exist during the update. <code>maxUnavailable</code> is how many Pods may be unavailable. Both default to 25%. For zero downtime set <code>maxUnavailable: 0</code> and <code>maxSurge: 1</code> (or higher), which needs spare capacity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0195","card":"q-kubernetes-022-updates-and-failover","sec":"kubernetes-s023","q":"How do you roll out a new image to a Deployment and check its progress?","a":"<p>Run <code>kubectl set image deployment/&lt;name&gt; &lt;container&gt;=&lt;image:tag&gt;</code> or apply an updated manifest. Watch with <code>kubectl rollout status deployment/&lt;name&gt;</code>, and see history with <code>kubectl rollout history</code>. A rollout stalls if new Pods never become Ready.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0196","card":"q-kubernetes-022-updates-and-failover","sec":"kubernetes-s023","q":"In Kubernetes, how does a rolling update avoid sending traffic to Pods that are not ready?","a":"<p>The Deployment waits for new Pods to be Ready before it removes old ones, and a Service only sends traffic to Pods that pass their readiness probe. Without a readiness probe, a Pod counts as ready as soon as the container starts, so users can hit a Pod that is still booting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0197","card":"q-kubernetes-022-updates-and-failover","sec":"kubernetes-s023","q":"What does Kubernetes do when a Pod or a whole node fails?","a":"<p>If a container crashes, the kubelet restarts it. If a Pod is deleted or its node is lost, the ReplicaSet creates a replacement and the scheduler places it on a healthy node. For a lost node, the node controller marks it NotReady, and after the toleration for <code>node.kubernetes.io/unreachable</code> (300 seconds by default) the Pods are evicted. Stateful Pods may need extra care because the volume must detach first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0198","card":"q-kubernetes-022-updates-and-failover","sec":"kubernetes-s023","q":"What measures make an application highly available on Kubernetes?","a":"<p>Run at least 2-3 replicas spread across nodes and zones (anti-affinity or topology spread), set readiness and liveness probes, define resource requests, add a PodDisruptionBudget so voluntary disruptions keep a minimum available, and use rolling updates with graceful shutdown.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0199","card":"q-kubernetes-022-updates-and-failover","sec":"kubernetes-s023","q":"What does a PodDisruptionBudget protect against?","a":"<p>Voluntary disruptions such as <code>kubectl drain</code> during node upgrades and cluster autoscaler scale-downs. It sets <code>minAvailable</code> or <code>maxUnavailable</code> so the eviction API refuses to remove too many replicas at once. It does not protect against involuntary failures such as node crashes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0200","card":"q-kubernetes-022-updates-and-failover","sec":"kubernetes-s023","q":"What happens when Kubernetes deletes a Pod, and how do <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> and a <code>preStop</code> hook help avoid dropped requests?","a":"<p>The Pod is marked Terminating and removed from Service endpoints, and in parallel the kubelet runs the <code>preStop</code> hook, then sends SIGTERM to the containers. If they have not exited after <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> (default 30) it sends SIGKILL. Endpoint removal and SIGTERM race, so some proxies keep sending traffic for a moment. A short <code>preStop</code> sleep gives time for endpoint removal to spread, the app should handle SIGTERM by finishing in-flight requests and then exiting, and the grace period must be longer than both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0201","card":"q-kubernetes-022-updates-and-failover","sec":"kubernetes-s023","q":"How do you roll out a new version of a Kubernetes Deployment without dropping requests?","a":"<p>Combine: a readiness probe so new Pods get traffic only when ready, <code>maxUnavailable: 0</code> with <code>maxSurge</code> of 1 or more, an app that handles SIGTERM gracefully, a <code>preStop</code> delay plus a sufficient <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code>, and a PodDisruptionBudget for voluntary disruptions. Also set <code>minReadySeconds</code> if you want new Pods to stay healthy for a while before the rollout continues.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0202","card":"q-kubernetes-023-liveness-and-readiness-probes","sec":"kubernetes-s024","q":"What is the role of liveness and readiness probes?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Liveness</strong>: restarts unhealthy Pods.</li>\n  <li><strong>Readiness</strong>: controls whether the Pod receives traffic.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0203","card":"q-kubernetes-023-liveness-and-readiness-probes","sec":"kubernetes-s024","q":"How do you configure liveness and readiness probes?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>livenessProbe:\n  httpGet:\n    path: /health\n    port: 80\n  initialDelaySeconds: 5\nreadinessProbe:\n  httpGet:\n    path: /ready\n    port: 80</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0204","card":"q-kubernetes-023-liveness-and-readiness-probes","sec":"kubernetes-s024","q":"What happens if a readiness probe fails?","a":"<p>The Pod is marked NotReady and traffic stops until the probe passes.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0205","card":"q-kubernetes-023-liveness-and-readiness-probes","sec":"kubernetes-s024","q":"In Kubernetes, how do liveness, readiness and startup probes differ?","a":"<p>Liveness: if it fails, the kubelet restarts the container. Readiness: if it fails, the Pod is removed from Service endpoints but not restarted. Startup: runs first for slow-starting apps, and liveness and readiness are disabled until it succeeds, which avoids a premature restart.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0206","card":"q-kubernetes-023-liveness-and-readiness-probes","sec":"kubernetes-s024","q":"What do the fields in this probe configuration do?","a":"<p><code>httpGet</code> makes the kubelet send an HTTP GET to <code>/health</code> on port 80. A status code from 200 to 399 is success. <code>initialDelaySeconds: 5</code> waits 5 seconds after the container starts before the first probe. Other tuning fields are <code>periodSeconds</code>, <code>timeoutSeconds</code>, <code>successThreshold</code> and <code>failureThreshold</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>livenessProbe:\n  httpGet:\n    path: /health\n    port: 80\n  initialDelaySeconds: 5</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0207","card":"q-kubernetes-023-liveness-and-readiness-probes","sec":"kubernetes-s024","q":"What other probe mechanisms does Kubernetes support besides <code>httpGet</code>?","a":"<p><code>tcpSocket</code> (success if the port accepts a connection), <code>exec</code> (runs a command in the container, success on exit code 0) and <code>grpc</code> (gRPC health checking protocol, stable since 1.27).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0208","card":"q-kubernetes-023-liveness-and-readiness-probes","sec":"kubernetes-s024","q":"What goes wrong if the liveness probe checks a downstream dependency such as the database?","a":"<p>When the database is down, every Pod fails liveness and is restarted, which does not fix the database and adds load and a restart storm. Keep liveness limited to whether the process itself is healthy or deadlocked. Put dependency checks in readiness, so Pods are only removed from traffic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0209","card":"q-kubernetes-023-liveness-and-readiness-probes","sec":"kubernetes-s024","q":"What happens when a readiness probe fails on a Pod behind a Service?","a":"<p>The Pod is marked NotReady and its IP is removed from the Service endpoints, so it gets no new traffic. The container keeps running. When the probe passes again, the Pod is added back.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0210","card":"q-kubernetes-023-liveness-and-readiness-probes","sec":"kubernetes-s024","q":"Pods restart repeatedly right after start-up although the app works when given more time. What is likely wrong?","a":"<p>The liveness probe starts too early or is too strict, so the kubelet kills the app before it is ready. Add a <code>startupProbe</code> or raise <code>initialDelaySeconds</code> and <code>failureThreshold</code>. Check <code>kubectl describe pod</code> for <code>Liveness probe failed</code> events.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0211","card":"q-kubernetes-024-rbac-roles-and-serviceaccounts","sec":"kubernetes-s025","q":"What is Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)?","a":"<p>It controls permissions by linking Roles and ClusterRoles to users through Bindings.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0212","card":"q-kubernetes-024-rbac-roles-and-serviceaccounts","sec":"kubernetes-s025","q":"What is the difference between a ClusterRole and a Role?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>ClusterRole</strong>: cluster-wide permissions.</li>\n  <li><strong>Role</strong>: namespace-specific permissions.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0213","card":"q-kubernetes-024-rbac-roles-and-serviceaccounts","sec":"kubernetes-s025","q":"What is a ServiceAccount, and how is it used?","a":"<p>It is an identity for Pods and provides an API token for app access.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0214","card":"q-kubernetes-024-rbac-roles-and-serviceaccounts","sec":"kubernetes-s025","q":"How do you create a Role and a RoleBinding?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Role\nmetadata:\n  name: pod-reader\nrules:\n- apiGroups: [&quot;&quot;]\n  resources: [&quot;pods&quot;]\n  verbs: [&quot;get&quot;, &quot;list&quot;]\n---\napiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\nkind: RoleBinding\nmetadata:\n  name: read-pods\nsubjects:\n- kind: User\n  name: &quot;user1&quot;\nroleRef:\n  kind: Role\n  name: pod-reader\n  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0215","card":"q-kubernetes-024-rbac-roles-and-serviceaccounts","sec":"kubernetes-s025","q":"How does Kubernetes RBAC grant permissions?","a":"<p>Permissions are only additive (there is no deny). A Role or ClusterRole lists rules: API groups, resources and verbs. A RoleBinding or ClusterRoleBinding attaches that role to subjects: users, groups or ServiceAccounts. Access is allowed if any binding grants it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0216","card":"q-kubernetes-024-rbac-roles-and-serviceaccounts","sec":"kubernetes-s025","q":"In Kubernetes RBAC, what is the difference between a Role and a ClusterRole, and between a RoleBinding and a ClusterRoleBinding?","a":"<p>A Role grants permissions inside one namespace. A ClusterRole is cluster-wide: it can cover non-namespaced resources (nodes, PersistentVolumes), non-resource URLs, or be reused across namespaces. A RoleBinding grants in one namespace and can reference a ClusterRole to reuse its rules there. A ClusterRoleBinding grants across all namespaces.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0217","card":"q-kubernetes-024-rbac-roles-and-serviceaccounts","sec":"kubernetes-s025","q":"What is a ServiceAccount and why would a Pod use a dedicated one?","a":"<p>A ServiceAccount is the identity a Pod uses when calling the Kubernetes API. Each namespace has a <code>default</code> one. Create a dedicated ServiceAccount with least-privilege RBAC for each app, so a compromise of one Pod does not give wide access. Set <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> for Pods that never call the API.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0218","card":"q-kubernetes-024-rbac-roles-and-serviceaccounts","sec":"kubernetes-s025","q":"In a Role rule, what do <code>apiGroups</code>, <code>resources</code> and <code>verbs</code> mean?","a":"<p><code>apiGroups: [\"\"]</code> is the core API group (Pods, Services, ConfigMaps). <code>apps</code> would be needed for Deployments. <code>resources</code> lists the object types. <code>verbs</code> lists the allowed actions. This rule lets the subject read and list Pods, but not create, delete or exec into them.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>rules:\n- apiGroups: [&quot;&quot;]\n  resources: [&quot;pods&quot;]\n  verbs: [&quot;get&quot;, &quot;list&quot;]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0219","card":"q-kubernetes-024-rbac-roles-and-serviceaccounts","sec":"kubernetes-s025","q":"How do you check what a user or ServiceAccount can do in Kubernetes?","a":"<p>Use <code>kubectl auth can-i &lt;verb&gt; &lt;resource&gt; --as &lt;user&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;</code>, for example <code>--as system:serviceaccount:&lt;ns&gt;:&lt;name&gt;</code>. <code>kubectl auth can-i --list</code> shows all permissions. This is the fastest way to debug a <code>Forbidden</code> error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0220","card":"q-kubernetes-024-rbac-roles-and-serviceaccounts","sec":"kubernetes-s025","q":"How do users authenticate to a Kubernetes cluster, since there is no User object?","a":"<p>Kubernetes does not store users. The API server trusts external identity: client certificates, bearer tokens, OIDC (SSO through an identity provider) and on cloud clusters the provider's IAM integration, such as EKS using IAM and IRSA or Pod Identity for Pods. The identity gets name and groups, which RBAC then matches in bindings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0221","card":"q-kubernetes-025-authentication-authorization-and-hardeni","sec":"kubernetes-s026","q":"How does Kubernetes handle authentication and authorization?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Authentication</strong>: certs, tokens, OIDC.</li>\n  <li><strong>Authorization</strong>: RBAC, ABAC, or webhooks.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0222","card":"q-kubernetes-025-authentication-authorization-and-hardeni","sec":"kubernetes-s026","q":"How do you secure the API server?","a":"<p>Use TLS and RBAC, disable anonymous access, and restrict network access.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0223","card":"q-kubernetes-025-authentication-authorization-and-hardeni","sec":"kubernetes-s026","q":"What are Pod Security Policies (PSPs)?","a":"<p>They enforce Pod security rules (for example no root access). PSPs are deprecated in favor of Pod Security Admission (PSA).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0224","card":"q-kubernetes-025-authentication-authorization-and-hardeni","sec":"kubernetes-s026","q":"What are some best practices for securing a cluster?","a":"<p>Use RBAC and TLS, encrypt Secrets, limit privileges, and enable audit logs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0225","card":"q-kubernetes-025-authentication-authorization-and-hardeni","sec":"kubernetes-s026","q":"How do you audit and monitor security events?","a":"<p>Enable API audit logs and use tools like Prometheus or Falco.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0226","card":"q-kubernetes-025-authentication-authorization-and-hardeni","sec":"kubernetes-s026","q":"What are the stages a request goes through in the Kubernetes API server?","a":"<p>Authentication (who are you: certificate, token, OIDC), authorization (are you allowed: RBAC, Node, webhook), then admission control (mutating and validating admission webhooks and built-in controllers such as Pod Security Admission, which can change or reject the object), then it is validated and stored in etcd.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0227","card":"q-kubernetes-025-authentication-authorization-and-hardeni","sec":"kubernetes-s026","q":"Pod Security Policies were used to restrict privileged Pods. What replaced them?","a":"<p>PodSecurityPolicy was deprecated in 1.21 and removed in Kubernetes 1.25. Pod Security Admission replaced it (stable in 1.25). It enforces the three Pod Security Standards (privileged, baseline, restricted) per namespace through labels. For custom rules use policy engines such as Kyverno or OPA Gatekeeper.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0228","card":"q-kubernetes-025-authentication-authorization-and-hardeni","sec":"kubernetes-s026","q":"How do you harden a container's security context in a Pod?","a":"<p>Set <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code> with a specific <code>runAsUser</code>, <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code>, <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code>, drop all Linux capabilities and add back only what is needed, and use the <code>RuntimeDefault</code> seccomp profile. Avoid <code>privileged: true</code> and <code>hostPath</code> mounts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0229","card":"q-kubernetes-025-authentication-authorization-and-hardeni","sec":"kubernetes-s026","q":"How do you secure the Kubernetes API server?","a":"<p>Serve only over TLS, disable anonymous auth, use RBAC (and no <code>cluster-admin</code> for apps), enable audit logging, enable admission controllers, keep the API endpoint off the public internet or restrict it by CIDR, and keep Kubernetes patched.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0230","card":"q-kubernetes-025-authentication-authorization-and-hardeni","sec":"kubernetes-s026","q":"How do you audit and detect suspicious activity in a cluster?","a":"<p>Enable API server audit logs and ship them to a log system. Use runtime security tools such as Falco to detect behaviour like a shell in a container or a sensitive file read, and scan images in CI. Monitor failed authentication and RBAC denials.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0231","card":"q-kubernetes-025-authentication-authorization-and-hardeni","sec":"kubernetes-s026","q":"What is the principle of least privilege in a cluster, and how do you apply it?","a":"<p>Give each identity only the permissions it needs. Use namespaced Roles over ClusterRoles, specific verbs and resource names, dedicated ServiceAccounts, restricted Pod Security settings, NetworkPolicies, and no privileged Pods. Review bindings regularly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0232","card":"q-kubernetes-026-restricting-traffic-with-network-policie","sec":"kubernetes-s027","q":"How do you restrict network traffic using Network Policies?","a":"<p>Select the target Pods and list which Pods may reach them. This policy lets only <code>frontend</code> Pods reach <code>backend</code> Pods on port 80.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: allow-specific\nspec:\n  podSelector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: backend\n  ingress:\n  - from:\n    - podSelector:\n        matchLabels:\n          app: frontend\n    ports:\n    - protocol: TCP\n      port: 80</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0233","card":"q-kubernetes-026-restricting-traffic-with-network-policie","sec":"kubernetes-s027","q":"What does this NetworkPolicy allow and what does it block?","a":"<p>It applies to Pods labelled <code>app: backend</code> in its own namespace. Incoming traffic is allowed only from Pods labelled <code>app: frontend</code> (same namespace) on TCP port 80. All other ingress to those backend Pods is dropped. Egress is not restricted, because no egress policy type is set. Other Pods are unaffected.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: allow-specific\nspec:\n  podSelector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: backend\n  ingress:\n  - from:\n    - podSelector:\n        matchLabels:\n          app: frontend\n    ports:\n    - protocol: TCP\n      port: 80</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0234","card":"q-kubernetes-026-restricting-traffic-with-network-policie","sec":"kubernetes-s027","q":"In a NetworkPolicy, what is the difference between <code>spec.podSelector</code> and the <code>podSelector</code> inside <code>from</code>?","a":"<p><code>spec.podSelector</code> chooses the Pods the policy protects (the destination of ingress). The <code>podSelector</code> in <code>from</code> chooses the allowed source Pods. If a Pod is not matched by any policy's <code>spec.podSelector</code>, it stays fully open.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0235","card":"q-kubernetes-026-restricting-traffic-with-network-policie","sec":"kubernetes-s027","q":"How do you allow traffic from a specific namespace in a NetworkPolicy?","a":"<p>Use <code>namespaceSelector</code> in the <code>from</code> entry, matching namespace labels such as <code>kubernetes.io/metadata.name: monitoring</code>. If <code>podSelector</code> and <code>namespaceSelector</code> are in the same list item they are ANDed (those Pods in those namespaces). In separate list items they are ORed. This indentation detail is a common mistake.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0236","card":"q-kubernetes-026-restricting-traffic-with-network-policie","sec":"kubernetes-s027","q":"After you lock down a namespace with default-deny egress, Pods can no longer resolve DNS names. How do you fix it?","a":"<p>Add an egress rule allowing UDP and TCP port 53 to the cluster DNS Pods (CoreDNS in <code>kube-system</code>), usually with a <code>namespaceSelector</code> and <code>podSelector</code> for them. Then add rules for other needed destinations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0237","card":"q-kubernetes-027-inspecting-pods-and-logs","sec":"kubernetes-s028","q":"How do you check the status of Pods in a cluster?","a":"<p><code>kubectl get pods --all-namespaces -o wide</code></p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0238","card":"q-kubernetes-027-inspecting-pods-and-logs","sec":"kubernetes-s028","q":"What is the purpose of <code>kubectl describe</code>?","a":"<p>It shows detailed resource information and is useful for debugging events.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0239","card":"q-kubernetes-027-inspecting-pods-and-logs","sec":"kubernetes-s028","q":"How do you view logs of a Pod?","a":"<p><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod-name&gt; -c &lt;container&gt;</code></p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0240","card":"q-kubernetes-027-inspecting-pods-and-logs","sec":"kubernetes-s028","q":"What does <code>kubectl get events</code> do?","a":"<p>It lists cluster events, which helps identify issues.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0241","card":"q-kubernetes-027-inspecting-pods-and-logs","sec":"kubernetes-s028","q":"How do you list Pods in every namespace with their node and IP in Kubernetes?","a":"<p>Run <code>kubectl get pods --all-namespaces -o wide</code> (or <code>-A -o wide</code>). <code>-o wide</code> adds the node, IP and more. Add <code>--show-labels</code> to see labels, or <code>-l app=web</code> to filter by label.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0242","card":"q-kubernetes-027-inspecting-pods-and-logs","sec":"kubernetes-s028","q":"What does <code>kubectl describe pod</code> show that <code>kubectl get pod</code> does not?","a":"<p>It shows the full spec and status plus an Events section at the bottom: scheduling decisions, image pulls, probe failures, mount errors, OOM kills and restarts. It is the first command for finding out why a Pod is Pending, not starting or restarting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0243","card":"q-kubernetes-027-inspecting-pods-and-logs","sec":"kubernetes-s028","q":"How do you read logs from a specific container of a multi-container Pod, and from a container that restarted?","a":"<p>Use <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt;</code> to pick the container. Add <code>--previous</code> (<code>-p</code>) to see the logs of the last terminated instance, which is what you need for crash loops. Other options: <code>-f</code> to follow, <code>--since=10m</code>, <code>--tail=100</code>, and <code>-l app=web</code> to read by label.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0244","card":"q-kubernetes-027-inspecting-pods-and-logs","sec":"kubernetes-s028","q":"When would you use <code>kubectl get events</code> rather than <code>describe</code>?","a":"<p>To see cluster-wide or namespace-wide events in one list, for example during a failing rollout. Sort them with <code>--sort-by=.lastTimestamp</code>. Events are short-lived (about an hour by default), so check them soon after the problem.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0245","card":"q-kubernetes-027-inspecting-pods-and-logs","sec":"kubernetes-s028","q":"How do you get a shell in a running container and what if the image has no shell?","a":"<p>Run <code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt; -- sh</code>. For minimal or distroless images with no shell, use an ephemeral debug container: <code>kubectl debug -it &lt;pod&gt; --image=busybox --target=&lt;container&gt;</code> (ephemeral containers are stable since Kubernetes 1.25).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0246","card":"q-kubernetes-027-inspecting-pods-and-logs","sec":"kubernetes-s028","q":"How do you control the output of <code>kubectl get</code> for scripts, and when do you use <code>-o yaml</code>, <code>-o jsonpath</code>, <code>-o custom-columns</code> and <code>--sort-by</code>?","a":"<p><code>-o yaml</code> or <code>-o json</code> show the full object, useful to inspect fields or copy a manifest. <code>-o jsonpath</code> extracts specific values for scripts, for example <code>kubectl get pod web -o jsonpath='{.status.podIP}'</code>. <code>-o custom-columns</code> builds a readable table of chosen fields, for example <code>-o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,NODE:.spec.nodeName</code>. <code>--sort-by</code> orders the list by a field path, for example <code>--sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp</code>. <code>-o name</code> gives just resource names for piping.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0247","card":"q-kubernetes-028-debugging-failures","sec":"kubernetes-s029","q":"How do you debug a Pod that is crashing?","a":"<p>Check logs (<code>kubectl logs --previous</code>), events, and the Pod spec.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0248","card":"q-kubernetes-028-debugging-failures","sec":"kubernetes-s029","q":"What are common reasons for a Pod to be in CrashLoopBackOff?","a":"<p>Application errors, a bad image, resource limits, or probe failures.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0249","card":"q-kubernetes-028-debugging-failures","sec":"kubernetes-s029","q":"How do you troubleshoot a service that is not responding?","a":"<p>Check endpoints, Pods, and networking, and test with <code>kubectl port-forward</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0250","card":"q-kubernetes-028-debugging-failures","sec":"kubernetes-s029","q":"A Pod is in CrashLoopBackOff. Walk through how you debug it.","a":"<p>Run <code>kubectl describe pod</code> for the last state, exit code and events. Read the crashed container's logs with <code>kubectl logs --previous</code>. Common causes: application error or bad config or missing env, wrong command or image, OOMKilled (exit 137) from a too-low memory limit, and a liveness probe killing a slow-starting app. Fix the cause. Do not just delete the Pod.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0251","card":"q-kubernetes-028-debugging-failures","sec":"kubernetes-s029","q":"What is the difference between ImagePullBackOff and CrashLoopBackOff?","a":"<p>ImagePullBackOff (or ErrImagePull) means the kubelet cannot pull the image: wrong name or tag, missing registry credentials (<code>imagePullSecrets</code>), or network or rate limit problems. The container never started. CrashLoopBackOff means the image was pulled and the container starts but keeps exiting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0252","card":"q-kubernetes-028-debugging-failures","sec":"kubernetes-s029","q":"A Service is not responding. How do you troubleshoot in order?","a":"<p>Check the Pods are Running and Ready. Check the Service has endpoints (<code>kubectl get endpoints</code>) and that its selector matches the Pod labels. Verify <code>targetPort</code> matches the container port. Test from inside the cluster with a debug Pod and <code>curl</code>, or from the workstation with <code>kubectl port-forward</code>. Then check NetworkPolicies, Ingress rules and DNS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0253","card":"q-kubernetes-028-debugging-failures","sec":"kubernetes-s029","q":"What does <code>kubectl port-forward</code> do and when would you use it?","a":"<p>It opens a tunnel from a local port to a Pod or Service port through the API server, such as <code>kubectl port-forward svc/web 8080:80</code>. Use it to test or debug an app without exposing it. It forwards to a single Pod and is not for production traffic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0254","card":"q-kubernetes-028-debugging-failures","sec":"kubernetes-s029","q":"A Pod shows exit code 137. What does that mean?","a":"<p>The container was killed with SIGKILL (128 + 9). The usual cause is the OOM killer after exceeding its memory limit (reason <code>OOMKilled</code>), but it can also be a kill after a failed graceful shutdown. Check <code>kubectl describe pod</code> for the reason and raise the memory limit or fix the leak.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0255","card":"q-kubernetes-029-metrics-and-dashboards","sec":"kubernetes-s030","q":"What is Prometheus, and how is it used with Kubernetes?","a":"<p>It monitors metrics by scraping cluster and app data for alerts and visualization.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0256","card":"q-kubernetes-029-metrics-and-dashboards","sec":"kubernetes-s030","q":"How do you monitor resource usage in a cluster?","a":"<p>Use <code>kubectl top</code>, Metrics Server, or Prometheus/Grafana.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0257","card":"q-kubernetes-029-metrics-and-dashboards","sec":"kubernetes-s030","q":"What is the Kubernetes Dashboard, and how do you access it?","a":"<p>A UI for cluster management, reached through <code>kubectl proxy</code> and a token.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0258","card":"q-kubernetes-029-metrics-and-dashboards","sec":"kubernetes-s030","q":"How does Prometheus collect metrics from Kubernetes workloads?","a":"<p>Prometheus pulls metrics over HTTP from <code>/metrics</code> endpoints of targets. In Kubernetes it discovers targets through the API using service discovery, or with the Prometheus Operator's ServiceMonitor and PodMonitor objects. It stores time series and evaluates alert rules, and Grafana is used for dashboards.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0259","card":"q-kubernetes-029-metrics-and-dashboards","sec":"kubernetes-s030","q":"What is the difference between the Metrics Server and Prometheus?","a":"<p>Metrics Server keeps only current CPU and memory usage in memory, and serves it through the Metrics API for <code>kubectl top</code> and for the HPA. It has no history. Prometheus stores long-term metrics from many targets, supports queries, alerting and custom application metrics.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0260","card":"q-kubernetes-029-metrics-and-dashboards","sec":"kubernetes-s030","q":"Which Kubernetes-related things would you alert on?","a":"<p>Pod restarts and CrashLoopBackOff, Pods stuck in Pending or not Ready, node NotReady, high CPU or memory against limits, PVC nearly full, failed Jobs, deployment replicas not matching desired, API server latency and error rates, and certificate expiry.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0261","card":"q-kubernetes-029-metrics-and-dashboards","sec":"kubernetes-s030","q":"In a Kubernetes monitoring stack, what do kube-state-metrics, node-exporter and cAdvisor each report?","a":"<p>kube-state-metrics turns the state of API objects into metrics, such as desired versus available replicas, Pod phase and Job status. node-exporter reports host-level metrics of each node, such as CPU, memory, disk and network. cAdvisor (built into the kubelet) reports per-container resource usage. They answer different questions: what Kubernetes thinks should be running, how the machine is doing, and how much each container uses. Metrics Server is different: it only serves current CPU and memory for <code>kubectl top</code> and the HPA, with no history.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0262","card":"q-kubernetes-030-deploying-from-pipelines","sec":"kubernetes-s031","q":"How do you integrate Kubernetes with a CI/CD pipeline?","a":"<p>Build and test in CI, then deploy via <code>kubectl apply</code> or Helm in CD.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0263","card":"q-kubernetes-030-deploying-from-pipelines","sec":"kubernetes-s031","q":"How do you automate deployments?","a":"<p>Use CI/CD pipelines, GitOps tools, or scripted <code>kubectl</code> commands.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0264","card":"q-kubernetes-030-deploying-from-pipelines","sec":"kubernetes-s031","q":"What is the difference between <code>kubectl apply</code> and <code>kubectl create</code>?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Apply</strong>: declarative, updates existing resources.</li>\n  <li><strong>Create</strong>: imperative, creates a resource once.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0265","card":"q-kubernetes-030-deploying-from-pipelines","sec":"kubernetes-s031","q":"How do you roll back a deployment?","a":"<p><code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/&lt;name&gt;</code></p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0266","card":"q-kubernetes-030-deploying-from-pipelines","sec":"kubernetes-s031","q":"How do you manage multiple environments (dev, staging, prod)?","a":"<p>Use Namespaces, ConfigMaps, Helm values, or Kustomize overlays.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0267","card":"q-kubernetes-030-deploying-from-pipelines","sec":"kubernetes-s031","q":"How would you design a CI/CD pipeline that deploys an app to Kubernetes?","a":"<p>CI: build the image, run tests and scans, tag with the commit SHA (not only <code>latest</code>) and push to a registry. CD: update the manifest or Helm values with the new tag and deploy using <code>kubectl apply</code>, Helm or a GitOps tool. Then wait for <code>kubectl rollout status</code>, run smoke tests, and roll back on failure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0268","card":"q-kubernetes-030-deploying-from-pipelines","sec":"kubernetes-s031","q":"How does <code>kubectl apply</code> differ from <code>kubectl create</code>?","a":"<p><code>create</code> is imperative: it fails if the object already exists. <code>apply</code> is declarative: it creates the object or updates it to match the manifest, keeping the last applied state so it can compute changes. Pipelines use <code>apply</code> so they are repeatable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0269","card":"q-kubernetes-030-deploying-from-pipelines","sec":"kubernetes-s031","q":"In a Helm or GitOps pipeline, how do you roll back a bad release?","a":"<p>With Helm, run <code>helm rollback &lt;release&gt; &lt;revision&gt;</code> (see <code>helm history &lt;release&gt;</code> for revisions). In GitOps, revert the Git commit and let the controller (Argo CD, Flux) sync the cluster back, so Git stays the source of truth. Do not only undo by hand in the cluster, because the controller would reapply the bad version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0270","card":"q-kubernetes-030-deploying-from-pipelines","sec":"kubernetes-s031","q":"How do you manage multiple environments such as dev, staging and prod?","a":"<p>Keep the same base manifests and vary the environment-specific parts: separate namespaces or clusters, Helm values files per environment, or Kustomize overlays. Production usually goes in its own cluster or account for isolation. Promote the same image through environments rather than rebuilding.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0271","card":"q-kubernetes-030-deploying-from-pipelines","sec":"kubernetes-s031","q":"What credentials should a CI pipeline use to deploy to a cluster?","a":"<p>A dedicated identity with namespace-scoped RBAC (deploy rights only), not cluster-admin. Prefer short-lived credentials such as OIDC federation to the cloud provider over long-lived kubeconfig files stored as CI secrets. Alternatively use GitOps so CI never needs cluster credentials.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0272","card":"q-kubernetes-030-deploying-from-pipelines","sec":"kubernetes-s031","q":"How would you do a canary or blue-green release on Kubernetes?","a":"<p>Blue-green: run two Deployments (blue and green) and switch the Service selector to the new one, with an instant rollback by switching back. Canary: run a small share of the new version alongside, by replica ratio or by traffic weights with an Ingress, service mesh or Argo Rollouts, and promote as metrics stay healthy. Plain Deployments only offer rolling updates.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0273","card":"q-kubernetes-030-deploying-from-pipelines","sec":"kubernetes-s031","q":"In a Kubernetes CI/CD pipeline, how do <code>kubectl diff</code> and <code>kubectl apply --dry-run=server</code> help before deploying?","a":"<p><code>kubectl diff -f manifest.yaml</code> shows what would change in the live objects compared with the manifest, so a reviewer can approve it. <code>--dry-run=server</code> sends the request to the API server, which runs validation and admission webhooks without persisting anything, so it catches errors that <code>--dry-run=client</code> cannot, such as policy rejections. Use them as a gate before the real <code>kubectl apply</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0274","card":"q-kubernetes-031-gitops","sec":"kubernetes-s032","q":"What is GitOps, and how does it work with Kubernetes?","a":"<p>Git is the source of truth, and tools sync manifests to the cluster automatically.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0275","card":"q-kubernetes-031-gitops","sec":"kubernetes-s032","q":"How do you use ArgoCD or Flux?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>ArgoCD</strong>: UI-driven sync.</li>\n  <li><strong>Flux</strong>: lightweight Git watcher.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0276","card":"q-kubernetes-031-gitops","sec":"kubernetes-s032","q":"What is GitOps, and what are its benefits over pushing manifests from a CI pipeline?","a":"<p>Git is the single source of truth for desired state, and an agent in the cluster (Argo CD or Flux) pulls and applies changes automatically. Benefits: an audit trail and review through pull requests, easy rollback by git revert, drift detection and self-healing back to the Git state, and no cluster credentials stored in CI.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0277","card":"q-kubernetes-031-gitops","sec":"kubernetes-s032","q":"How do Argo CD and Flux differ?","a":"<p>Both reconcile a cluster with Git. Argo CD has a web UI, an Application resource and a visual diff and sync view, and is popular for multi-cluster and teams that want visibility. Flux is a set of lightweight controllers (GitOps Toolkit) driven by CRDs and the CLI, and is more modular with no built-in UI.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0278","card":"q-kubernetes-032-helm","sec":"kubernetes-s033","q":"What is Helm, and how does it simplify deployments?","a":"<p>A package manager that uses charts to templatize and deploy applications.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0279","card":"q-kubernetes-032-helm","sec":"kubernetes-s033","q":"What are Helm charts, and how do you create one?","a":"<p>Bundled manifests. Run <code>helm create &lt;name&gt;</code> and edit <code>values.yaml</code>.</p>\n<p>A simple web application chart has these files.</p>\n<p><code>Chart.yaml</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v2\nname: nginx-chart\nversion: 0.1.0</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>values.yaml</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>replicaCount: 3\nimage:\n  repository: nginx\n  tag: latest\nservice:\n  type: LoadBalancer\n  port: 80</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>templates/deployment.yaml</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: {{ .Release.Name }}-nginx\nspec:\n  replicas: {{ .Values.replicaCount }}\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: nginx\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: nginx\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: nginx\n        image: {{ .Values.image.repository }}:{{ .Values.image.tag }}\n        ports:\n        - containerPort: 80</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>templates/service.yaml</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: {{ .Release.Name }}-nginx-service\nspec:\n  selector:\n    app: nginx\n  ports:\n  - port: {{ .Values.service.port }}\n    targetPort: 80\n  type: {{ .Values.service.type }}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0280","card":"q-kubernetes-032-helm","sec":"kubernetes-s033","q":"What problem does Helm solve compared to applying raw YAML?","a":"<p>Raw manifests repeat values and differ per environment. Helm packages them as a chart with Go templates and a <code>values.yaml</code>, so one chart deploys to many environments with overrides. Helm also tracks releases, so you can upgrade, see history and roll back.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0281","card":"q-kubernetes-032-helm","sec":"kubernetes-s033","q":"What is the role of <code>Chart.yaml</code>, <code>values.yaml</code> and the <code>templates</code> directory in a Helm chart?","a":"<p><code>Chart.yaml</code> has chart metadata: name, chart version, <code>apiVersion: v2</code> for Helm 3 and dependencies. <code>values.yaml</code> has the default configuration. <code>templates/</code> contains the Kubernetes manifests with placeholders. Helm renders templates with the values and sends the result to the cluster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0282","card":"q-kubernetes-032-helm","sec":"kubernetes-s033","q":"In a Helm template, what do these expressions mean?","a":"<p><code>{{ .Release.Name }}</code> is a built-in object holding the name given at install time, so the same chart can be installed several times without name clashes. <code>{{ .Values.replicaCount }}</code> reads the value from <code>values.yaml</code> or from an override such as <code>--set replicaCount=5</code> or <code>-f prod-values.yaml</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>metadata:\n  name: {{ .Release.Name }}-nginx\nspec:\n  replicas: {{ .Values.replicaCount }}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0283","card":"q-kubernetes-032-helm","sec":"kubernetes-s033","q":"How do you override chart values at install time in Helm, and which wins?","a":"<p>Use <code>-f custom.yaml</code> and/or <code>--set key=value</code>. Precedence from lowest to highest: chart <code>values.yaml</code>, then <code>-f</code> files in order, then <code>--set</code>. Prefer values files in Git over <code>--set</code> for repeatability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0284","card":"q-kubernetes-032-helm","sec":"kubernetes-s033","q":"How do you preview what a Helm chart will deploy and how do you upgrade and roll back?","a":"<p><code>helm template</code> renders manifests locally and <code>helm install --dry-run</code> or <code>helm diff</code> (plugin) shows changes. <code>helm upgrade --install &lt;release&gt; &lt;chart&gt;</code> upgrades or installs, <code>helm history</code> lists revisions and <code>helm rollback &lt;release&gt; &lt;revision&gt;</code> reverts. <code>helm lint</code> checks the chart.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0285","card":"q-kubernetes-032-helm","sec":"kubernetes-s033","q":"What changed between Helm 2 and Helm 3?","a":"<p>Helm 3 (2019) removed Tiller, the in-cluster server component with broad permissions. The Helm client talks to the API server using the user's own kubeconfig and RBAC, and release data is stored as Secrets in the release's namespace. Chart <code>apiVersion: v2</code> was introduced.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0286","card":"q-kubernetes-032-helm","sec":"kubernetes-s033","q":"When would you choose Kustomize over Helm?","a":"<p>Kustomize patches plain YAML with overlays and has no templating language. It is built into <code>kubectl -k</code> and suits in-house apps with small per-environment differences. Helm suits packaging and distributing apps with many options, dependencies and release management. Many teams use both, for example Argo CD rendering a Helm chart and patching it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0287","card":"q-kubernetes-033-extending-kubernetes","sec":"kubernetes-s034","q":"What is a Custom Resource Definition (CRD)?","a":"<p>It extends the API with custom resources (for example MyApp).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0288","card":"q-kubernetes-033-extending-kubernetes","sec":"kubernetes-s034","q":"How do you extend Kubernetes with custom controllers?","a":"<p>Write logic (for example in Go) that manages CRD instances.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0289","card":"q-kubernetes-033-extending-kubernetes","sec":"kubernetes-s034","q":"What is a Kubernetes operator, and how does it work?","a":"<p>A custom controller that manages an application's lifecycle via CRDs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0290","card":"q-kubernetes-033-extending-kubernetes","sec":"kubernetes-s034","q":"What is a sidecar container, and how is it used?","a":"<p>A helper container in a Pod that adds logging, proxying, and similar functions.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0291","card":"q-kubernetes-033-extending-kubernetes","sec":"kubernetes-s034","q":"In Kubernetes, what does applying a CustomResourceDefinition (CRD) actually do, and why is a CRD alone not enough to get behavior?","a":"<p>A CRD registers a new resource type with the API server. The API server then serves a REST endpoint for it, validates objects against its schema, and stores them in etcd. Nothing acts on those objects. You need a controller that watches them and creates or changes real resources. CRD plus controller is the operator pattern.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0292","card":"q-kubernetes-033-extending-kubernetes","sec":"kubernetes-s034","q":"What is the reconcile loop in a Kubernetes controller, and why must a reconcile function be idempotent?","a":"<p>The controller reads the desired state (the spec of a resource), reads the actual state, and takes the steps that close the gap. It repeats this on every change and on a periodic resync. Events can be duplicated, delayed or missed, so the same reconcile may run many times for one object. Running it twice must give the same result as running it once. Controllers should be level-triggered (act on the current state) and not edge-triggered (act on \"what just happened\").</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0293","card":"q-kubernetes-033-extending-kubernetes","sec":"kubernetes-s034","q":"When would you write an operator instead of using a Helm chart for an application?","a":"<p>Use a Helm chart when installing and upgrading is enough. Write an operator when the application needs ongoing, application-specific operations after install: failover, backups and restores, version upgrades with data migrations, scaling that needs app knowledge. Helm renders templates once. An operator keeps watching and reacting. The cost of an operator is code to build, test and maintain.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0294","card":"q-kubernetes-033-extending-kubernetes","sec":"kubernetes-s034","q":"What frameworks can you use to build a Kubernetes operator?","a":"<p>Kubebuilder and the Operator SDK (Go, built on controller-runtime) are the most common. Kopf is a Python framework. Java Operator SDK exists for Java. Go is the usual choice because client-go and controller-runtime are the reference implementations with caching, leader election and work queues. Pick the language your team can maintain.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0295","card":"q-kubernetes-033-extending-kubernetes","sec":"kubernetes-s034","q":"In a Kubernetes custom resource, why do you need a finalizer when the operator must clean up something outside the cluster?","a":"<p>When a user deletes the resource, Kubernetes removes it immediately unless it has a finalizer. A finalizer is a string in <code>metadata.finalizers</code>. The object stays in a terminating state (with <code>deletionTimestamp</code> set) until the controller finishes its cleanup, for example deleting a cloud database, and removes the finalizer. Without it the object disappears and the controller never gets the chance to clean up.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0296","card":"q-kubernetes-033-extending-kubernetes","sec":"kubernetes-s034","q":"What is a sidecar container in Kubernetes, and why use one instead of building the feature into the application?","a":"<p>A sidecar is a second container in the same Pod that adds a capability next to the main container: log shipping, a proxy (service mesh, TLS termination), config reloading, a credential refresher. It shares the Pod network and can share volumes. You get the feature without changing the application code or image, in any language, and you can update it independently. The cost is extra CPU and memory in every Pod.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0297","card":"q-kubernetes-033-extending-kubernetes","sec":"kubernetes-s034","q":"In Kubernetes 1.29 and later, how do native sidecar containers differ from a regular second container in a Pod?","a":"<p>A native sidecar is declared in <code>initContainers</code> with <code>restartPolicy: Always</code>. It starts before the main containers, keeps running next to them, and is stopped after them. This fixes two problems of the old pattern: the sidecar was not guaranteed to be ready before the app starts, and a sidecar in a Job kept the Job from completing. The feature is beta and on by default from 1.29 and stable in 1.33.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0298","card":"q-kubernetes-033-extending-kubernetes","sec":"kubernetes-s034","q":"What is the difference between a sidecar and a DaemonSet logging agent?","a":"<p>A sidecar runs in every Pod that needs it, so it can be configured per application and sees that Pod's volumes and network. A DaemonSet agent runs once per node and collects logs of all Pods on that node from the node's log files. The DaemonSet uses fewer resources. The sidecar is needed when logs are in files inside the container, or when the app needs its own proxy or settings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0299","card":"q-kubernetes-033-extending-kubernetes","sec":"kubernetes-s034","q":"What are mutating and validating admission webhooks in Kubernetes, and how do they extend it?","a":"<p>They are HTTP callbacks the API server calls while processing a create or update request, after authentication and authorization. A mutating webhook can modify the object (for example inject a sidecar, as service meshes do). A validating webhook can only accept or reject it (for example enforce that every Pod has resource limits). They let you enforce policy and change defaults without changing Kubernetes itself. A broken webhook can block all creates, so set <code>failurePolicy</code> and scope it with selectors deliberately.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0300","card":"q-kubernetes-034-platform-and-operations","sec":"kubernetes-s035","q":"How does Kubernetes handle multi-tenancy?","a":"<p>Namespaces, RBAC, and quotas. Isolation is limited without additional tools.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0301","card":"q-kubernetes-034-platform-and-operations","sec":"kubernetes-s035","q":"What is Federation, and when is it used?","a":"<p>It manages multiple clusters and is used for multi-region setups or disaster recovery.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0302","card":"q-kubernetes-034-platform-and-operations","sec":"kubernetes-s035","q":"How do you upgrade a cluster without downtime?","a":"<p>Do rolling upgrades of the control plane and nodes: drain, update, uncordon.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0303","card":"q-kubernetes-034-platform-and-operations","sec":"kubernetes-s035","q":"What is the difference between Kubernetes and OpenShift?","a":"<p>OpenShift is Kubernetes plus enterprise features (UI, CI/CD, security).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0304","card":"q-kubernetes-034-platform-and-operations","sec":"kubernetes-s035","q":"How do you configure a cluster on a cloud provider?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>EKS</strong>: <code>eksctl create</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>GKE</strong>: <code>gcloud container</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>AKS</strong>: <code>az aks</code>.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0305","card":"q-kubernetes-034-platform-and-operations","sec":"kubernetes-s035","q":"What is KubeVirt, and how does it integrate VMs with Kubernetes?","a":"<p>It runs VMs as Pods, using CRDs and QEMU.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0306","card":"q-kubernetes-034-platform-and-operations","sec":"kubernetes-s035","q":"How does Kubernetes handle disaster recovery?","a":"<p>Back up etcd, replicate clusters, and use external storage.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0307","card":"q-kubernetes-034-platform-and-operations","sec":"kubernetes-s035","q":"How would you build multi-tenancy in a shared Kubernetes cluster, and where does it fall short?","a":"<p>Combine one namespace per tenant, RBAC scoped to that namespace, ResourceQuota and LimitRange for fair resource use, NetworkPolicies for network isolation, and Pod Security Admission to restrict privileged Pods. This is soft multi-tenancy. The tenants still share the kernel, the API server and cluster-wide resources, so a node escape or a noisy neighbour can cross tenants. For untrusted tenants use separate clusters, virtual clusters (such as vcluster), or sandboxed runtimes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0308","card":"q-kubernetes-034-platform-and-operations","sec":"kubernetes-s035","q":"Why would an organization run several Kubernetes clusters instead of one big cluster?","a":"<p>Fault isolation (a bad upgrade or control plane failure hits only one cluster), regional placement for latency and data residency, hard separation of prod from non-prod and of tenants, compliance, and staying under per-cluster scale limits. The cost is more to operate: upgrades, policy, observability and deployments must be repeated or centralized per cluster. Tools like Argo CD ApplicationSets or Karmada manage workloads across clusters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0309","card":"q-kubernetes-034-platform-and-operations","sec":"kubernetes-s035","q":"What happened to Kubernetes Federation (KubeFed), and what do teams use for multi-cluster today?","a":"<p>KubeFed was the original project for pushing the same resources to several clusters. The project is archived and not recommended for new use. Teams use GitOps with one definition applied to many clusters (Argo CD ApplicationSets, Flux), Karmada for propagation and scheduling across clusters, and a multi-cluster service mesh or global load balancer for traffic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0310","card":"q-kubernetes-034-platform-and-operations","sec":"kubernetes-s035","q":"How do you upgrade a Kubernetes cluster without downtime, and in what order?","a":"<p>Upgrade one minor version at a time. First the control plane, then the nodes, because the kubelet may not be newer than the API server (since 1.28 it may be up to three minor versions older). For each node: cordon, drain, upgrade kubelet and kubeadm or replace the node with a new image, then uncordon. Run several replicas of each workload and set PodDisruptionBudgets so the drain keeps the app available. On managed services use surge or blue-green node pool upgrades. Read the release notes for removed APIs first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0311","card":"q-kubernetes-034-platform-and-operations","sec":"kubernetes-s035","q":"In Kubernetes, why does <code>kubectl drain</code> need <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code>, and what does the drain do to bare Pods?","a":"<p>A DaemonSet Pod is recreated on the node immediately by its controller, so draining it makes no sense and the drain refuses to continue unless you pass the flag. Bare Pods (not owned by a controller) are not recreated anywhere, so drain refuses unless you force it, and they are then lost. Drain cordons the node first, then evicts Pods through the Eviction API, which respects PodDisruptionBudgets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0312","card":"q-kubernetes-034-platform-and-operations","sec":"kubernetes-s035","q":"How does OpenShift differ from upstream Kubernetes?","a":"<p>OpenShift is Red Hat's Kubernetes distribution with extra built-in parts: a web console, built-in image registry and builds, Routes (an older Ingress-like object), integrated CI/CD and monitoring, the <code>oc</code> CLI, and stricter security defaults through Security Context Constraints (containers run as a random non-root user by default). The trade-offs are cost and some vendor-specific objects that make manifests less portable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0313","card":"q-kubernetes-034-platform-and-operations","sec":"kubernetes-s035","q":"Which commands create a managed Kubernetes cluster on AWS, Google Cloud and Azure, and what do you gain over self-managing?","a":"<p>EKS: <code>eksctl create cluster</code>. GKE: <code>gcloud container clusters create</code>. AKS: <code>az aks create</code>. The provider runs and upgrades the control plane (API server, etcd, scheduler), and you manage the worker nodes or use node pools or autopilot modes. You give up control over the control plane configuration, and you cannot access etcd directly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0314","card":"q-kubernetes-034-platform-and-operations","sec":"kubernetes-s035","q":"What is KubeVirt and when would you use it?","a":"<p>KubeVirt is an add-on that runs virtual machines on Kubernetes. A VM is described by a custom resource, and each VM runs inside a Pod that hosts a QEMU/KVM process. Use it when you have workloads that cannot be containerized yet (legacy apps, other operating systems) and you want one platform, one network and one set of tooling for both VMs and containers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0315","card":"q-kubernetes-034-platform-and-operations","sec":"kubernetes-s035","q":"What do you back up for disaster recovery of a Kubernetes cluster, and how?","a":"<p>Back up the cluster state (etcd snapshots with <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code>, or Velero for objects), the persistent data (volume snapshots, application-level backups), and keep manifests in Git so everything can be recreated. Store backups outside the cluster and region, and test restores regularly. RPO is set by the backup frequency and RTO by how fast you can rebuild. On managed clusters you cannot snapshot etcd, so use Velero plus GitOps.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0316","card":"q-kubernetes-034-platform-and-operations","sec":"kubernetes-s035","q":"In Kubernetes disaster recovery, why is a GitOps repository not a full backup on its own?","a":"<p>Git holds the desired configuration, not runtime state. It does not hold the data in persistent volumes, Secrets created by hand or by operators, custom resources written by controllers, or certificates issued at runtime. So combine Git for configuration with volume snapshots or Velero for data and state created inside the cluster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0317","card":"q-kubernetes-035-pods-services-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s036","q":"A Pod is stuck in Pending. How do you troubleshoot it?","a":"<p>Check resources, taints, and PVCs via <code>kubectl describe</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0318","card":"q-kubernetes-035-pods-services-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s036","q":"A service is not accessible externally. What steps would you take?","a":"<p>Verify the service type, endpoints, Pods, and network rules.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0319","card":"q-kubernetes-035-pods-services-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s036","q":"You notice high CPU usage in your cluster. How do you identify the cause?","a":"<p>Use <code>kubectl top</code>, logs, and Prometheus to find the culprits.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0320","card":"q-kubernetes-035-pods-services-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s036","q":"How would you handle a node failure?","a":"<p>Drain the node, replace it, reschedule Pods, and scale if needed.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0321","card":"q-kubernetes-035-pods-services-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s036","q":"A StatefulSet is not creating Pods as expected. What could be the issue?","a":"<p>Check PVC binding, resource limits, or a headless Service misconfiguration.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0322","card":"q-kubernetes-035-pods-services-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s036","q":"A Pod is Pending and <code>kubectl describe pod</code> shows \"0/5 nodes are available: 5 Insufficient memory\". What does this mean and what are your options?","a":"<p>The scheduler found no node whose free allocatable memory (allocatable minus the sum of requests of Pods already there) can fit this Pod's memory request. It compares requests, not real usage. Options: lower the request if it is inflated, free capacity by removing or shrinking other workloads, add nodes (the Cluster Autoscaler does this if enabled), or use larger nodes if one Pod is bigger than any node.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0323","card":"q-kubernetes-035-pods-services-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s036","q":"A Pod is Pending with the event \"had untolerated taint\". What does this mean and what do you do?","a":"<p>Every node that could host the Pod has a taint that the Pod does not tolerate (for example a control plane taint, or a dedicated-node taint). Either add a matching toleration to the Pod, remove the taint if it is not wanted (<code>kubectl taint nodes &lt;node&gt; key=value:NoSchedule-</code>), or add nodes without that taint.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0324","card":"q-kubernetes-035-pods-services-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s036","q":"A Pod is Pending with no events from the scheduler at all. What could be wrong?","a":"<p>The scheduler is not seeing or handling the Pod. Check that the kube-scheduler Pods are running, and whether the Pod sets a <code>schedulerName</code> that no scheduler serves. Also check the Pod has not been bound already to a node that is down, and look at the scheduler logs in <code>kube-system</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0325","card":"q-kubernetes-035-pods-services-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s036","q":"A <code>type: LoadBalancer</code> Service shows <code>&lt;pending&gt;</code> under EXTERNAL-IP. What do you check?","a":"<p>Something must provision the load balancer. On a cloud cluster check that the cloud-controller-manager is running with correct permissions, the cloud account quotas, and the Service events (<code>kubectl describe svc</code>). On bare metal or local clusters nothing provisions it by default, so install MetalLB or use a NodePort or Ingress instead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0326","card":"q-kubernetes-035-pods-services-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s036","q":"A Service is reachable inside the cluster but not from outside. What is the order of checks?","a":"<p>Check the Service type (ClusterIP is internal only). For NodePort, check the node port (range 30000-32767 by default) and that the node firewall or cloud security group allows it. For LoadBalancer, check the external address, the cloud load balancer health checks and security groups. For Ingress, check the controller and the Ingress rules and DNS. Also confirm the Service has endpoints.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0327","card":"q-kubernetes-035-pods-services-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s036","q":"Your cluster shows high CPU. How do you find which workload and node are responsible?","a":"<p>Run <code>kubectl top nodes</code> to find the node, then <code>kubectl top pods -A --sort-by=cpu</code> to find the Pods. Both need metrics-server. Then look at the Pod's logs and its requests and limits (a missing CPU limit lets one Pod take a whole node), and use Prometheus and Grafana for history and per-container detail. Decide if it is real load (scale out with HPA) or a bug such as a busy loop.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0328","card":"q-kubernetes-035-pods-services-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s036","q":"A worker node loses power. What does Kubernetes do with the Pods on it, and how long does it take?","a":"<p>The kubelet stops sending heartbeats, so the node controller marks the node <code>NotReady</code>/<code>Unknown</code> after the grace period (40 seconds by default) and adds the <code>node.kubernetes.io/unreachable</code> taint. Pods tolerate that taint for 300 seconds by default, then they are evicted and their controllers (ReplicaSet, etc.) create replacements on other nodes. So expect several minutes of reduced capacity unless you lower <code>tolerationSeconds</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0329","card":"q-kubernetes-035-pods-services-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s036","q":"After a node fails, why do StatefulSet Pods on it often stay in Terminating and not move to another node?","a":"<p>A StatefulSet guarantees at most one Pod with a given identity (for example <code>db-0</code>). The control plane cannot confirm the Pod is really stopped on the unreachable node, and starting a second <code>db-0</code> could corrupt data. So it waits until the node returns or is confirmed gone: delete the node object, or apply the out-of-service taint (<code>node.kubernetes.io/out-of-service</code>, stable in 1.28 and later) once you are sure the node is down.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0330","card":"q-kubernetes-035-pods-services-and-nodes","sec":"kubernetes-s036","q":"A StatefulSet creates <code>web-0</code> but never creates <code>web-1</code>. What is the likely cause?","a":"<p>By default a StatefulSet uses <code>podManagementPolicy: OrderedReady</code>: it creates Pods one at a time and waits until the previous Pod is Running and Ready. If <code>web-0</code> is not Ready (failing readiness probe, unbound PVC, crash), <code>web-1</code> is not started. Fix <code>web-0</code>. If the app does not need ordering, use <code>podManagementPolicy: Parallel</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0331","card":"q-kubernetes-036-releases-migration-and-recovery","sec":"kubernetes-s037","q":"A deployment fails after an update. How do you roll it back?","a":"<p><code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/&lt;name&gt;</code></p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0332","card":"q-kubernetes-036-releases-migration-and-recovery","sec":"kubernetes-s037","q":"How do you migrate an application from a single server to Kubernetes?","a":"<p>Containerize it, define a Deployment and Service, move data, and cut over.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0333","card":"q-kubernetes-036-releases-migration-and-recovery","sec":"kubernetes-s037","q":"A Secret is accidentally exposed. What do you do?","a":"<p>Rotate the Secret, delete the old one, audit access, and encrypt etcd.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0334","card":"q-kubernetes-036-releases-migration-and-recovery","sec":"kubernetes-s037","q":"How do you recover a cluster if the etcd database is corrupted?","a":"<p>Restore from backup, restart the control plane, and verify.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0335","card":"q-kubernetes-036-releases-migration-and-recovery","sec":"kubernetes-s037","q":"What does <code>spec.revisionHistoryLimit</code> control in a Kubernetes Deployment, and what happens if you set it to 0?","a":"<p>It sets how many old ReplicaSets the Deployment keeps for rollback (default 10). Setting it to 0 removes the history, so <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> has nothing to go back to. Keep a sensible number for rollbacks, and lower it only to reduce clutter in the cluster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0336","card":"q-kubernetes-036-releases-migration-and-recovery","sec":"kubernetes-s037","q":"You roll back a Deployment, but the application still fails. What might a rollback not have reverted?","a":"<p>A rollback reverts only the Pod template of the Deployment (image, env, probes, resources). It does not revert a ConfigMap or Secret that changed, database schema migrations, data written by the new version, or other objects such as Services and Ingress. Version these changes together, make migrations backward compatible, and use a name or hash per config version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0337","card":"q-kubernetes-036-releases-migration-and-recovery","sec":"kubernetes-s037","q":"How would you migrate an application from a single server to Kubernetes with little downtime?","a":"<p>Containerize the app, move its configuration into environment variables, ConfigMaps and Secrets, and make it stateless or move state into a database or volume. Add health endpoints for probes and write the Deployment, Service and Ingress. Run it in parallel with the old server, move data, then shift traffic gradually (weighted DNS or load balancer) and keep the old server for a quick rollback. Add logging, metrics and resource requests before cutover.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0338","card":"q-kubernetes-036-releases-migration-and-recovery","sec":"kubernetes-s037","q":"A Secret value was committed to a public Git repository. What do you do?","a":"<p>Treat it as compromised. Revoke and rotate the credential at its source first (the database, the API provider). Then update the Kubernetes Secret and restart workloads that use it. Remove it from Git history and audit access logs for use of the old value. Deleting only the Kubernetes Secret does not help because the exposed value still works. Afterward enable etcd encryption at rest, restrict RBAC on Secrets and use sealed or external secrets so values never reach Git.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0339","card":"q-kubernetes-036-releases-migration-and-recovery","sec":"kubernetes-s037","q":"How do you recover a Kubernetes control plane when etcd is corrupted?","a":"<p>Stop the API server, restore the latest good snapshot with <code>etcdctl snapshot restore</code> into a new data directory, point etcd at it, and restart the control plane. In a multi-member cluster restore all members from the same snapshot and re-form the cluster. Then verify nodes, workloads and certificates. Changes made after the snapshot are lost, so snapshot frequency sets your RPO. Running workloads usually keep running on the nodes during this, but cannot be changed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0340","card":"q-kubernetes-037-exposing-a-deployment","sec":"kubernetes-s038","q":"Write a YAML file to create a Deployment with 3 replicas.","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: nginx-deployment\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: nginx\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: nginx\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: nginx\n        image: nginx:latest\n        ports:\n        - containerPort: 80</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0341","card":"q-kubernetes-037-exposing-a-deployment","sec":"kubernetes-s038","q":"How do you expose a Deployment as a LoadBalancer service?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: nginx-service\nspec:\n  selector:\n    app: nginx\n  ports:\n  - port: 80\n    targetPort: 80\n  type: LoadBalancer</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0342","card":"q-kubernetes-037-exposing-a-deployment","sec":"kubernetes-s038","q":"In a Kubernetes <code>apps/v1</code> Deployment, what must match for the object to be accepted?","a":"<p><code>spec.selector.matchLabels</code> must match the labels in <code>spec.template.metadata.labels</code>. The API server rejects the Deployment if they differ. The selector is how the Deployment and its ReplicaSet find the Pods they own. It is also immutable after creation in <code>apps/v1</code>, so choose it carefully.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>spec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: nginx\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: nginx</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0343","card":"q-kubernetes-037-exposing-a-deployment","sec":"kubernetes-s038","q":"In a Kubernetes container spec, what does <code>ports: - containerPort: 80</code> do?","a":"<p>It documents the port the container listens on. It does not open, publish or restrict anything: traffic to other ports in the Pod still works. Tools and named ports use it (a Service <code>targetPort</code> can refer to a port by name). The Service <code>targetPort</code> is what actually decides where traffic is sent.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>containers:\n- name: nginx\n  image: nginx:latest\n  ports:\n  - containerPort: 80</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0344","card":"q-kubernetes-037-exposing-a-deployment","sec":"kubernetes-s038","q":"In a Kubernetes Service of <code>type: LoadBalancer</code>, which other objects or resources are created behind it?","a":"<p>A LoadBalancer Service is a superset of the other types. It also gets a ClusterIP and, by default, a node port on every node. The cloud-controller-manager provisions an external load balancer that forwards to those node ports (or directly to Pods on some clouds). Provisioning a cloud load balancer usually costs money per Service, so for many HTTP apps put one Ingress behind a single load balancer.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>spec:\n  selector:\n    app: nginx\n  ports:\n  - port: 80\n    targetPort: 80\n  type: LoadBalancer</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0345","card":"q-kubernetes-037-exposing-a-deployment","sec":"kubernetes-s038","q":"In Kubernetes, why does a Service selector select Pods and not a Deployment?","a":"<p>A Service is a stable virtual IP with a list of endpoints. Its selector is matched against Pod labels, and the endpoint controller adds the IPs of matching Ready Pods. The Service does not know about Deployments, so Pods from several Deployments, or from a bare Pod, can be behind one Service if their labels match. This is what makes blue-green switching by changing a label possible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0346","card":"q-kubernetes-038-services-and-configmaps","sec":"kubernetes-s039","q":"How do you create a Service to expose a Deployment?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: my-service\nspec:\n  selector:\n    app: nginx\n  ports:\n  - protocol: TCP\n    port: 80\n    targetPort: 80\n  type: ClusterIP</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0347","card":"q-kubernetes-038-services-and-configmaps","sec":"kubernetes-s039","q":"How do you create a Service and a Pod it selects?","a":"<p>The Pod label must match the Service selector.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: my-service\nspec:\n  selector:\n    app: my-app\n  ports:\n  - protocol: TCP\n    port: 80\n    targetPort: 80\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: my-pod\n  labels:\n    app: my-app\nspec:\n  containers:\n  - name: nginx\n    image: nginx</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0348","card":"q-kubernetes-038-services-and-configmaps","sec":"kubernetes-s039","q":"How do you create a ConfigMap?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: ConfigMap\nmetadata:\n  name: my-configmap\ndata:\n  key1: value1\n  key2: value2</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0349","card":"q-kubernetes-038-services-and-configmaps","sec":"kubernetes-s039","q":"How do you attach a ConfigMap to a Pod as an environment variable?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: my-pod-with-configmap\nspec:\n  containers:\n  - name: nginx\n    image: nginx\n    env:\n    - name: KEY1\n      valueFrom:\n        configMapKeyRef:\n          name: my-configmap\n          key: key1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0350","card":"q-kubernetes-038-services-and-configmaps","sec":"kubernetes-s039","q":"In a Kubernetes Service manifest, which type do you get if you leave out <code>type</code>, and which protocols can <code>protocol</code> take?","a":"<p>The default type is <code>ClusterIP</code>, an internal-only virtual IP. <code>protocol</code> defaults to TCP and can also be UDP or SCTP. A Service is not reachable from outside the cluster unless you change the type or put an Ingress in front.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>spec:\n  selector:\n    app: nginx\n  ports:\n  - protocol: TCP\n    port: 80\n    targetPort: 80</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0351","card":"q-kubernetes-038-services-and-configmaps","sec":"kubernetes-s039","q":"In a Kubernetes ConfigMap, what is the difference between <code>data</code> and <code>binaryData</code>, and what is a common mistake with values?","a":"<p><code>data</code> holds UTF-8 strings. <code>binaryData</code> holds base64-encoded binary content. Every value in <code>data</code> must be a string, so write <code>\"true\"</code> and <code>\"8080\"</code> in quotes. An unquoted <code>true</code> or number makes the API server reject the object, or YAML converts it in a way you did not intend.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: ConfigMap\nmetadata:\n  name: my-configmap\ndata:\n  key1: value1\n  key2: value2</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0352","card":"q-kubernetes-038-services-and-configmaps","sec":"kubernetes-s039","q":"In a Pod spec, what do the fields of <code>configMapKeyRef</code> do, and what happens if the key does not exist?","a":"<p><code>name</code> is the ConfigMap, <code>key</code> is the key inside it, and the value becomes the environment variable <code>KEY1</code> (the variable name need not equal the key name). If the ConfigMap or key is missing, the container fails to start with <code>CreateContainerConfigError</code>. Add <code>optional: true</code> to start without it. The value is read once at container start, so a later ConfigMap change does not reach it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>env:\n- name: KEY1\n  valueFrom:\n    configMapKeyRef:\n      name: my-configmap\n      key: key1</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0353","card":"q-kubernetes-038-services-and-configmaps","sec":"kubernetes-s039","q":"Why do many teams keep a ConfigMap and the Deployment that uses it in the same manifest or Helm release?","a":"<p>The Deployment only picks up changed config when its Pods restart. Deploying both together, and giving the Pod template an annotation such as a hash of the config, makes the change roll out in one controlled rolling update that can be rolled back together. Separate lifecycles lead to Pods running with stale config.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0354","card":"q-kubernetes-039-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s040","q":"How do you create a Secret?","a":"<p>Values under <code>data</code> are base64-encoded.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Secret\nmetadata:\n  name: my-secret\ntype: Opaque\ndata:\n  username: YWRtaW4=   # base64 for &quot;admin&quot;\n  password: cGFzc3dvcmQ=   # base64 for &quot;password&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0355","card":"q-kubernetes-039-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s040","q":"How do you attach a Secret to a Pod?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: my-pod-with-secret\nspec:\n  containers:\n  - name: nginx\n    image: nginx\n    env:\n    - name: USERNAME\n      valueFrom:\n        secretKeyRef:\n          name: my-secret\n          key: username</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0356","card":"q-kubernetes-039-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s040","q":"How do you create a TLS Secret?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl create secret tls tls-secret --key tls.key --cert tls.crt</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0357","card":"q-kubernetes-039-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s040","q":"In a Kubernetes Secret, what is the difference between <code>data</code> and <code>stringData</code>?","a":"<p>Values under <code>data</code> must be base64-encoded. <code>stringData</code> takes plain text, and the API server encodes it into <code>data</code> on write (it is write-only, you never read it back). Use <code>stringData</code> when writing manifests by hand so you do not make encoding mistakes. Both are stored identically.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Secret\nmetadata:\n  name: my-secret\ntype: Opaque\ndata:\n  username: YWRtaW4=</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0358","card":"q-kubernetes-039-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s040","q":"You create a Secret value with <code>echo \"admin\" | base64</code> and the login fails. What is wrong?","a":"<p><code>echo</code> adds a trailing newline, so you encoded <code>admin\\n</code>. Use <code>echo -n \"admin\" | base64</code>, or <code>printf</code>. Alternatively use <code>kubectl create secret generic</code> with <code>--from-literal</code>, which encodes it correctly, or <code>stringData</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0359","card":"q-kubernetes-039-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s040","q":"What Secret types does Kubernetes have besides <code>Opaque</code>?","a":"<p><code>kubernetes.io/tls</code> (keys <code>tls.crt</code> and <code>tls.key</code>), <code>kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson</code> (registry credentials for <code>imagePullSecrets</code>), <code>kubernetes.io/basic-auth</code>, <code>kubernetes.io/ssh-auth</code>, <code>kubernetes.io/service-account-token</code>, and <code>bootstrap.kubernetes.io/token</code>. A type makes the API server validate that the required keys exist. <code>Opaque</code> means no validation of the keys.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0360","card":"q-kubernetes-039-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s040","q":"How do you let a Pod pull an image from a private registry in Kubernetes?","a":"<p>Create a Secret of type <code>kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson</code> (<code>kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred --docker-server=... --docker-username=... --docker-password=...</code>) in the Pod's namespace, then reference it under <code>spec.imagePullSecrets</code> in the Pod, or attach it to the ServiceAccount so every Pod using that account gets it. Without it the Pod ends in <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>. On cloud clusters, node IAM roles often replace this.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0361","card":"q-kubernetes-039-secrets","sec":"kubernetes-s040","q":"What does this command store, and where is the result usually used?","a":"<p>It creates a Secret of type <code>kubernetes.io/tls</code> with the keys <code>tls.crt</code> and <code>tls.key</code>. It is mostly referenced by an Ingress under <code>spec.tls[].secretName</code>, so the ingress controller terminates HTTPS with that certificate. The Secret must be in the same namespace as the Ingress. cert-manager can create and renew it automatically.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl create secret tls tls-secret --key tls.key --cert tls.crt</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0362","card":"q-kubernetes-040-persistent-storage-manifests","sec":"kubernetes-s041","q":"How do you create a PersistentVolume (PV)?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: PersistentVolume\nmetadata:\n  name: my-pv\nspec:\n  capacity:\n    storage: 1Gi\n  accessModes:\n  - ReadWriteOnce\n  hostPath:\n    path: /mnt/data</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0363","card":"q-kubernetes-040-persistent-storage-manifests","sec":"kubernetes-s041","q":"How do you create a PVC and attach it to a Pod?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: PersistentVolumeClaim\nmetadata:\n  name: my-pvc\nspec:\n  accessModes:\n  - ReadWriteOnce\n  resources:\n    requests:\n      storage: 1Gi\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: my-pod-with-pvc\nspec:\n  containers:\n  - name: nginx\n    image: nginx\n    volumeMounts:\n    - mountPath: /data\n      name: my-volume\n  volumes:\n  - name: my-volume\n    persistentVolumeClaim:\n      claimName: my-pvc</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0364","card":"q-kubernetes-040-persistent-storage-manifests","sec":"kubernetes-s041","q":"How do you attach a PVC to a Deployment?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: my-deployment-with-pvc\nspec:\n  replicas: 1\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: nginx\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: nginx\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: nginx\n        image: nginx\n        volumeMounts:\n        - mountPath: /data\n          name: my-volume\n      volumes:\n      - name: my-volume\n        persistentVolumeClaim:\n          claimName: my-pvc</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0365","card":"q-kubernetes-040-persistent-storage-manifests","sec":"kubernetes-s041","q":"What is the YAML for a 10Gi PVC with a StorageClass?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: PersistentVolumeClaim\nmetadata:\n  name: my-pvc\nspec:\n  accessModes: [ReadWriteOnce]\n  resources:\n    requests:\n      storage: 10Gi\n  storageClassName: standard</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0366","card":"q-kubernetes-040-persistent-storage-manifests","sec":"kubernetes-s041","q":"In a Kubernetes PersistentVolume, why is <code>hostPath</code> not suitable for production?","a":"<p>The data lives on one node's disk. If the Pod is rescheduled to another node it sees different, empty data, and if the node dies the data is gone. It also exposes the node's filesystem to the Pod. It is fine for single-node test clusters. In production use a CSI-backed volume (cloud disk, network storage) or a local PV with node affinity.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>spec:\n  capacity:\n    storage: 1Gi\n  accessModes:\n  - ReadWriteOnce\n  hostPath:\n    path: /mnt/data</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0367","card":"q-kubernetes-040-persistent-storage-manifests","sec":"kubernetes-s041","q":"How does Kubernetes decide which PersistentVolume a PersistentVolumeClaim binds to?","a":"<p>The claim binds to an available PV that matches its StorageClass, has all the requested access modes, and has capacity at least as large as the request, and matches any selector or <code>volumeName</code>. The smallest matching PV is preferred. A binding is one-to-one: the whole PV goes to one claim, even if the claim asks for less. With dynamic provisioning a new PV is created to match the claim.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0368","card":"q-kubernetes-040-persistent-storage-manifests","sec":"kubernetes-s041","q":"A PVC does not set <code>storageClassName</code>. What happens, and how do you ask for no dynamic provisioning?","a":"<p>If the cluster has a default StorageClass, the claim uses it and a volume is provisioned dynamically. Without a default, the claim stays Pending unless a PV with no class matches. Setting <code>storageClassName: \"\"</code> explicitly disables dynamic provisioning and binds only to PVs that have no class.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0369","card":"q-kubernetes-040-persistent-storage-manifests","sec":"kubernetes-s041","q":"A single-replica Deployment uses a ReadWriteOnce PVC. A rolling update leaves the new Pod stuck. Why, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>A rolling update starts the new Pod before stopping the old one. If the new Pod lands on a different node, it cannot attach the RWO volume that is still in use by the old Pod, so it waits. Use <code>strategy: type: Recreate</code> for such Deployments (accepting a short downtime), or use a StatefulSet, or a ReadWriteMany volume if the app supports concurrent access.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0370","card":"q-kubernetes-040-persistent-storage-manifests","sec":"kubernetes-s041","q":"In Kubernetes storage requests, what is the difference between <code>10Gi</code> and <code>10G</code>?","a":"<p><code>Gi</code> is a binary unit (gibibyte, 2^30 bytes). <code>G</code> is decimal (10^9 bytes). <code>10Gi</code> is about 7% larger than <code>10G</code>. The same applies to memory (<code>Mi</code> and <code>M</code>). Mixing them is a common cause of \"my quota is smaller than expected\" confusion.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0371","card":"q-kubernetes-041-workload-controllers","sec":"kubernetes-s042","q":"How do you create a ReplicaSet with 2 replicas?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: ReplicaSet\nmetadata:\n  name: my-replicaset\nspec:\n  replicas: 2\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: nginx\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: nginx\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: nginx\n        image: nginx</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0372","card":"q-kubernetes-041-workload-controllers","sec":"kubernetes-s042","q":"How do you create a StatefulSet with 2 replicas?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: StatefulSet\nmetadata:\n  name: my-statefulset\nspec:\n  serviceName: &quot;nginx&quot;\n  replicas: 2\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: nginx\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: nginx\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: nginx\n        image: nginx</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0373","card":"q-kubernetes-041-workload-controllers","sec":"kubernetes-s042","q":"How do you create a DaemonSet?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: DaemonSet\nmetadata:\n  name: my-daemonset\nspec:\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: fluentd\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: fluentd\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: fluentd\n        image: fluentd</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0374","card":"q-kubernetes-041-workload-controllers","sec":"kubernetes-s042","q":"In Kubernetes, why do you almost never create a ReplicaSet directly?","a":"<p>A ReplicaSet only keeps N identical Pods running. It has no rolling update or rollback. A Deployment creates and manages ReplicaSets for you and adds those features. Create a ReplicaSet directly only if you need custom update orchestration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0375","card":"q-kubernetes-041-workload-controllers","sec":"kubernetes-s042","q":"You change the image in the Pod template of a bare ReplicaSet. What happens to the running Pods?","a":"<p>Nothing. A ReplicaSet only compares the number of matching Pods with <code>replicas</code>. Existing Pods keep the old image, and only Pods created later use the new template. You must delete the old Pods yourself. This is why Deployments exist: they create a new ReplicaSet and shift Pods gradually.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0376","card":"q-kubernetes-041-workload-controllers","sec":"kubernetes-s042","q":"In this Kubernetes StatefulSet, what does <code>serviceName</code> refer to and what names do the Pods get?","a":"<p><code>serviceName</code> names a headless Service (<code>clusterIP: None</code>) that must exist. It gives each Pod a stable DNS name: <code>my-statefulset-0.nginx.&lt;namespace&gt;.svc.cluster.local</code> and <code>my-statefulset-1.nginx...</code>. The Pod names are stable ordinals, not random suffixes. The StatefulSet does not create the Service for you.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: StatefulSet\nmetadata:\n  name: my-statefulset\nspec:\n  serviceName: &quot;nginx&quot;\n  replicas: 2</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0377","card":"q-kubernetes-041-workload-controllers","sec":"kubernetes-s042","q":"In Kubernetes, how do you give each StatefulSet replica its own persistent disk?","a":"<p>Use <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> in the StatefulSet spec, as in the skeleton. The controller creates one PVC per Pod (<code>data-&lt;pod name&gt;</code>), and the same PVC is re-attached when that Pod is rescheduled. A shared <code>persistentVolumeClaim</code> volume in the Pod template would give all replicas the same disk.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>spec:\n  volumeClaimTemplates:\n  - metadata:\n      name: data\n    spec:\n      accessModes: [&quot;ReadWriteOnce&quot;]\n      resources:\n        requests:\n          storage: 10Gi</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0378","card":"q-kubernetes-041-workload-controllers","sec":"kubernetes-s042","q":"Why does a Kubernetes DaemonSet manifest have no <code>replicas</code> field?","a":"<p>The number of Pods is decided by the number of matching nodes: one per node (or per node that matches a <code>nodeSelector</code> or affinity). When a node joins, a Pod is created; when it leaves, the Pod is removed. Tolerations are needed to run on tainted nodes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0379","card":"q-kubernetes-041-workload-controllers","sec":"kubernetes-s042","q":"How do you do a staged (canary-like) rollout of a StatefulSet update?","a":"<p>Set <code>updateStrategy.rollingUpdate.partition: N</code>. Only Pods with an ordinal greater than or equal to N get the new revision. Lower the partition step by step, checking health as you go. Pods update in reverse ordinal order, highest first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0380","card":"q-kubernetes-042-jobs-and-cronjobs","sec":"kubernetes-s043","q":"How do you create a Job?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: batch/v1\nkind: Job\nmetadata:\n  name: my-job\nspec:\n  template:\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: pi\n        image: perl\n        command: [&quot;perl&quot;, &quot;-Mbignum=bpi&quot;, &quot;-wle&quot;, &quot;print bpi(2000)&quot;]\n      restartPolicy: Never</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0381","card":"q-kubernetes-042-jobs-and-cronjobs","sec":"kubernetes-s043","q":"How do you create a CronJob that echoes \"Hello\" every 5 minutes?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: batch/v1\nkind: CronJob\nmetadata:\n  name: hello-cronjob\nspec:\n  schedule: &quot;*/5 * * * *&quot;\n  jobTemplate:\n    spec:\n      template:\n        spec:\n          containers:\n          - name: hello\n            image: alpine:latest\n            command: [&quot;echo&quot;, &quot;Hello&quot;]\n          restartPolicy: OnFailure</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0382","card":"q-kubernetes-042-jobs-and-cronjobs","sec":"kubernetes-s043","q":"In a Kubernetes Job, why must <code>restartPolicy</code> be <code>Never</code> or <code>OnFailure</code>, and how do they differ?","a":"<p>A Job must finish, so <code>Always</code> is rejected. With <code>OnFailure</code>, the kubelet restarts the failing container inside the same Pod. With <code>Never</code>, a failed Pod stays for inspection and the Job controller creates a new Pod for the retry. Both are limited by <code>backoffLimit</code> (default 6). <code>Never</code> leaves more Pods and logs behind to debug.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>spec:\n  template:\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: pi\n        image: perl\n      restartPolicy: Never</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0383","card":"q-kubernetes-042-jobs-and-cronjobs","sec":"kubernetes-s043","q":"How do you run a Kubernetes Job that processes 10 work items, three at a time?","a":"<p>Set <code>completions: 10</code> and <code>parallelism: 3</code>. The Job keeps three Pods running until ten have succeeded. For work queues, omit <code>completions</code> and let Pods pull from the queue (<code>parallelism</code> only). Use <code>completionMode: Indexed</code> if each Pod needs its own index.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0384","card":"q-kubernetes-042-jobs-and-cronjobs","sec":"kubernetes-s043","q":"What do <code>backoffLimit</code>, <code>activeDeadlineSeconds</code> and <code>ttlSecondsAfterFinished</code> do in a Kubernetes Job?","a":"<p><code>backoffLimit</code> is how many failed retries are allowed before the Job is marked failed (default 6, with exponential delay). <code>activeDeadlineSeconds</code> is the maximum total run time, after which the Job is terminated. <code>ttlSecondsAfterFinished</code> deletes the finished Job and its Pods automatically after that many seconds, so completed Jobs do not pile up.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0385","card":"q-kubernetes-042-jobs-and-cronjobs","sec":"kubernetes-s043","q":"In a Kubernetes container spec, how do <code>command</code> and <code>args</code> relate to a Docker image's ENTRYPOINT and CMD?","a":"<p><code>command</code> replaces the image's ENTRYPOINT and <code>args</code> replaces its CMD. Here <code>echo Hello</code> runs instead of whatever the image defines. If you only set <code>args</code>, the image's ENTRYPOINT still runs with your arguments. There is no shell, so pipes and variable expansion need <code>[\"sh\", \"-c\", \"...\"]</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>containers:\n- name: hello\n  image: alpine:latest\n  command: [&quot;echo&quot;, &quot;Hello&quot;]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0386","card":"q-kubernetes-042-jobs-and-cronjobs","sec":"kubernetes-s043","q":"In a Kubernetes CronJob, how do you read the schedule <code>\"*/5 * * * *\"</code>, and in which time zone does it run?","a":"<p>Fields are minute, hour, day of month, month, day of week. <code>*/5</code> in the minute field means every 5 minutes. By default the schedule is interpreted in the time zone of the kube-controller-manager (usually UTC). In Kubernetes 1.27 and later you can set <code>spec.timeZone</code> (for example <code>Europe/Berlin</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0387","card":"q-kubernetes-042-jobs-and-cronjobs","sec":"kubernetes-s043","q":"What do <code>startingDeadlineSeconds</code> and the history limits do in a Kubernetes CronJob?","a":"<p><code>startingDeadlineSeconds</code> is how late a run may start after its scheduled time (for example because the controller was down). If the window is missed the run is skipped. <code>successfulJobsHistoryLimit</code> (default 3) and <code>failedJobsHistoryLimit</code> (default 1) say how many finished Jobs are kept for inspection.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0388","card":"q-kubernetes-042-jobs-and-cronjobs","sec":"kubernetes-s043","q":"Why must the work done by a Kubernetes CronJob be idempotent?","a":"<p>Kubernetes only guarantees roughly at-least-once scheduling. A run can occasionally be started twice, or overlap if <code>concurrencyPolicy</code> is <code>Allow</code>, or be retried after a failure. If the job sends emails or charges customers, duplicates are harmful. Make the work safe to repeat (use unique keys, check state before acting) and set <code>concurrencyPolicy: Forbid</code> when runs must not overlap.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0389","card":"q-kubernetes-043-ingress","sec":"kubernetes-s044","q":"How do you create an Ingress resource?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Ingress\nmetadata:\n  name: my-ingress\nspec:\n  rules:\n  - host: example.com\n    http:\n      paths:\n      - path: /\n        pathType: Prefix\n        backend:\n          service:\n            name: my-service\n            port:\n              number: 80</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0390","card":"q-kubernetes-043-ingress","sec":"kubernetes-s044","q":"How do you route <code>/api</code> to a service with an Ingress?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Ingress\nmetadata:\n  name: api-ingress\nspec:\n  rules:\n  - http:\n      paths:\n      - path: /api\n        pathType: Prefix\n        backend:\n          service:\n            name: api-service\n            port:\n              number: 8080</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0391","card":"q-kubernetes-043-ingress","sec":"kubernetes-s044","q":"How do you configure an Ingress with TLS?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Ingress\nmetadata:\n  name: ingress-tls\nspec:\n  tls:\n  - hosts:\n    - example.com\n    secretName: tls-secret\n  rules:\n  - host: example.com\n    http:\n      paths:\n      - path: /\n        pathType: Prefix\n        backend:\n          service:\n            name: nginx-service\n            port:\n              number: 80</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0392","card":"q-kubernetes-043-ingress","sec":"kubernetes-s044","q":"In a Kubernetes Ingress rule, what are the <code>pathType</code> values and how do they differ?","a":"<p><code>Exact</code> matches only the exact path. <code>Prefix</code> matches by path segments, so <code>/api</code> matches <code>/api</code> and <code>/api/users</code> but not <code>/apiv2</code>. <code>ImplementationSpecific</code> leaves the matching to the ingress controller. <code>pathType</code> is required in <code>networking.k8s.io/v1</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>paths:\n- path: /api\n  pathType: Prefix\n  backend:\n    service:\n      name: api-service\n      port:\n        number: 8080</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0393","card":"q-kubernetes-043-ingress","sec":"kubernetes-s044","q":"In Kubernetes, an Ingress sends <code>/api/users</code> to a backend service, but the backend expects <code>/users</code> and returns 404. What is the cause and the fix?","a":"<p>The ingress controller forwards the original path unchanged, including the <code>/api</code> prefix. Configure a rewrite: with ingress-nginx use a regex path and the <code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target</code> annotation, or make the backend serve under <code>/api</code>. Rewrite support is controller-specific, so annotations differ between controllers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0394","card":"q-kubernetes-043-ingress","sec":"kubernetes-s044","q":"How does an Ingress do TLS, and where must the certificate Secret live?","a":"<p>The ingress controller terminates TLS using the certificate and key in the Secret <code>tls-secret</code>, which must be type <code>kubernetes.io/tls</code> and in the same namespace as the Ingress. Traffic from the controller to the Pods is plain HTTP unless you configure backend HTTPS. cert-manager can issue and renew the certificate (for example from Let's Encrypt) and write the Secret.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>spec:\n  tls:\n  - hosts:\n    - example.com\n    secretName: tls-secret</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0395","card":"q-kubernetes-043-ingress","sec":"kubernetes-s044","q":"What is the role of <code>ingressClassName</code> in a Kubernetes Ingress, and what did it replace?","a":"<p>It selects which ingress controller handles the Ingress, by naming an <code>IngressClass</code>. This matters when several controllers (for example nginx and a cloud load balancer) run in one cluster. It replaced the <code>kubernetes.io/ingress.class</code> annotation, which has been deprecated since Kubernetes 1.18 and is not supported by every controller anymore.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0396","card":"q-kubernetes-043-ingress","sec":"kubernetes-s044","q":"How do you route by host name and by path in the same Kubernetes Ingress?","a":"<p>List several rules. Each rule can have its own <code>host</code> (host-based routing, for example <code>api.example.com</code> and <code>www.example.com</code>) and each host has a list of <code>paths</code> (path-based routing). A rule without a host matches every host. A <code>defaultBackend</code> handles requests that match no rule.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0397","card":"q-kubernetes-043-ingress","sec":"kubernetes-s044","q":"Which traffic can a Kubernetes Ingress handle, and what do you use for other protocols?","a":"<p>Ingress is for HTTP and HTTPS (layer 7) only. For raw TCP or UDP (databases, MQTT, game servers) use a Service of type LoadBalancer or NodePort, or a controller-specific TCP configuration. The Gateway API covers more protocols: GRPCRoute is GA since Gateway API v1.1, while TCPRoute and UDPRoute are still in the experimental channel.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0398","card":"q-kubernetes-044-namespaces-quotas-and-limits","sec":"kubernetes-s045","q":"How do you create a Namespace?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl create namespace my-namespace</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0399","card":"q-kubernetes-044-namespaces-quotas-and-limits","sec":"kubernetes-s045","q":"How do you create a <code>staging</code> Namespace with the label <code>env=staging</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Namespace\nmetadata:\n  name: staging\n  labels:\n    env: staging</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0400","card":"q-kubernetes-044-namespaces-quotas-and-limits","sec":"kubernetes-s045","q":"How do you create a ResourceQuota in a Namespace?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: ResourceQuota\nmetadata:\n  name: my-resourcequota\n  namespace: my-namespace\nspec:\n  hard:\n    pods: &quot;10&quot;\n    requests.cpu: &quot;2&quot;\n    requests.memory: 4Gi</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0401","card":"q-kubernetes-044-namespaces-quotas-and-limits","sec":"kubernetes-s045","q":"How do you create a LimitRange in a Namespace?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: LimitRange\nmetadata:\n  name: my-limitrange\n  namespace: my-namespace\nspec:\n  limits:\n  - type: Container\n    default:\n      cpu: &quot;500m&quot;\n      memory: &quot;512Mi&quot;\n    defaultRequest:\n      cpu: &quot;200m&quot;\n      memory: &quot;256Mi&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0402","card":"q-kubernetes-044-namespaces-quotas-and-limits","sec":"kubernetes-s045","q":"Which Kubernetes resources are not namespaced, and how do you list them?","a":"<p>Cluster-scoped resources include Nodes, PersistentVolumes, Namespaces, StorageClasses, ClusterRoles and ClusterRoleBindings, and CustomResourceDefinitions. List them with <code>kubectl api-resources --namespaced=false</code>. Namespaced ones (Pods, Services, Deployments, PVCs, ConfigMaps, Secrets, Roles) can have the same name in different namespaces.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0403","card":"q-kubernetes-044-namespaces-quotas-and-limits","sec":"kubernetes-s045","q":"What happens when you delete a Kubernetes Namespace, and why can it get stuck in Terminating?","a":"<p>Everything inside it is deleted: Pods, Services, Secrets, PVCs, and so on. That is why deleting a namespace in the wrong context is dangerous. It sticks in Terminating when some resource inside has a finalizer that never completes (for example a CRD whose controller is gone, or an API service that is unavailable). Find the remaining objects, fix the controller, or remove the finalizer as a last resort.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0404","card":"q-kubernetes-044-namespaces-quotas-and-limits","sec":"kubernetes-s045","q":"Why would you put a label on a Kubernetes Namespace?","a":"<p>Other objects select namespaces by label: a NetworkPolicy <code>namespaceSelector</code>, Pod Security Admission levels (<code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce</code>), and admission policies. Since Kubernetes 1.21 every namespace also gets the automatic label <code>kubernetes.io/metadata.name</code>, which is handy for selecting one namespace by name.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Namespace\nmetadata:\n  name: staging\n  labels:\n    env: staging</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0405","card":"q-kubernetes-044-namespaces-quotas-and-limits","sec":"kubernetes-s045","q":"In a Kubernetes namespace that has a ResourceQuota on <code>requests.cpu</code>, why are Pods without CPU requests rejected?","a":"<p>The quota can only add up requests that are declared. If a quota covers a resource, every new Pod must specify it, or admission fails with a \"must specify requests.cpu\" error. Fix it by setting requests on the containers, or add a LimitRange with defaults so containers get requests automatically.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>spec:\n  hard:\n    pods: &quot;10&quot;\n    requests.cpu: &quot;2&quot;\n    requests.memory: 4Gi</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0406","card":"q-kubernetes-044-namespaces-quotas-and-limits","sec":"kubernetes-s045","q":"What happens when you try to create the 11th Pod in a namespace with <code>pods: \"10\"</code> in its ResourceQuota?","a":"<p>The API server rejects the create request with a <code>Forbidden: exceeded quota</code> error. Existing Pods are not affected. For a Deployment, the ReplicaSet fails to create the extra Pods, and you see the error in the ReplicaSet events and not at <code>kubectl apply</code> time. Quotas are checked on creation only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0407","card":"q-kubernetes-044-namespaces-quotas-and-limits","sec":"kubernetes-s045","q":"In a Kubernetes LimitRange, what do <code>default</code> and <code>defaultRequest</code> set, and when do they apply?","a":"<p><code>default</code> is the limit applied to containers that declare none. <code>defaultRequest</code> is the request applied to containers that declare none. They are applied at admission to new Pods only, not to running Pods. A LimitRange can also set <code>min</code>, <code>max</code> and a limit-to-request ratio to reject unreasonable values.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>limits:\n- type: Container\n  default:\n    cpu: &quot;500m&quot;\n    memory: &quot;512Mi&quot;\n  defaultRequest:\n    cpu: &quot;200m&quot;\n    memory: &quot;256Mi&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0408","card":"q-kubernetes-045-autoscaling-and-network-policy","sec":"kubernetes-s046","q":"How do you create a HorizontalPodAutoscaler for a Deployment?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: autoscaling/v2\nkind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler\nmetadata:\n  name: my-hpa\nspec:\n  scaleTargetRef:\n    apiVersion: apps/v1\n    kind: Deployment\n    name: my-deployment\n  minReplicas: 1\n  maxReplicas: 5\n  metrics:\n  - type: Resource\n    resource:\n      name: cpu\n      target:\n        type: Utilization\n        averageUtilization: 50</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0409","card":"q-kubernetes-045-autoscaling-and-network-policy","sec":"kubernetes-s046","q":"How do you create a NetworkPolicy to restrict Pod traffic?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: my-network-policy\nspec:\n  podSelector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: nginx\n  policyTypes:\n  - Ingress\n  ingress:\n  - from:\n    - podSelector:\n        matchLabels:\n          app: frontend\n    ports:\n    - protocol: TCP\n      port: 80</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0410","card":"q-kubernetes-045-autoscaling-and-network-policy","sec":"kubernetes-s046","q":"How does the HPA compute the desired replica count, for example with 4 Pods at 90% CPU and a target of 50%?","a":"<p><code>desired = ceil(currentReplicas * currentMetric / targetMetric)</code>, so <code>ceil(4 * 90 / 50) = ceil(7.2) = 8</code>. Utilization is measured against the Pods' CPU requests. The result is clamped between <code>minReplicas</code> and <code>maxReplicas</code>, and the HPA skips changes smaller than a tolerance of about 10%.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>metrics:\n- type: Resource\n  resource:\n    name: cpu\n    target:\n      type: Utilization\n      averageUtilization: 50</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0411","card":"q-kubernetes-045-autoscaling-and-network-policy","sec":"kubernetes-s046","q":"What is the difference between <code>autoscaling/v1</code> and <code>autoscaling/v2</code> for the HorizontalPodAutoscaler?","a":"<p><code>autoscaling/v1</code> supports only CPU utilization. <code>autoscaling/v2</code> (stable since Kubernetes 1.23) supports memory, custom and external metrics, several metrics at once (the highest desired replica count wins), and a <code>behavior</code> section to control scale-up and scale-down speed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0412","card":"q-kubernetes-045-autoscaling-and-network-policy","sec":"kubernetes-s046","q":"Why does the HPA scale down slowly even after the load drops, and how do you tune it?","a":"<p>To avoid flapping, the HPA uses a scale-down stabilization window of 300 seconds by default: it picks the highest recommendation seen in that window. Tune it under <code>spec.behavior.scaleDown</code> with <code>stabilizationWindowSeconds</code> and <code>policies</code> (for example at most 10% of Pods per minute), and the same for <code>scaleUp</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0413","card":"q-kubernetes-045-autoscaling-and-network-policy","sec":"kubernetes-s046","q":"Why should you leave <code>replicas</code> out of a Deployment manifest that is managed by an HPA and applied from Git?","a":"<p>Each <code>kubectl apply</code> or GitOps sync would reset <code>spec.replicas</code> to the value in Git and fight the HPA, causing a sudden drop in Pods. If the field is absent, apply does not touch it and the HPA owns the replica count.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0414","card":"q-kubernetes-045-autoscaling-and-network-policy","sec":"kubernetes-s046","q":"In this NetworkPolicy, what happens to outgoing traffic from the selected Pods?","a":"<p>Nothing changes for egress. <code>policyTypes</code> lists only Ingress, so the policy isolates the selected Pods for incoming traffic only, and outgoing traffic stays unrestricted. To restrict outgoing traffic, add <code>Egress</code> to <code>policyTypes</code> and write egress rules.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>spec:\n  podSelector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: nginx\n  policyTypes:\n  - Ingress</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0415","card":"q-kubernetes-045-autoscaling-and-network-policy","sec":"kubernetes-s046","q":"In a NetworkPolicy <code>from</code> list, what is the difference between one list item with both a <code>namespaceSelector</code> and a <code>podSelector</code>, and two separate items?","a":"<p>Within one item the selectors are ANDed: Pods matching the podSelector in namespaces matching the namespaceSelector. As separate list items they are ORed: any Pod matching the podSelector (in the policy's own namespace), or any Pod in the matching namespaces. A misplaced dash is a common cause of a policy that is too open.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0416","card":"q-kubernetes-045-autoscaling-and-network-policy","sec":"kubernetes-s046","q":"If two Kubernetes NetworkPolicies select the same Pod, how are they combined?","a":"<p>They are additive. Traffic is allowed if any policy allows it. There are no deny rules and no priority order, so you cannot override one policy with another. Start from a default-deny policy and add allow policies.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0417","card":"q-kubernetes-046-rbac-and-serviceaccounts","sec":"kubernetes-s047","q":"How do you create a Role and attach it to a ServiceAccount?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Role\nmetadata:\n  name: my-role\n  namespace: default\nrules:\n- apiGroups: [&quot;&quot;]\n  resources: [&quot;pods&quot;]\n  verbs: [&quot;get&quot;, &quot;list&quot;]\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: ServiceAccount\nmetadata:\n  name: my-serviceaccount\n  namespace: default\n---\napiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\nkind: RoleBinding\nmetadata:\n  name: my-role-binding\n  namespace: default\nsubjects:\n- kind: ServiceAccount\n  name: my-serviceaccount\n  namespace: default\nroleRef:\n  kind: Role\n  name: my-role\n  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0418","card":"q-kubernetes-046-rbac-and-serviceaccounts","sec":"kubernetes-s047","q":"How do you create a ClusterRole and attach it to a ServiceAccount?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\nkind: ClusterRole\nmetadata:\n  name: my-clusterrole\nrules:\n- apiGroups: [&quot;&quot;]\n  resources: [&quot;pods&quot;]\n  verbs: [&quot;get&quot;, &quot;list&quot;]\n---\napiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\nkind: ClusterRoleBinding\nmetadata:\n  name: my-clusterrole-binding\nsubjects:\n- kind: ServiceAccount\n  name: my-serviceaccount\n  namespace: default\nroleRef:\n  kind: ClusterRole\n  name: my-clusterrole\n  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0419","card":"q-kubernetes-046-rbac-and-serviceaccounts","sec":"kubernetes-s047","q":"How do you create a ServiceAccount and attach it to a Pod?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: ServiceAccount\nmetadata:\n  name: my-serviceaccount\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: my-pod-with-sa\nspec:\n  serviceAccountName: my-serviceaccount\n  containers:\n  - name: nginx\n    image: nginx</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0420","card":"q-kubernetes-046-rbac-and-serviceaccounts","sec":"kubernetes-s047","q":"In Kubernetes RBAC, what is a Role without a RoleBinding worth?","a":"<p>Nothing. A Role only lists permissions. A RoleBinding (or ClusterRoleBinding) attaches that Role to subjects (users, groups, ServiceAccounts). Without a binding, nobody has those permissions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0421","card":"q-kubernetes-046-rbac-and-serviceaccounts","sec":"kubernetes-s047","q":"In Kubernetes RBAC, what happens when you bind a ClusterRole with a RoleBinding?","a":"<p>The permissions apply only inside the namespace of the RoleBinding. This lets you define a reusable role once (for example <code>view</code> or <code>edit</code>) and grant it per namespace, without giving cluster-wide access. A ClusterRoleBinding would grant it in all namespaces.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0422","card":"q-kubernetes-046-rbac-and-serviceaccounts","sec":"kubernetes-s047","q":"In Kubernetes RBAC, which fields of a RoleBinding cannot be changed after creation?","a":"<p><code>roleRef</code> is immutable. To point a binding at a different role you delete and recreate the binding. This is a safety feature, so someone who may only update the binding cannot swap it to a more powerful role.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>roleRef:\n  kind: Role\n  name: my-role\n  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0423","card":"q-kubernetes-046-rbac-and-serviceaccounts","sec":"kubernetes-s047","q":"In a RoleBinding, why does a ServiceAccount subject need a <code>namespace</code> but a User subject does not?","a":"<p>ServiceAccounts are namespaced objects, so the name alone is ambiguous across namespaces, and the binding may be in a different namespace than the account. Users and Groups come from outside the cluster (certificates, OIDC) and have no namespace.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>subjects:\n- kind: ServiceAccount\n  name: my-serviceaccount\n  namespace: default</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0424","card":"q-kubernetes-046-rbac-and-serviceaccounts","sec":"kubernetes-s047","q":"What does <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> do on a Kubernetes Pod, and when should you use it?","a":"<p>It stops Kubernetes from mounting the ServiceAccount token into the Pod (under <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount</code>). Use it for Pods that never call the Kubernetes API, so a compromised container cannot use the token. It can be set on the ServiceAccount or on the Pod.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0425","card":"q-kubernetes-046-rbac-and-serviceaccounts","sec":"kubernetes-s047","q":"In Kubernetes 1.24 and later, how are ServiceAccount tokens different from earlier versions?","a":"<p>Before 1.24 a long-lived token Secret was created automatically for every ServiceAccount. From 1.24 no such Secret is created. Pods get a short-lived, audience-bound token projected into the Pod and refreshed by the kubelet (the bound token feature, default since 1.22). If you need a token for external use, request one with <code>kubectl create token &lt;sa&gt;</code>, or create a token Secret explicitly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0426","card":"q-kubernetes-046-rbac-and-serviceaccounts","sec":"kubernetes-s047","q":"In a Kubernetes Role, how do you allow reading Pod logs, and how do you limit a rule to one named object?","a":"<p>Logs are a subresource, so use <code>resources: [\"pods/log\"]</code> with <code>verbs: [\"get\"]</code>. Permission on <code>pods</code> alone does not include <code>pods/log</code> or <code>pods/exec</code>. To restrict a rule to specific objects add <code>resourceNames: [\"my-config\"]</code>, which works for verbs such as get, update and delete. Only create and deletecollection cannot be limited by name. list and watch can be limited only when the request carries a <code>metadata.name</code> field selector.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0427","card":"q-kubernetes-046-rbac-and-serviceaccounts","sec":"kubernetes-s047","q":"In Kubernetes RBAC, why can't a user grant a Role that has more permissions than the user holds?","a":"<p>The API server blocks privilege escalation: you can create or bind a Role only if you already have all the permissions in it, unless you have the special <code>escalate</code> verb on roles or <code>bind</code> on the role. Without this rule, anyone who can create Roles could make themselves cluster admin.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0428","card":"q-kubernetes-047-listing-logs-and-exec","sec":"kubernetes-s048","q":"How do you list Pods in the <code>default</code> namespace that are Running?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl get pods -n default --field-selector=status.phase=Running</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0429","card":"q-kubernetes-047-listing-logs-and-exec","sec":"kubernetes-s048","q":"How do you check logs of one container in a multi-container Pod?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt; -c &lt;container-name&gt;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0430","card":"q-kubernetes-047-listing-logs-and-exec","sec":"kubernetes-s048","q":"How do you exec into <code>app-pod</code> to check processes?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl exec -it app-pod -n &lt;namespace&gt; -- /bin/sh -c &quot;ps aux&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0431","card":"q-kubernetes-047-listing-logs-and-exec","sec":"kubernetes-s048","q":"How do you check Pod events?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;pod-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0432","card":"q-kubernetes-047-listing-logs-and-exec","sec":"kubernetes-s048","q":"How do you check Pod CPU and memory usage?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl top pods -n &lt;namespace&gt;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0433","card":"q-kubernetes-047-listing-logs-and-exec","sec":"kubernetes-s048","q":"In <code>kubectl</code>, what is the difference between <code>--field-selector</code> and <code>-l</code> (label selector)?","a":"<p><code>-l</code> filters by labels you or your tools put on objects. <code>--field-selector</code> filters by built-in object fields, such as <code>status.phase</code>, <code>metadata.name</code> or <code>spec.nodeName</code>. Only a few fields are supported per resource type. Both are applied on the server, so they save bandwidth.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl get pods -n default --field-selector=status.phase=Running</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0434","card":"q-kubernetes-047-listing-logs-and-exec","sec":"kubernetes-s048","q":"In this <code>kubectl exec</code> command, why are there <code>--</code> and <code>sh -c</code>?","a":"<p><code>--</code> ends kubectl's own flags, so everything after it is the command to run in the container, and its flags are not parsed by kubectl. <code>sh -c \"...\"</code> runs the string through a shell, which you need for pipes, redirects and several commands. <code>-i</code> keeps stdin open and <code>-t</code> allocates a terminal. The container must contain the shell and tools you call.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl exec -it app-pod -n &lt;namespace&gt; -- /bin/sh -c &quot;ps aux&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0435","card":"q-kubernetes-047-listing-logs-and-exec","sec":"kubernetes-s048","q":"How do you stream the logs of all Pods behind a Kubernetes Deployment at the same time?","a":"<p>Select by label: <code>kubectl logs -l app=web -f --all-containers=true --prefix</code>. <code>--prefix</code> shows which Pod each line came from, and <code>--max-log-requests</code> raises the default limit of 5 concurrent streams. For serious use, ship logs to a central system (Loki, Elasticsearch, CloudWatch) because <code>kubectl logs</code> reads only what the node still holds.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0436","card":"q-kubernetes-047-listing-logs-and-exec","sec":"kubernetes-s048","q":"Why can you not read the logs of a Pod that was deleted, and what is the fix?","a":"<p><code>kubectl logs</code> reads the container log files on the node, and the kubelet removes them when the Pod is deleted or the node rotates logs. Run a log collector (a DaemonSet such as Fluent Bit or the Promtail agent) that ships logs to central storage, so they survive Pods and nodes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0437","card":"q-kubernetes-047-listing-logs-and-exec","sec":"kubernetes-s048","q":"How long does Kubernetes keep Events, and why does that matter when you debug?","a":"<p>Events are kept for one hour by default (the API server's <code>--event-ttl</code>). If a Pod failed yesterday, <code>kubectl describe</code> may show no events. Send events to your logging system or a tool such as an event exporter if you need history.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0438","card":"q-kubernetes-048-pending-imagepullbackoff-and-crashloopba","sec":"kubernetes-s049","q":"How do you troubleshoot a Pod stuck in Pending?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl get pod &lt;pod-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;\nkubectl describe pod &lt;pod-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;\nkubectl get nodes\nkubectl describe quota -n &lt;namespace&gt;\nkubectl logs -n kube-system -l component=kube-scheduler</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0439","card":"q-kubernetes-048-pending-imagepullbackoff-and-crashloopba","sec":"kubernetes-s049","q":"How do you debug ImagePullBackOff?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl get pod &lt;pod-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;\nkubectl describe pod &lt;pod-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;\ndocker pull &lt;image-name:tag&gt;\nkubectl get secret &lt;secret-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0440","card":"q-kubernetes-048-pending-imagepullbackoff-and-crashloopba","sec":"kubernetes-s049","q":"How do you delete Pods in CrashLoopBackOff?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl get pods -n &lt;namespace&gt; -o jsonpath=&#x27;{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{&quot;\\t&quot;}{.status.containerStatuses[0].state.waiting.reason}{&quot;\\n&quot;}{end}&#x27; | grep CrashLoopBackOff | awk &#x27;{print $1}&#x27; | xargs kubectl delete pod -n &lt;namespace&gt;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0441","card":"q-kubernetes-048-pending-imagepullbackoff-and-crashloopba","sec":"kubernetes-s049","q":"How do you force delete a Pod stuck in Terminating?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl delete pod &lt;pod-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt; --force --grace-period=0</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0442","card":"q-kubernetes-048-pending-imagepullbackoff-and-crashloopba","sec":"kubernetes-s049","q":"In Kubernetes, what is the difference between <code>ErrImagePull</code> and <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>?","a":"<p><code>ErrImagePull</code> is the status when the pull has just failed. After repeated failures the kubelet waits longer between attempts (exponential back-off, capped at 5 minutes), and the status shows <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>. Both mean the image cannot be pulled, so read the event message for the cause.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0443","card":"q-kubernetes-048-pending-imagepullbackoff-and-crashloopba","sec":"kubernetes-s049","q":"What are the common causes of ImagePullBackOff, and how do you tell them apart?","a":"<p>A typo in the image name or tag, a tag that does not exist, a private registry with missing or wrong <code>imagePullSecrets</code>, registry rate limits (Docker Hub anonymous pulls), or the node having no network path to the registry. <code>kubectl describe pod</code> events usually say \"not found\", \"unauthorized\" or \"toomanyrequests\", which tells you which one it is.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0444","card":"q-kubernetes-048-pending-imagepullbackoff-and-crashloopba","sec":"kubernetes-s049","q":"To debug ImagePullBackOff you try <code>docker pull &lt;image&gt;</code> on your laptop and it works. Why does that not prove the cluster can pull it?","a":"<p>The kubelet pulls from the node, using the node's network and the Pod's <code>imagePullSecrets</code>, not your credentials. Your laptop may be logged in or on a VPN. Also most nodes now use containerd or CRI-O, not Docker. To test on a node use <code>crictl pull &lt;image&gt;</code>. Check that the Secret exists in the Pod's namespace.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0445","card":"q-kubernetes-048-pending-imagepullbackoff-and-crashloopba","sec":"kubernetes-s049","q":"What does Kubernetes do when a container keeps crashing, and what does CrashLoopBackOff tell you?","a":"<p>The kubelet restarts it (according to <code>restartPolicy</code>) with an exponentially growing delay: 10s, 20s, 40s and so on, capped at 5 minutes. <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> means the container starts and exits repeatedly, so the bug is inside the container (bad config, missing dependency, crash, failing command). Look at <code>kubectl logs --previous</code> and the last state's exit code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0446","card":"q-kubernetes-048-pending-imagepullbackoff-and-crashloopba","sec":"kubernetes-s049","q":"A pipeline deletes every Pod that is in CrashLoopBackOff, as shown. Why is this a bad fix?","a":"<p>The controller immediately creates a replacement that crashes the same way, so the root cause stays. The only effect is resetting the back-off timer. It also looks only at container index 0, so crashes in other containers are missed, and it can delete Pods that are not owned by a controller permanently. Read the logs and events and fix the cause.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl get pods -n &lt;namespace&gt; -o jsonpath=&#x27;{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{&quot;\\t&quot;}{.status.containerStatuses[0].state.waiting.reason}{&quot;\\n&quot;}{end}&#x27; | grep CrashLoopBackOff | awk &#x27;{print $1}&#x27; | xargs kubectl delete pod -n &lt;namespace&gt;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0447","card":"q-kubernetes-048-pending-imagepullbackoff-and-crashloopba","sec":"kubernetes-s049","q":"A Pod shows <code>CreateContainerConfigError</code>. What does it mean?","a":"<p>The kubelet cannot build the container's configuration, most often because a referenced ConfigMap or Secret (or a key in it) does not exist, or a volume cannot be set up. Run <code>kubectl describe pod</code> to see which one, create the missing object or key, or mark the reference <code>optional: true</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0448","card":"q-kubernetes-048-pending-imagepullbackoff-and-crashloopba","sec":"kubernetes-s049","q":"Why is <code>kubectl delete pod &lt;pod&gt; --force --grace-period=0</code> dangerous, and why can a Pod be stuck in Terminating?","a":"<p>The force option removes the Pod from the API server at once, without waiting for the kubelet to confirm the containers stopped. The containers may still be running on the node. For a StatefulSet this can create two Pods with the same identity writing to the same data. A Pod is stuck in Terminating because the node is unreachable, a finalizer is set, a volume cannot unmount, or the process ignores SIGTERM. Fix the cause first and force only if you know the node is gone.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0449","card":"q-kubernetes-048-pending-imagepullbackoff-and-crashloopba","sec":"kubernetes-s049","q":"When troubleshooting a Pending Pod, what do <code>kubectl describe quota</code> and the kube-scheduler logs each tell you?","a":"<p><code>kubectl describe quota</code> shows whether a ResourceQuota is exhausted, which blocks Pod creation (the Pod would not even exist, or the ReplicaSet reports the error). The scheduler log shows decisions for Pods that exist, such as no fitting node. Pending Pods almost always show a <code>FailedScheduling</code> event in <code>describe</code> that already names the reason, so read events first, then logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0450","card":"q-kubernetes-049-scaling-rollouts-and-patching","sec":"kubernetes-s050","q":"How do you scale the deployment <code>web-app</code> to 5 replicas?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl scale deployment web-app --replicas=5 -n &lt;namespace&gt;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0451","card":"q-kubernetes-049-scaling-rollouts-and-patching","sec":"kubernetes-s050","q":"How do you roll back a deployment to the previous revision?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl rollout undo deployment &lt;deployment-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0452","card":"q-kubernetes-049-scaling-rollouts-and-patching","sec":"kubernetes-s050","q":"How do you investigate a deployment that is not updating?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl get deployment &lt;deployment-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;\nkubectl describe deployment &lt;deployment-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;\nkubectl get rs -n &lt;namespace&gt;\nkubectl rollout history deployment &lt;deployment-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;\nkubectl rollout restart deployment &lt;deployment-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0453","card":"q-kubernetes-049-scaling-rollouts-and-patching","sec":"kubernetes-s050","q":"How do you patch a deployment to add an environment variable?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl patch deployment &lt;deployment-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt; --type=&#x27;json&#x27; -p=&#x27;[{&quot;op&quot;: &quot;add&quot;, &quot;path&quot;: &quot;/spec/template/spec/containers/0/env/-&quot;, &quot;value&quot;: {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;NEW_VAR&quot;, &quot;value&quot;: &quot;new-value&quot;}}]&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0454","card":"q-kubernetes-049-scaling-rollouts-and-patching","sec":"kubernetes-s050","q":"How do you check ReplicaSet status?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl get rs &lt;replicaset-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;\nkubectl describe rs &lt;replicaset-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0455","card":"q-kubernetes-049-scaling-rollouts-and-patching","sec":"kubernetes-s050","q":"Why can <code>kubectl scale</code> be undone by a GitOps tool such as Argo CD, and how do you scale properly in that setup?","a":"<p><code>kubectl scale</code> changes the live object but not the manifest in Git. A GitOps controller sees the drift and sets the replicas back to the value in Git (with self-heal on). Change <code>replicas</code> in Git, or let an HPA own the count and leave <code>replicas</code> out of the manifest.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0456","card":"q-kubernetes-049-scaling-rollouts-and-patching","sec":"kubernetes-s050","q":"When is scaling a Deployment to 0 replicas useful?","a":"<p>To stop a workload without deleting its configuration: nightly shutdown of dev environments to save cost, pausing a consumer while you fix a downstream system, or draining an app before maintenance. The Deployment, Services and PVCs stay, and scaling back up restores it. Tools like KEDA can scale to 0 automatically when there is no work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0457","card":"q-kubernetes-049-scaling-rollouts-and-patching","sec":"kubernetes-s050","q":"What do <code>kubectl rollout pause</code> and <code>kubectl rollout resume</code> do, and when would you use them?","a":"<p><code>pause</code> stops the Deployment controller from rolling out changes, so you can edit the Deployment several times (image, resources, env) without triggering one rollout per edit. <code>resume</code> then applies all changes in one rollout. A paused Deployment that is never resumed looks as if it is \"not updating\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0458","card":"q-kubernetes-049-scaling-rollouts-and-patching","sec":"kubernetes-s050","q":"A Deployment is not updating after you change the image. What are the possible causes?","a":"<p>The rollout is paused. The new Pods fail their readiness probe, so with <code>maxUnavailable</code> limits the rollout stalls. The image cannot be pulled. A ResourceQuota or lack of node capacity blocks the new Pods. The change was applied to the wrong namespace or to a field outside the Pod template. After <code>progressDeadlineSeconds</code> (default 600) the Deployment reports <code>ProgressDeadlineExceeded</code>. Check <code>kubectl rollout status</code>, <code>describe deployment</code> and the ReplicaSets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0459","card":"q-kubernetes-049-scaling-rollouts-and-patching","sec":"kubernetes-s050","q":"What do the columns DESIRED, CURRENT and READY of <code>kubectl get rs</code> tell you?","a":"<p>DESIRED is <code>spec.replicas</code>. CURRENT is how many Pods the ReplicaSet owns. READY is how many of them pass readiness. If CURRENT is higher than READY, the Pods exist but are not ready (probe failing, still starting). If CURRENT is lower than DESIRED, Pods cannot be created (quota, admission or API errors). Old ReplicaSets at 0 are kept for rollback.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0460","card":"q-kubernetes-049-scaling-rollouts-and-patching","sec":"kubernetes-s050","q":"In this <code>kubectl patch</code> command, what do <code>op: add</code> and <code>/env/-</code> mean, and what fails if the container has no <code>env</code>?","a":"<p>This is a JSON Patch (RFC 6902). <code>op: add</code> with a path ending in <code>/-</code> appends an item to the end of an array. <code>containers/0</code> is the first container. If the <code>env</code> array does not exist yet, the patch fails because the parent path is missing. Then add the whole array in one operation. Changing the Pod template also triggers a rolling update.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl patch deployment &lt;deployment-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt; --type=&#x27;json&#x27; -p=&#x27;[{&quot;op&quot;: &quot;add&quot;, &quot;path&quot;: &quot;/spec/template/spec/containers/0/env/-&quot;, &quot;value&quot;: {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;NEW_VAR&quot;, &quot;value&quot;: &quot;new-value&quot;}}]&#x27;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0461","card":"q-kubernetes-049-scaling-rollouts-and-patching","sec":"kubernetes-s050","q":"What are the three kinds of <code>kubectl patch</code> and how do they differ?","a":"<p>Strategic merge (the default for built-in types) merges objects and merges lists by a key such as the container name. JSON merge patch (<code>--type=merge</code>) merges objects but replaces whole lists. JSON patch (<code>--type=json</code>) is an explicit list of operations (add, remove, replace) by path. Custom resources do not support strategic merge.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0462","card":"q-kubernetes-050-nodes-and-services","sec":"kubernetes-s051","q":"How do you drain a node for maintenance?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl drain &lt;node-name&gt; --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data\nkubectl uncordon &lt;node-name&gt;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0463","card":"q-kubernetes-050-nodes-and-services","sec":"kubernetes-s051","q":"How do you cordon a node and reschedule its Pods?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl cordon &lt;node-name&gt;\nkubectl drain &lt;node-name&gt; --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0464","card":"q-kubernetes-050-nodes-and-services","sec":"kubernetes-s051","q":"How do you taint a node to block Pods?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl taint nodes &lt;node-name&gt; key=value:NoSchedule</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0465","card":"q-kubernetes-050-nodes-and-services","sec":"kubernetes-s051","q":"How do you describe a service and check its endpoints?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl describe service &lt;service-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;\nkubectl get endpoints &lt;service-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0466","card":"q-kubernetes-050-nodes-and-services","sec":"kubernetes-s051","q":"What is the difference between <code>kubectl cordon</code>, <code>drain</code> and <code>uncordon</code>?","a":"<p><code>cordon</code> marks the node unschedulable, so no new Pods land there, but existing Pods keep running. <code>drain</code> cordons it and then evicts the Pods so they are rescheduled elsewhere. <code>uncordon</code> makes the node schedulable again. Drain does not bring Pods back automatically afterwards.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl drain &lt;node-name&gt; --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data\nkubectl uncordon &lt;node-name&gt;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0467","card":"q-kubernetes-050-nodes-and-services","sec":"kubernetes-s051","q":"Why does <code>kubectl drain</code> need <code>--delete-emptydir-data</code>, and why can a drain hang?","a":"<p>Pods that use <code>emptyDir</code> volumes lose that data when evicted, so drain refuses unless you confirm with the flag. A drain can hang because a PodDisruptionBudget does not allow another Pod to go down (for example a single replica with <code>minAvailable: 1</code>), or a Pod will not terminate. Fix the PDB or add replicas.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0468","card":"q-kubernetes-050-nodes-and-services","sec":"kubernetes-s051","q":"How do you remove a taint from a Kubernetes node, and what happens to Pods already running there?","a":"<p>Repeat the same command with a trailing minus: <code>kubectl taint nodes &lt;node-name&gt; key=value:NoSchedule-</code>. A <code>NoSchedule</code> taint only affects new scheduling, so running Pods stay. Use <code>NoExecute</code> to also evict running Pods that do not tolerate the taint.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl taint nodes &lt;node-name&gt; key=value:NoSchedule</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0469","card":"q-kubernetes-050-nodes-and-services","sec":"kubernetes-s051","q":"What changed for the Kubernetes Endpoints API in version 1.33, and what should you query instead?","a":"<p>The Endpoints API is deprecated in 1.33 in favor of EndpointSlices, which scale better because a large Service is split into several small slices and not one huge object. Query them with <code>kubectl get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=&lt;service&gt;</code>. An empty result for a Service means no Ready Pod matches its selector, so check labels and readiness.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0470","card":"q-kubernetes-050-nodes-and-services","sec":"kubernetes-s051","q":"A node shows <code>NotReady</code>. How do you investigate?","a":"<p>Run <code>kubectl describe node</code> and read Conditions (Ready, MemoryPressure, DiskPressure, PIDPressure, NetworkUnavailable) and events. Then log into the node and check the kubelet (<code>systemctl status kubelet</code>, <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code>), the container runtime, disk space, memory, and connectivity to the API server. Certificates that expired or a stopped CNI plugin are common causes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0471","card":"q-kubernetes-051-ansible-tasks-that-drive-kubectl","sec":"kubernetes-s052","q":"How do you use Ansible to apply a Kubernetes manifest and verify it?","a":"<p>Apply <code>pod.yaml</code>, read the phase with jsonpath, and fail the play if the Pod is not Running.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Apply K8s\n  hosts: localhost\n  tasks:\n  - name: Apply\n    ansible.builtin.command: kubectl apply -f pod.yaml\n  - name: Verify\n    ansible.builtin.command: kubectl get pod nginx-pod -o jsonpath=&#x27;{.status.phase}&#x27;\n    register: pod_status\n  - name: Fail if not running\n    ansible.builtin.fail: { msg: &quot;Pod not Running&quot; }\n    when: pod_status.stdout != &quot;Running&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0472","card":"q-kubernetes-051-ansible-tasks-that-drive-kubectl","sec":"kubernetes-s052","q":"How do you use Ansible to install <code>kubectl</code> and check the cluster?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Install kubectl\n  hosts: all\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n  - name: Install\n    ansible.builtin.shell: |\n      curl -LO &quot;https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl&quot;\n      chmod +x kubectl &amp;&amp; mv kubectl /usr/local/bin/\n    args: { creates: /usr/local/bin/kubectl }\n  - name: Check cluster\n    ansible.builtin.command: kubectl cluster-info\n    register: cluster_info\n  - name: Debug\n    ansible.builtin.debug: { var: cluster_info.stdout }</code></pre></div>\n<p>Run it with <code>ansible-playbook install_kubectl.yml</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0473","card":"q-kubernetes-051-ansible-tasks-that-drive-kubectl","sec":"kubernetes-s052","q":"How do you use Ansible to update a ConfigMap and restart the Pods?","a":"<p>Apply <code>configmap.yaml</code>, then restart the Deployment.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Update ConfigMap\n  hosts: localhost\n  tasks:\n  - name: Apply\n    ansible.builtin.command: kubectl apply -f configmap.yaml\n  - name: Restart\n    ansible.builtin.command: kubectl rollout restart deployment web-app -n default</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0474","card":"q-kubernetes-051-ansible-tasks-that-drive-kubectl","sec":"kubernetes-s052","q":"Why use the <code>kubernetes.core.k8s</code> Ansible module and not <code>ansible.builtin.command: kubectl apply</code>?","a":"<p>The <code>k8s</code> module talks to the API directly, reports <code>changed</code> only when something really changed (so the task is idempotent and works with check mode and diff), supports <code>state: present/absent</code>, <code>wait</code>, and templated definitions. The <code>command</code> task always reports changed and needs <code>kubectl</code> installed. The module needs the <code>kubernetes</code> Python library and the <code>kubernetes.core</code> collection on the host that runs it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Apply\n  ansible.builtin.command: kubectl apply -f pod.yaml</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0475","card":"q-kubernetes-051-ansible-tasks-that-drive-kubectl","sec":"kubernetes-s052","q":"After <code>kubectl apply -f pod.yaml</code>, a verify task reads the Pod phase once and fails if it is not Running. What is the problem?","a":"<p>The check runs a moment after apply, when the Pod is usually still Pending while the image is pulled, so the play fails even though the Pod would become Running. Wait for the condition: <code>kubectl wait --for=condition=Ready pod/nginx-pod --timeout=120s</code>, or use <code>retries</code> and <code>until</code> on the task, or <code>wait: true</code> in the <code>k8s</code> module.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Verify\n  ansible.builtin.command: kubectl get pod nginx-pod -o jsonpath=&#x27;{.status.phase}&#x27;\n  register: pod_status\n- name: Fail if not running\n  ansible.builtin.fail: { msg: &quot;Pod not Running&quot; }\n  when: pod_status.stdout != &quot;Running&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0476","card":"q-kubernetes-051-ansible-tasks-that-drive-kubectl","sec":"kubernetes-s052","q":"In Ansible, when do you use <code>ansible.builtin.shell</code> and not <code>ansible.builtin.command</code>?","a":"<p><code>command</code> runs a program directly with no shell. <code>shell</code> runs through <code>/bin/sh</code>, which is needed for <code>$(...)</code>, pipes, <code>&amp;&amp;</code> and redirects, as here. <code>command</code> is safer because no shell injection or expansion happens, so prefer it when you do not need shell features.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible.builtin.shell: |\n  curl -LO &quot;https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl&quot;\n  chmod +x kubectl &amp;&amp; mv kubectl /usr/local/bin/\nargs: { creates: /usr/local/bin/kubectl }</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0477","card":"q-kubernetes-051-ansible-tasks-that-drive-kubectl","sec":"kubernetes-s052","q":"What does <code>args: creates: /usr/local/bin/kubectl</code> do in an Ansible task?","a":"<p>The task is skipped if that file already exists. This makes an otherwise non-idempotent shell task safe to run repeatedly. <code>removes:</code> is the opposite: run only if the file exists.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0478","card":"q-kubernetes-051-ansible-tasks-that-drive-kubectl","sec":"kubernetes-s052","q":"What is risky about installing kubectl in a playbook with the <code>stable.txt</code> URL?","a":"<p>It downloads whatever the latest stable version is on the day of the run, with no checksum verification. Hosts end up with different versions, and kubectl is supported only within one minor version of the cluster's API server. Pin the version, verify the checksum, or install from a package repository.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0479","card":"q-kubernetes-051-ansible-tasks-that-drive-kubectl","sec":"kubernetes-s052","q":"How do you stop an Ansible task that only reads state, such as <code>kubectl get</code>, from showing as changed?","a":"<p>Add <code>changed_when: false</code> to the task. Use <code>failed_when</code> to define your own failure condition from the registered output, instead of a separate <code>fail</code> task.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0480","card":"q-kubernetes-052-checking-logs-and-exposing-services-with","sec":"kubernetes-s053","q":"How do you check Pod logs with Ansible and fail on errors?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Check logs\n  hosts: localhost\n  tasks:\n  - name: Get logs\n    ansible.builtin.command: kubectl logs &lt;pod-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;\n    register: pod_logs\n  - name: Fail on error\n    ansible.builtin.fail: { msg: &quot;Errors in logs&quot; }\n    when: &quot;&#x27;error&#x27; in pod_logs.stdout | lower&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0481","card":"q-kubernetes-052-checking-logs-and-exposing-services-with","sec":"kubernetes-s053","q":"How do you use Ansible to deploy a Service and expose it externally?","a":"<p>Apply a ClusterIP Service (<code>port: 80</code>, <code>targetPort: 8080</code>), then expose it as a LoadBalancer.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Deploy service\n  hosts: localhost\n  tasks:\n  - name: Apply\n    ansible.builtin.command: kubectl apply -f service.yaml\n  - name: Expose\n    ansible.builtin.command: kubectl expose service web-service --type=LoadBalancer --port=80 --name=web-lb -n default</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0482","card":"q-kubernetes-052-checking-logs-and-exposing-services-with","sec":"kubernetes-s053","q":"How do you troubleshoot an Ansible-deployed Pod that is not running?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl get pod &lt;pod-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;\nkubectl describe pod &lt;pod-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;\nkubectl logs &lt;pod-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;\nkubectl get pod &lt;pod-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt; -o yaml</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0483","card":"q-kubernetes-052-checking-logs-and-exposing-services-with","sec":"kubernetes-s053","q":"Why is failing a playbook when the Pod logs contain the word \"error\" fragile?","a":"<p>It matches harmless lines (for example \"0 errors\", or a debug message containing the word) and misses failures that use other wording. It also reads the whole log. Use readiness checks, exit codes, or structured log levels, and limit what you read with <code>--tail</code> or <code>--since</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Fail on error\n  ansible.builtin.fail: { msg: &quot;Errors in logs&quot; }\n  when: &quot;&#x27;error&#x27; in pod_logs.stdout | lower&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0484","card":"q-kubernetes-052-checking-logs-and-exposing-services-with","sec":"kubernetes-s053","q":"In Ansible, how does <code>register</code> work, and how is the result used in <code>when</code>?","a":"<p><code>register: pod_logs</code> saves the task's result into a variable. For <code>command</code> it has fields such as <code>stdout</code>, <code>stderr</code>, <code>rc</code> and <code>changed</code>. Later tasks use them in conditions (<code>when: pod_logs.rc != 0</code>) or templates. The variable exists only for the rest of that host's play.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0485","card":"q-kubernetes-052-checking-logs-and-exposing-services-with","sec":"kubernetes-s053","q":"In Kubernetes, how does <code>kubectl expose</code> compare with applying a Service manifest, and which do you prefer in automation?","a":"<p><code>kubectl expose</code> is imperative: it creates a Service from the selector of an existing object in one command, which is quick for experiments. A manifest is declarative, reviewable and repeatable in Git, and <code>expose</code> fails on the second run because the Service already exists. Use manifests in automation.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Expose\n  ansible.builtin.command: kubectl expose service web-service --type=LoadBalancer --port=80 --name=web-lb -n default</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0486","card":"q-kubernetes-052-checking-logs-and-exposing-services-with","sec":"kubernetes-s053","q":"What do you see in <code>kubectl get pod &lt;pod&gt; -o yaml</code> that helps debug a Pod that is not running?","a":"<p>The <code>status</code> section: <code>phase</code>, <code>conditions</code> (PodScheduled, Initialized, ContainersReady, Ready) with reasons, and <code>containerStatuses</code> with the current <code>state</code>, the <code>lastState</code> (including exit code and reason of the previous crash), and restart count. It also shows the final spec after defaulting and admission mutations, such as injected sidecars.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0487","card":"q-kubernetes-052-checking-logs-and-exposing-services-with","sec":"kubernetes-s053","q":"When would you use Ansible to manage Kubernetes and when would you use GitOps or Helm?","a":"<p>Ansible suits imperative orchestration that mixes systems: configuring the nodes, bootstrapping a cluster, calling cloud APIs, and then applying manifests. For continuous delivery of applications, GitOps or Helm fit better, because they keep reconciling the cluster to Git, detect drift and roll back. Many teams use Ansible to build the platform and GitOps to run applications on it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0488","card":"q-kubernetes-053-github-actions-workflows-that-run-kubect","sec":"kubernetes-s054","q":"How do you scale a Kubernetes deployment from GitHub Actions on a version tag?","a":"<p>Write the kubeconfig from a secret, then run <code>kubectl scale</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Scale K8s\non: { push: { tags: [&#x27;v*&#x27;] } }\njobs:\n  scale:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n    - uses: azure/setup-kubectl@v3\n    - run: echo &quot;${{ secrets.KUBECONFIG }}&quot; &gt; $HOME/.kube/config\n    - run: kubectl scale deployment web-app --replicas=5 -n default</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0489","card":"q-kubernetes-053-github-actions-workflows-that-run-kubect","sec":"kubernetes-s054","q":"How do you restart a rollout from GitHub Actions when config changes?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Restart Deployment\non: { push: { branches: [main], paths: [&#x27;config/*&#x27;] } }\njobs:\n  restart:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n    - uses: azure/setup-kubectl@v3\n    - run: echo &quot;${{ secrets.KUBECONFIG }}&quot; &gt; $HOME/.kube/config\n    - run: kubectl apply -f config/configmap.yaml\n    - run: kubectl rollout restart deployment web-app -n default</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0490","card":"q-kubernetes-053-github-actions-workflows-that-run-kubect","sec":"kubernetes-s054","q":"What are the problems with writing the kubeconfig in this GitHub Actions step?","a":"<p>The <code>.kube</code> directory may not exist, so add <code>mkdir -p $HOME/.kube</code>, and the file should be <code>chmod 600</code>. The secret is a long-lived credential, often cluster-admin, and anyone who can change the workflow can use it. Prefer a short-lived token: OIDC federation to the cloud provider (for example <code>aws eks update-kubeconfig</code> after assuming a role), and a ServiceAccount with a narrow Role. Use GitHub environments to protect production.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- run: echo &quot;${{ secrets.KUBECONFIG }}&quot; &gt; $HOME/.kube/config\n- run: kubectl scale deployment web-app --replicas=5 -n default</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0491","card":"q-kubernetes-053-github-actions-workflows-that-run-kubect","sec":"kubernetes-s054","q":"In GitHub Actions, how do the triggers <code>on: push: tags: ['v*']</code> and <code>on: push: paths: ['config/*']</code> differ?","a":"<p>The first runs when a tag matching <code>v*</code> (for example <code>v1.2.0</code>) is pushed, which fits release deployments. The second runs on pushes that change files under <code>config/</code>, with the <code>branches</code> filter limiting it to <code>main</code>. Filters in the same event are ANDed. Use <code>paths</code> to avoid deploying when nothing relevant changed.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>on: { push: { tags: [&#x27;v*&#x27;] } }</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0492","card":"q-kubernetes-053-github-actions-workflows-that-run-kubect","sec":"kubernetes-s054","q":"A GitHub-hosted runner cannot reach your cluster's API server. What are your options?","a":"<p>GitHub-hosted runners come from public IP ranges, so a private API endpoint is unreachable. Options: run a self-hosted runner inside the network or the cluster (for example with Actions Runner Controller), use a VPN or bastion, allow-list the runner ranges on a public endpoint (weak), or avoid push access entirely and use a pull-based GitOps controller inside the cluster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0493","card":"q-kubernetes-053-github-actions-workflows-that-run-kubect","sec":"kubernetes-s054","q":"Why is <code>kubectl scale</code> from a workflow a poor way to manage capacity in a cluster that uses an HPA?","a":"<p>The HPA owns the replica count and will overwrite your value within seconds, or your scale fights the HPA's decision. Change <code>minReplicas</code> and <code>maxReplicas</code> on the HPA object instead, or scale only workloads without an autoscaler.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0494","card":"q-kubernetes-053-github-actions-workflows-that-run-kubect","sec":"kubernetes-s054","q":"Why should you pin third-party actions such as <code>azure/setup-kubectl</code> in a deploy workflow?","a":"<p>A deploy job holds cluster credentials, and an action runs with access to them. A moving tag can be repointed, so a compromised action could steal the credentials. Pin to a full commit SHA (and let Dependabot update it) for third-party actions. Also set the <code>kubectl</code> version to match the cluster within one minor version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0495","card":"q-kubernetes-054-configuring-nodes-with-ansible-and-githu","sec":"kubernetes-s055","q":"How do you use GitHub Actions with Ansible to configure a Kubernetes node and verify it?","a":"<p>The workflow installs Ansible, runs the playbook, then lists nodes with <code>kubectl</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Configure Node\non: { push: { branches: [main] } }\njobs:\n  configure:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n    - run: sudo apt update &amp;&amp; sudo apt install -y ansible\n    - run: ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini configure_node.yml\n    - uses: azure/setup-kubectl@v3\n    - run: echo &quot;${{ secrets.KUBECONFIG }}&quot; &gt; $HOME/.kube/config\n    - run: kubectl get nodes</code></pre></div>\n<p>The playbook <code>configure_node.yml</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Configure node\n  hosts: k8s_nodes\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n  - name: Install kubeadm\n    ansible.builtin.package: { name: kubeadm, state: present }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0496","card":"q-kubernetes-054-configuring-nodes-with-ansible-and-githu","sec":"kubernetes-s055","q":"Why use Ansible and not Kubernetes manifests to configure Kubernetes nodes?","a":"<p>Node setup happens outside the Kubernetes API: OS packages, disabling swap, kernel modules and sysctls, container runtime, kubelet and kubeadm installation, joining the cluster. Kubernetes objects cannot do that on a host, so you need a host configuration tool such as Ansible, or immutable images built with Packer and cloud-init. Projects such as Kubespray automate it with Ansible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0497","card":"q-kubernetes-054-configuring-nodes-with-ansible-and-githu","sec":"kubernetes-s055","q":"In a playbook that installs <code>kubeadm</code> with the <code>ansible.builtin.package</code> module, what else must be set up first?","a":"<p>The Kubernetes package repository must be configured, since distribution repos do not carry current versions. The old <code>apt.kubernetes.io</code> and <code>yum.kubernetes.io</code> repos were deprecated and frozen in September 2023 and have since been removed. Use <code>pkgs.k8s.io</code>, which has one repository per minor version. You also need a container runtime installed, swap disabled (the kubelet refuses to start with swap on by default), and the kernel modules and sysctls for networking. Pin the version so nodes match.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Configure node\n  hosts: k8s_nodes\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n  - name: Install kubeadm\n    ansible.builtin.package: { name: kubeadm, state: present }</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0498","card":"q-kubernetes-054-configuring-nodes-with-ansible-and-githu","sec":"kubernetes-s055","q":"In this playbook, what do <code>hosts: k8s_nodes</code> and <code>become: yes</code> mean, and how does the workflow reach the machines?","a":"<p><code>hosts</code> names a group in the inventory file (<code>inventory.ini</code>), so the play runs on those machines over SSH. <code>become: yes</code> escalates to root with sudo, which package installs need. The CI runner therefore needs network access to the nodes and an SSH key stored as a secret, which is another reason to use a self-hosted runner for private nodes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0499","card":"q-kubernetes-055-why-use-python-for-kubernetes","sec":"kubernetes-s056","q":"What are the benefits of using Python to automate Kubernetes resource management?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Rich libraries</strong>: the <code>kubernetes</code> Python client simplifies API interactions for pods, services, and deployments.</li>\n  <li><strong>Flexibility</strong>: scripts can handle complex logic, error handling, and integrations with other tools.</li>\n  <li><strong>Community support</strong>: extensive documentation and community-driven tools.</li>\n  <li><strong>Portability</strong>: Python scripts work across environments.</li>\n  <li><strong>Automation</strong>: repetitive tasks like scaling, monitoring, and updating resources can be scripted.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0500","card":"q-kubernetes-055-why-use-python-for-kubernetes","sec":"kubernetes-s056","q":"What are the challenges of managing multi-cloud resources (for example AWS and Kubernetes) with Python?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Complexity</strong>: each API has its own nuances.</li>\n  <li><strong>Authentication</strong>: different credential mechanisms (AWS IAM vs Kubernetes RBAC).</li>\n  <li><strong>State management</strong>: reconciling states across clouds (Terraform state vs Kubernetes resources).</li>\n  <li><strong>Error handling</strong>: varied error formats and retry policies.</li>\n  <li><strong>Dependencies</strong>: library versions and compatibility.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0501","card":"q-kubernetes-055-why-use-python-for-kubernetes","sec":"kubernetes-s056","q":"How can Python be used to interact with the Kubernetes API for cluster management?","a":"<p>The <code>kubernetes</code> client supports CRUD operations on resources, watching resource changes, and executing commands in pods.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from kubernetes import client, config\nconfig.load_kube_config()\nv1 = client.CoreV1Api()\npods = v1.list_pod_for_all_namespaces()\nfor pod in pods.items:\n    print(f&quot;Pod: {pod.metadata.name}&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0502","card":"q-kubernetes-055-why-use-python-for-kubernetes","sec":"kubernetes-s056","q":"When would you use the Python Kubernetes client rather than <code>kubectl</code> in shell scripts, or client-go in Go?","a":"<p>Python fits automation scripts with real logic: loops, error handling, calling other APIs (cloud, Slack, databases), data analysis and ML platform glue. Shell is fine for one-off commands. Go (client-go, controller-runtime) is the better choice for production controllers and operators, because of its caching informers, work queues and ecosystem. Python clients are not as efficient for watching big clusters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0503","card":"q-kubernetes-055-why-use-python-for-kubernetes","sec":"kubernetes-s056","q":"In the Python <code>kubernetes</code> client, why is there a separate class such as <code>CoreV1Api</code> and <code>AppsV1Api</code>?","a":"<p>Each API group and version has its own client class, mirroring the REST API. <code>CoreV1Api</code> serves the core group (Pods, Services, ConfigMaps, Secrets, Nodes, Namespaces, Events). <code>AppsV1Api</code> serves Deployments, StatefulSets and DaemonSets. <code>BatchV1Api</code> serves Jobs and CronJobs. <code>RbacAuthorizationV1Api</code> serves roles. For custom resources use <code>CustomObjectsApi</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from kubernetes import client, config\nconfig.load_kube_config()\nv1 = client.CoreV1Api()\npods = v1.list_pod_for_all_namespaces()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0504","card":"q-kubernetes-055-why-use-python-for-kubernetes","sec":"kubernetes-s056","q":"In the Python <code>kubernetes</code> client, what does <code>config.load_kube_config()</code> do and why is it called before creating an API object?","a":"<p>It reads <code>~/.kube/config</code> (or the path in <code>KUBECONFIG</code>) and sets the default API server address, credentials and CA in the client's global configuration. API objects created afterward use that configuration. Without it the client has no host and fails to connect. Inside a Pod use <code>config.load_incluster_config()</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0505","card":"q-kubernetes-055-why-use-python-for-kubernetes","sec":"kubernetes-s056","q":"Why does <code>list_pod_for_all_namespaces()</code> become a problem on large clusters, and what do you do about it?","a":"<p>It returns every Pod in one response, which is heavy for the API server and for your memory. Use <code>list_namespaced_pod</code> with a namespace, filter on the server with <code>label_selector</code> and <code>field_selector</code>, paginate with <code>limit</code> and <code>_continue</code>, or use a watch with a cache instead of polling the full list.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0506","card":"q-kubernetes-055-why-use-python-for-kubernetes","sec":"kubernetes-s056","q":"Python scripts that manage both AWS and Kubernetes hit state drift. How do you reduce it?","a":"<p>Give each layer a single owner. Provision cloud infrastructure with a declarative tool such as Terraform (it keeps state), and deploy Kubernetes workloads from Git with Helm or GitOps. Use Python only for operations that need logic. Use separate credentials per system with least privilege, and make scripts idempotent so reruns are safe.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0507","card":"q-kubernetes-056-authenticating-and-listing-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s057","q":"How do you authenticate a Python script to a Kubernetes cluster's API?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Kubeconfig file</strong>: <code>config.load_kube_config()</code> reads <code>~/.kube/config</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>In-cluster config</strong>: inside a Pod, use <code>config.load_incluster_config()</code> with a service account token.</li>\n  <li><strong>Manual token</strong>: provide a bearer token and cluster URL explicitly.</li>\n  <li><strong>Certificates</strong>: use client certificates.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In-cluster example:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from kubernetes import client, config\n# Assumes running in a pod with a service account\nconfig.load_incluster_config()\nv1 = client.CoreV1Api()\npods = v1.list_pod_for_all_namespaces()\nfor pod in pods.items:\n    print(f&quot;Pod: {pod.metadata.name}&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0508","card":"q-kubernetes-056-authenticating-and-listing-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s057","q":"How do you list all Pods in a namespace with the <code>kubernetes</code> client?","a":"<p>Use <code>CoreV1Api.list_namespaced_pod</code>. Configure credentials with a kubeconfig or service account token.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from kubernetes import client, config\n# Use config.load_incluster_config() when running in a cluster\nconfig.load_kube_config()\nv1 = client.CoreV1Api()\nnamespace = &quot;default&quot;\npods = v1.list_namespaced_pod(namespace=namespace)\nfor pod in pods.items:\n    print(f&quot;Pod: {pod.metadata.name}, Status: {pod.status.phase}&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0509","card":"q-kubernetes-056-authenticating-and-listing-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s057","q":"How do you load credentials in a Python script that must run both on a laptop and inside a Pod?","a":"<p>Try the in-cluster config first and fall back to the kubeconfig:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>try:\n    config.load_incluster_config()\nexcept config.ConfigException:\n    config.load_kube_config()</code></pre></div>\n<p>The in-cluster loader needs the service environment variables and the mounted ServiceAccount token, which exist only inside a Pod.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from kubernetes import client, config</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0510","card":"q-kubernetes-056-authenticating-and-listing-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s057","q":"What does <code>config.load_incluster_config()</code> read, and what must you set up for the call to succeed?","a":"<p>It reads the API server address from the <code>KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST</code> and <code>KUBERNETES_SERVICE_PORT</code> environment variables, and the token and CA certificate from <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount</code>. The Pod must have the token mounted (not disabled with <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code>). Permissions come from the ServiceAccount's RBAC bindings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0511","card":"q-kubernetes-056-authenticating-and-listing-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s057","q":"A Python script running in a Pod gets <code>403 Forbidden</code> when listing Pods. What is the cause and the fix?","a":"<p>Authentication worked, but authorization failed: the Pod's ServiceAccount (the <code>default</code> one if none is set) has no RBAC permission to list Pods in that scope. Create a Role (or ClusterRole for all namespaces) allowing <code>get</code> and <code>list</code> on <code>pods</code>, and bind it to the ServiceAccount. Check with <code>kubectl auth can-i list pods --as=system:serviceaccount:&lt;ns&gt;:&lt;sa&gt;</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0512","card":"q-kubernetes-056-authenticating-and-listing-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s057","q":"In the Python <code>kubernetes</code> client, how do you load a specific context from a kubeconfig with several clusters?","a":"<p><code>config.load_kube_config(context=\"prod-cluster\")</code>, and <code>config_file=\"/path/to/config\"</code> for a non-default file. Without <code>context</code> the file's current context is used. In scripts that can change production, always pass the context explicitly so you do not hit the wrong cluster by accident.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0513","card":"q-kubernetes-056-authenticating-and-listing-pods","sec":"kubernetes-s057","q":"In the Python <code>kubernetes</code> client, what do <code>label_selector</code> and <code>field_selector</code> do when passed to <code>list_namespaced_pod</code>?","a":"<p>They list only Pods labeled <code>app=web</code> that run on node <code>node-1</code>. Both are sent to the API server, which filters before returning, so you transfer less data. The result is a <code>V1PodList</code>, and the Pods are in <code>.items</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pods = v1.list_namespaced_pod(\n    namespace=&quot;default&quot;,\n    label_selector=&quot;app=web&quot;,\n    field_selector=&quot;spec.nodeName=node-1&quot;,\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0514","card":"q-kubernetes-057-creating-and-scaling-deployments","sec":"kubernetes-s058","q":"How do you deploy a Kubernetes Deployment with the <code>kubernetes</code> client?","a":"<p>Build a <code>V1Deployment</code> and create it with <code>AppsV1Api</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from kubernetes import client, config\nconfig.load_kube_config()\napps_v1 = client.AppsV1Api()\ndeployment = client.V1Deployment(\n    metadata=client.V1ObjectMeta(name=&quot;nginx-deployment&quot;),\n    spec=client.V1DeploymentSpec(\n        replicas=3,\n        selector=client.V1LabelSelector(match_labels={&quot;app&quot;: &quot;nginx&quot;}),\n        template=client.V1PodTemplateSpec(\n            metadata=client.V1ObjectMeta(labels={&quot;app&quot;: &quot;nginx&quot;}),\n            spec=client.V1PodSpec(\n                containers=[\n                    client.V1Container(\n                        name=&quot;nginx&quot;,\n                        image=&quot;nginx:latest&quot;,\n                        ports=[client.V1ContainerPort(container_port=80)]\n                    )\n                ]\n            )\n        )\n    )\n)\nnamespace = &quot;default&quot;\napps_v1.create_namespaced_deployment(namespace=namespace, body=deployment)\nprint(&quot;Deployment created successfully&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0515","card":"q-kubernetes-057-creating-and-scaling-deployments","sec":"kubernetes-s058","q":"How do you scale a Deployment programmatically?","a":"<p>Use <code>AppsV1Api.patch_namespaced_deployment_scale</code> with the deployment name and namespace.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from kubernetes import client, config\nconfig.load_kube_config()\napps_v1 = client.AppsV1Api()\nnamespace = &quot;default&quot;\ndeployment_name = &quot;nginx-deployment&quot;\nreplicas = 5\nscale = client.V1Scale(\n    metadata=client.V1ObjectMeta(name=deployment_name, namespace=namespace),\n    spec=client.V1ScaleSpec(replicas=replicas)\n)\napps_v1.patch_namespaced_deployment_scale(\n    name=deployment_name,\n    namespace=namespace,\n    body=scale\n)\nprint(f&quot;Scaled {deployment_name} to {replicas} replicas&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0516","card":"q-kubernetes-057-creating-and-scaling-deployments","sec":"kubernetes-s058","q":"In the Python <code>kubernetes</code> client, why does a <code>V1Deployment</code> need <code>selector</code> and <code>template.metadata.labels</code> to agree?","a":"<p>The client objects map one-to-one to the YAML fields (with snake_case names). The selector is how the Deployment finds its Pods, so it must match the template labels or the API server rejects the object. The nested model classes are verbose, but they validate types before you send the request. A dictionary body also works.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>spec=client.V1DeploymentSpec(\n    replicas=3,\n    selector=client.V1LabelSelector(match_labels={&quot;app&quot;: &quot;nginx&quot;}),\n    template=client.V1PodTemplateSpec(\n        metadata=client.V1ObjectMeta(labels={&quot;app&quot;: &quot;nginx&quot;}),\n    ),\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0517","card":"q-kubernetes-057-creating-and-scaling-deployments","sec":"kubernetes-s058","q":"What happens when you call <code>create_namespaced_deployment</code> for a Deployment that already exists, and how do you make the script idempotent?","a":"<p>The API returns <code>409 Conflict</code> and the client raises <code>ApiException</code>. Catch it and then patch or replace the existing object, or check with <code>read_namespaced_deployment</code> first. For fully declarative behavior use server-side apply, or a dynamic client with an <code>apply</code> call, or call <code>kubectl apply</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0518","card":"q-kubernetes-057-creating-and-scaling-deployments","sec":"kubernetes-s058","q":"In the Python client, how does scaling through <code>patch_namespaced_deployment_scale</code> differ from patching <code>spec.replicas</code> of the Deployment?","a":"<p>The scale subresource takes a small <code>Scale</code> body that only changes the replica count. It is the same endpoint the HPA and <code>kubectl scale</code> use, and RBAC can grant <code>deployments/scale</code> without permission to edit the whole Deployment. Patching the Deployment works too but needs wider update permission and races with other fields.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0519","card":"q-kubernetes-057-creating-and-scaling-deployments","sec":"kubernetes-s058","q":"How can you pass a plain dictionary and not model classes to the Python Kubernetes client?","a":"<p>Every API method accepts a dict body with the same field names as the JSON API (camelCase keys, such as <code>matchLabels</code> and not <code>match_labels</code>). It is shorter and easier to build from YAML loaded with <code>yaml.safe_load</code>. Model classes give type checking and code completion.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apps_v1.patch_namespaced_deployment_scale(\n    name=&quot;nginx-deployment&quot;,\n    namespace=&quot;default&quot;,\n    body={&quot;spec&quot;: {&quot;replicas&quot;: 5}},\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0520","card":"q-kubernetes-057-creating-and-scaling-deployments","sec":"kubernetes-s058","q":"After scaling or updating a Deployment from Python, how do you wait until it is actually ready?","a":"<p>The API call returns when the change is accepted, not when Pods are ready. Poll <code>read_namespaced_deployment_status</code> until <code>status.ready_replicas</code> and <code>status.updated_replicas</code> equal <code>spec.replicas</code> and <code>observed_generation</code> has caught up, or use a watch with a timeout. Always set a deadline.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0521","card":"q-kubernetes-058-configmaps-and-services","sec":"kubernetes-s059","q":"How do you create a ConfigMap with the <code>kubernetes</code> client?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from kubernetes import client, config\nconfig.load_kube_config()\nv1 = client.CoreV1Api()\nconfig_map = client.V1ConfigMap(\n    metadata=client.V1ObjectMeta(name=&quot;my-configmap&quot;),\n    data={\n        &quot;database_url&quot;: &quot;mysql://user:pass@localhost:3306/db&quot;,\n        &quot;log_level&quot;: &quot;DEBUG&quot;\n    }\n)\nnamespace = &quot;default&quot;\nv1.create_namespaced_config_map(namespace=namespace, body=config_map)\nprint(&quot;ConfigMap created successfully&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0522","card":"q-kubernetes-058-configmaps-and-services","sec":"kubernetes-s059","q":"How do you delete a Service with the <code>kubernetes</code> client?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from kubernetes import client, config\nconfig.load_kube_config()\nv1 = client.CoreV1Api()\nnamespace = &quot;default&quot;\nservice_name = &quot;my-service&quot;\nv1.delete_namespaced_service(name=service_name, namespace=namespace)\nprint(f&quot;Service {service_name} deleted successfully&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0523","card":"q-kubernetes-058-configmaps-and-services","sec":"kubernetes-s059","q":"In Kubernetes, what is wrong with storing a database URL that contains a password in a ConfigMap?","a":"<p>ConfigMaps are not meant for secrets: they are plain text, readable by anyone with get access, shown in <code>kubectl describe</code>, and often committed to Git. Put the credential in a Secret (or an external secret manager) and build the URL in the app from separate values.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>data={&quot;database_url&quot;: &quot;mysql://user:pass@db:3306/app&quot;}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0524","card":"q-kubernetes-058-configmaps-and-services","sec":"kubernetes-s059","q":"Why must every value in a ConfigMap <code>data</code> map be a string when you create it from Python?","a":"<p>The API defines <code>data</code> as a map of strings. Passing an int or bool raises a validation error in the client or API server. Convert with <code>str(...)</code>, and parse the string back in the application. Use <code>binary_data</code> for bytes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0525","card":"q-kubernetes-058-configmaps-and-services","sec":"kubernetes-s059","q":"What happens to the Pods when you call <code>delete_namespaced_service</code> in the Python client?","a":"<p>Only the Service is deleted: the Pods keep running but lose their stable virtual IP and DNS name. Clients that call the Service start failing. For a <code>LoadBalancer</code> Service the cloud load balancer is released too. If the Service does not exist the client raises <code>ApiException</code> with status 404.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>v1.delete_namespaced_service(name=&quot;my-service&quot;, namespace=&quot;default&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0526","card":"q-kubernetes-059-events-errors-and-rbac","sec":"kubernetes-s060","q":"How do you monitor Kubernetes events with Python?","a":"<p>Call <code>list_namespaced_event</code>, or open a watch stream with <code>watch.Watch</code> for real-time events.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from kubernetes import client, config, watch\nconfig.load_kube_config()\nv1 = client.CoreV1Api()\nnamespace = &quot;default&quot;\nw = watch.Watch()\nfor event in w.stream(v1.list_namespaced_event, namespace=namespace):\n    evt = event[&#x27;object&#x27;]\n    print(f&quot;Event: {evt.type}, Reason: {evt.reason}, Message: {evt.message}&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0527","card":"q-kubernetes-059-events-errors-and-rbac","sec":"kubernetes-s060","q":"How do you handle Kubernetes API errors in Python?","a":"<p>Catch <code>ApiException</code> and check the status code and reason (404 not found, 403 unauthorized).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from kubernetes import client, config\nfrom kubernetes.client.rest import ApiException\nconfig.load_kube_config()\nv1 = client.CoreV1Api()\ntry:\n    namespace = &quot;default&quot;\n    pod_name = &quot;non-existent-pod&quot;\n    v1.read_namespaced_pod(name=pod_name, namespace=namespace)\nexcept ApiException as e:\n    if e.status == 404:\n        print(f&quot;Pod {pod_name} not found&quot;)\n    elif e.status == 403:\n        print(&quot;Permission denied&quot;)\n    else:\n        print(f&quot;Error: {e}&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0528","card":"q-kubernetes-059-events-errors-and-rbac","sec":"kubernetes-s060","q":"How do you manage Kubernetes RBAC policies with Python?","a":"<p>Use <code>RbacAuthorizationV1Api</code> to create, update, or delete <code>Role</code>, <code>RoleBinding</code>, <code>ClusterRole</code>, and <code>ClusterRoleBinding</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from kubernetes import client, config\nconfig.load_kube_config()\nrbac_v1 = client.RbacAuthorizationV1Api()\nrole = client.V1Role(\n    metadata=client.V1ObjectMeta(name=&quot;pod-reader&quot;),\n    rules=[\n        client.V1PolicyRule(\n            api_groups=[&quot;&quot;],\n            resources=[&quot;pods&quot;],\n            verbs=[&quot;get&quot;, &quot;list&quot;, &quot;watch&quot;]\n        )\n    ]\n)\nnamespace = &quot;default&quot;\nrbac_v1.create_namespaced_role(namespace=namespace, body=role)\nrole_binding = client.V1RoleBinding(\n    metadata=client.V1ObjectMeta(name=&quot;pod-reader-binding&quot;),\n    subjects=[\n        client.V1Subject(kind=&quot;User&quot;, name=&quot;jane&quot;, api_group=&quot;rbac.authorization.k8s.io&quot;)\n    ],\n    role_ref=client.V1RoleRef(\n        kind=&quot;Role&quot;,\n        name=&quot;pod-reader&quot;,\n        api_group=&quot;rbac.authorization.k8s.io&quot;\n    )\n)\nrbac_v1.create_namespaced_role_binding(namespace=namespace, body=role_binding)\nprint(&quot;Role and RoleBinding created successfully&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0529","card":"q-kubernetes-059-events-errors-and-rbac","sec":"kubernetes-s060","q":"In this Python watch loop, what is the difference between <code>event['type']</code> and <code>evt.type</code>?","a":"<p><code>event['type']</code> is the watch notification kind: <code>ADDED</code>, <code>MODIFIED</code> or <code>DELETED</code>. <code>event['object']</code> is the Kubernetes Event object, and its <code>type</code> field is the severity, <code>Normal</code> or <code>Warning</code>. Filter on <code>evt.type == \"Warning\"</code> to find problems.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>w = watch.Watch()\nfor event in w.stream(v1.list_namespaced_event, namespace=namespace):\n    evt = event[&#x27;object&#x27;]\n    print(f&quot;Event: {evt.type}, Reason: {evt.reason}, Message: {evt.message}&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0530","card":"q-kubernetes-059-events-errors-and-rbac","sec":"kubernetes-s060","q":"Why use <code>watch.Watch().stream(...)</code> instead of calling <code>list_namespaced_event</code> in a loop?","a":"<p>A watch is a long-lived HTTP stream: the API server pushes each change as it happens, so you get low latency and avoid repeated full lists. Polling is slower to notice changes, can miss short-lived states between polls, and loads the API server.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0531","card":"q-kubernetes-059-events-errors-and-rbac","sec":"kubernetes-s060","q":"A Python watch stream stops after a while or fails with <code>410 Gone</code>. How do you make it reliable?","a":"<p>A watch ends when the server timeout is hit or the connection drops. A <code>410 Gone</code> means the <code>resourceVersion</code> you resume from is too old and was compacted. Wrap the stream in a loop: on end, resume from the last seen <code>resourceVersion</code>, and on 410 do a fresh list, then start a new watch. Set <code>timeout_seconds</code>. Controllers and informers do exactly this.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0532","card":"q-kubernetes-059-events-errors-and-rbac","sec":"kubernetes-s060","q":"In Python, what do the HTTP status codes in <code>ApiException.status</code> mean for Kubernetes calls?","a":"<p>401 means unauthenticated (bad or expired credentials). 403 means authenticated but not allowed by RBAC. 404 means the object or namespace does not exist. 409 means conflict (already exists, or a stale <code>resourceVersion</code> on update). 422 means invalid object. 429 and 5xx are throttling or server problems, which are worth retrying with back-off.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0533","card":"q-kubernetes-059-events-errors-and-rbac","sec":"kubernetes-s060","q":"In Python RBAC code, why does the Role include the <code>watch</code> verb together with <code>get</code> and <code>list</code>?","a":"<p><code>get</code> reads one object, <code>list</code> reads a collection, and <code>watch</code> streams changes. Controllers, informers and <code>kubectl get -w</code> need <code>watch</code>, and without it they fail with 403 even though <code>list</code> works. Give only the verbs the client uses.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>client.V1PolicyRule(\n    api_groups=[&quot;&quot;],\n    resources=[&quot;pods&quot;],\n    verbs=[&quot;get&quot;, &quot;list&quot;, &quot;watch&quot;]\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0534","card":"q-kubernetes-059-events-errors-and-rbac","sec":"kubernetes-s060","q":"Why might creating a Role from a script fail with <code>403 Forbidden</code> even though the YAML is right?","a":"<p>Kubernetes prevents privilege escalation: the identity creating the Role must already hold every permission the Role grants, or have the <code>escalate</code> verb. The script's own account needs both permission to create Roles and the permissions it hands out.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0535","card":"q-kubernetes-060-operators-and-custom-controllers","sec":"kubernetes-s061","q":"What is a Kubernetes operator, and how can Python create one?","a":"<p>An operator is a custom controller that extends Kubernetes to manage complex applications. It uses custom resources and controllers to automate tasks like scaling or backups. In Python, use <code>kopf</code> (Kubernetes Operator Pythonic Framework) or the <code>kubernetes</code> client to define custom resources (CRDs), reconcile desired and actual state, and deploy the operator as a Pod in the cluster.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0536","card":"q-kubernetes-060-operators-and-custom-controllers","sec":"kubernetes-s061","q":"How can Python create a custom Kubernetes controller?","a":"<p>A controller watches changes to custom resources and reconciles the desired state. Use the <code>kubernetes</code> client with <code>watch.Watch</code> to monitor CRDs, or use <code>kopf</code> or <code>operator-sdk</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import kopf\nfrom kubernetes import client, config\nconfig.load_kube_config()\n\n@kopf.on.create(&#x27;example.com&#x27;, &#x27;v1&#x27;, &#x27;myresources&#x27;)\ndef create_fn(spec, name, namespace, **kwargs):\n    print(f&quot;Creating resource {name} in namespace {namespace}&quot;)\n    # Add logic to reconcile the resource\n    return {&#x27;message&#x27;: &#x27;Resource created&#x27;}\n\n@kopf.on.update(&#x27;example.com&#x27;, &#x27;v1&#x27;, &#x27;myresources&#x27;)\ndef update_fn(spec, name, namespace, **kwargs):\n    print(f&quot;Updating resource {name} in namespace {namespace}&quot;)\n    # Add logic to update the resource</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0537","card":"q-kubernetes-060-operators-and-custom-controllers","sec":"kubernetes-s061","q":"A Python operator is not responding to custom resource events. How do you troubleshoot it?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Check logs</strong>: inspect operator logs for errors.</li>\n  <li><strong>Verify RBAC</strong>: the operator needs permission to watch custom resources.</li>\n  <li><strong>Test event handling</strong>: simulate events to verify the handler.</li>\n  <li><strong>Debug code</strong>: add logging to the event loop.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from kubernetes import client, config, watch\nimport logging\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO)\n\ndef watch_custom_resources(namespace, group, version, plural):\n    config.load_kube_config()\n    custom_api = client.CustomObjectsApi()\n    w = watch.Watch()\n    for event in w.stream(custom_api.list_namespaced_custom_object, group, version, namespace, plural):\n        logging.info(f&quot;Event: {event[&#x27;type&#x27;]}, Object: {event[&#x27;object&#x27;][&#x27;metadata&#x27;][&#x27;name&#x27;]}&quot;)\n        # Add event handling logic here\n\nwatch_custom_resources(&quot;default&quot;, &quot;example.com&quot;, &quot;v1&quot;, &quot;myresources&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0538","card":"q-kubernetes-060-operators-and-custom-controllers","sec":"kubernetes-s061","q":"What do the arguments of this Kopf decorator identify?","a":"<p>They name the custom resource type to watch: the API group (<code>example.com</code>), the version (<code>v1</code>) and the plural resource name (<code>myresources</code>), as defined in the CRD. The handler runs when an object of that type is created. <code>spec</code>, <code>name</code> and <code>namespace</code> are passed in by Kopf from the object. Kopf has similar decorators for update, delete, timers and field changes.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>@kopf.on.create(&#x27;example.com&#x27;, &#x27;v1&#x27;, &#x27;myresources&#x27;)\ndef create_fn(spec, name, namespace, **kwargs):\n    print(f&quot;Creating resource {name} in namespace {namespace}&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0539","card":"q-kubernetes-060-operators-and-custom-controllers","sec":"kubernetes-s061","q":"Why does the Kubernetes API use the plural resource name (<code>myresources</code>) and not the kind (<code>MyResource</code>) when you call it from code?","a":"<p>REST paths are built from the plural resource name: <code>/apis/&lt;group&gt;/&lt;version&gt;/namespaces/&lt;ns&gt;/&lt;plural&gt;</code>. The kind is the type name inside the object body. Both are defined in the CRD (<code>names.plural</code> and <code>names.kind</code>). Using the wrong one is a common reason for a 404 when calling <code>CustomObjectsApi</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0540","card":"q-kubernetes-060-operators-and-custom-controllers","sec":"kubernetes-s061","q":"What does a Kopf handler's return value do?","a":"<p>Kopf stores the returned value in the status of the custom resource, under the handler's name. So you can see handler results with <code>kubectl get myresource -o yaml</code>. Exceptions raised by the handler make Kopf retry it with back-off. A handler must be idempotent for that reason.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>@kopf.on.create(&#x27;example.com&#x27;, &#x27;v1&#x27;, &#x27;myresources&#x27;)\ndef create_fn(spec, name, namespace, **kwargs):\n    return {&#x27;message&#x27;: &#x27;Resource created&#x27;}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0541","card":"q-kubernetes-060-operators-and-custom-controllers","sec":"kubernetes-s061","q":"Your Python operator does not react when you create a custom resource. What do you check?","a":"<p>The CRD is installed and its group, version and plural match the handler. The operator Pod is running and its logs show startup without errors. The operator's ServiceAccount has RBAC to list and watch that resource (and to create what it manages). The operator watches the right namespace. Run it locally with <code>kopf run</code> against the cluster to see the logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0542","card":"q-kubernetes-060-operators-and-custom-controllers","sec":"kubernetes-s061","q":"In <code>CustomObjectsApi.list_namespaced_custom_object</code>, what do the positional arguments identify?","a":"<p>They are the API group, API version, namespace and the plural resource name of the custom resource to list. Together they form the REST path. For cluster-scoped custom resources use <code>list_cluster_custom_object</code> (no namespace).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>custom_api.list_namespaced_custom_object(group, version, namespace, plural)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0543","card":"q-kubernetes-060-operators-and-custom-controllers","sec":"kubernetes-s061","q":"What do you need to package and run a Python operator in a cluster?","a":"<p>A container image with the code, a Deployment (usually one replica, or several with leader election so only one is active), a ServiceAccount with RBAC to watch and change the resources it manages, and the CRD installed before it starts. Operators are long-running, so add probes and resource limits and ship logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0544","card":"q-kubernetes-060-operators-and-custom-controllers","sec":"kubernetes-s061","q":"Why does an operator keep working correctly after it was down for an hour and missed events?","a":"<p>Controllers are level-based. On start the operator lists current objects and reconciles each one toward its spec, so it does not depend on having seen every past event. This is why reconcile logic should compare desired and actual state and not replay the history of changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0545","card":"q-kubernetes-061-python-troubleshooting-scenarios","sec":"kubernetes-s062","q":"A Pod is failing because of a misconfigured ConfigMap. How do you diagnose and fix it with Python?","a":"<p>Inspect the Pod status, read the ConfigMap, and update it if a key is missing.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from kubernetes import client, config\n\ndef fix_configmap(namespace, pod_name, configmap_name):\n    config.load_kube_config()  # Or load_incluster_config() in a cluster\n    v1 = client.CoreV1Api()\n    pod = v1.read_namespaced_pod(pod_name, namespace)\n    if pod.status.phase != &quot;Running&quot;:\n        print(f&quot;Pod {pod_name} is not running: {pod.status.phase}&quot;)\n        for container_status in pod.status.container_statuses:\n            if container_status.state.terminated:\n                print(f&quot;Exit code: {container_status.state.terminated.exit_code}&quot;)\n    configmap = v1.read_namespaced_config_map(configmap_name, namespace)\n    if &quot;required_key&quot; not in configmap.data:\n        configmap.data[&quot;required_key&quot;] = &quot;required_value&quot;\n        v1.replace_namespaced_config_map(configmap_name, namespace, configmap)\n        print(f&quot;Updated ConfigMap {configmap_name}&quot;)\n\nfix_configmap(&quot;default&quot;, &quot;my-pod&quot;, &quot;my-configmap&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0546","card":"q-kubernetes-061-python-troubleshooting-scenarios","sec":"kubernetes-s062","q":"An Ansible playbook is failing on certain Kubernetes nodes because of network issues. How do you diagnose it with Python?","a":"<p>Check node status with the <code>kubernetes</code> client and verify network configuration (VPC, security groups) with <code>boto3</code>. Restart or replace the problematic nodes.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from kubernetes import client, config\nimport boto3\n\ndef diagnose_k8s_nodes(namespace):\n    config.load_kube_config()\n    v1 = client.CoreV1Api()\n    nodes = v1.list_node()\n    for node in nodes.items:\n        for condition in node.status.conditions:\n            if condition.type == &quot;NetworkUnavailable&quot; and condition.status == &quot;True&quot;:\n                print(f&quot;Node {node.metadata.name} has network issues&quot;)\n                ec2 = boto3.client(&quot;ec2&quot;)\n                response = ec2.describe_instances(Filters=[{&quot;Name&quot;: &quot;private-dns-name&quot;, &quot;Values&quot;: [node.metadata.name]}])\n                instance_id = response[&quot;Reservations&quot;][0][&quot;Instances&quot;][0][&quot;InstanceId&quot;]\n                ec2.reboot_instances(InstanceIds=[instance_id])\n                print(f&quot;Rebooted node {node.metadata.name}&quot;)\n\ndiagnose_k8s_nodes(&quot;default&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0547","card":"q-kubernetes-061-python-troubleshooting-scenarios","sec":"kubernetes-s062","q":"In this Python Pod diagnosis code, why might it print nothing useful for a Pod that is in CrashLoopBackOff?","a":"<p>In CrashLoopBackOff the container's current <code>state</code> is usually <code>waiting</code> (with reason CrashLoopBackOff), so <code>state.terminated</code> is empty. The exit code of the last crash is in <code>container_status.last_state.terminated</code>. Also, <code>container_statuses</code> can be <code>None</code> for a Pod that has not started yet, so the loop raises <code>TypeError</code> unless you check for it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>for container_status in pod.status.container_statuses:\n    if container_status.state.terminated:\n        print(f&quot;Exit code: {container_status.state.terminated.exit_code}&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0548","card":"q-kubernetes-061-python-troubleshooting-scenarios","sec":"kubernetes-s062","q":"Why is it risky to have a script automatically patch a ConfigMap by adding a default value when a key is missing?","a":"<p>It hides a deployment mistake and may put a wrong value into production. It also changes state outside Git, so the next deploy overwrites it. Pods reading the ConfigMap through environment variables need a restart to see it. Alert and fix the source of truth, and auto-remediate only safe, well-understood cases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0549","card":"q-kubernetes-061-python-troubleshooting-scenarios","sec":"kubernetes-s062","q":"In this Python and boto3 diagnosis, what is dangerous about rebooting the instance inside the loop?","a":"<p>Rebooting a node abruptly kills every Pod on it, without cordon and drain, so workloads see an outage. <code>response[\"Reservations\"][0]</code> raises <code>IndexError</code> if no instance matches (the node name may not equal the private DNS name). It can reboot several nodes in a row during a wider network problem. Cordon and drain first, limit how many nodes are touched, and prefer the node group's auto-repair or replacement.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>if condition.type == &quot;NetworkUnavailable&quot; and condition.status == &quot;True&quot;:\n    ec2 = boto3.client(&quot;ec2&quot;)\n    response = ec2.describe_instances(Filters=[{&quot;Name&quot;: &quot;private-dns-name&quot;, &quot;Values&quot;: [node.metadata.name]}])\n    instance_id = response[&quot;Reservations&quot;][0][&quot;Instances&quot;][0][&quot;InstanceId&quot;]\n    ec2.reboot_instances(InstanceIds=[instance_id])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0550","card":"q-kubernetes-061-python-troubleshooting-scenarios","sec":"kubernetes-s062","q":"Why does a diagnosis of a node network problem look at both the Kubernetes API and AWS APIs?","a":"<p>The Kubernetes API shows node conditions (<code>NetworkUnavailable</code>, <code>Ready</code>) and events, which tell you which node is affected. The cause is often in the infrastructure layer: security groups, route tables, subnets and ENI limits, which only the cloud API (boto3) shows. A cloud-hosted node is just an EC2 instance, so you need both views.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0551","card":"q-kubernetes-062-python-autoscaling-and-resource-analysis","sec":"kubernetes-s063","q":"How do you scale a deployment on CPU usage with Python and the Kubernetes API?","a":"<p>Read CPU usage from the Metrics API and scale with the AppsV1 API.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from kubernetes import client, config\nimport time\n\ndef scale_deployment(namespace, deployment_name, cpu_threshold=70, max_replicas=10):\n    config.load_kube_config()\n    apps_v1 = client.AppsV1Api()\n    metrics_api = client.CustomObjectsApi()\n    while True:\n        deployment = apps_v1.read_namespaced_deployment(deployment_name, namespace)\n        current_replicas = deployment.spec.replicas\n        metrics = metrics_api.list_namespaced_custom_object(\n            group=&quot;metrics.k8s.io&quot;, version=&quot;v1beta1&quot;, namespace=namespace, plural=&quot;pods&quot;\n        )\n        cpu_usage = 0\n        for item in metrics[&quot;items&quot;]:\n            if item[&quot;metadata&quot;][&quot;name&quot;].startswith(deployment_name):\n                cpu_usage += sum(int(c[&quot;usage&quot;][&quot;cpu&quot;].rstrip(&quot;n&quot;)) for c in item[&quot;containers&quot;])\n        if cpu_usage &gt; cpu_threshold and current_replicas &lt; max_replicas:\n            new_replicas = min(current_replicas + 1, max_replicas)\n            deployment.spec.replicas = new_replicas\n            apps_v1.patch_namespaced_deployment(deployment_name, namespace, deployment)\n            print(f&quot;Scaled up to {new_replicas} replicas&quot;)\n        elif cpu_usage &lt; cpu_threshold / 2 and current_replicas &gt; 1:\n            new_replicas = max(current_replicas - 1, 1)\n            deployment.spec.replicas = new_replicas\n            apps_v1.patch_namespaced_deployment(deployment_name, namespace, deployment)\n            print(f&quot;Scaled down to {new_replicas} replicas&quot;)\n        time.sleep(60)\n\nscale_deployment(&quot;default&quot;, &quot;my-deployment&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0552","card":"q-kubernetes-062-python-autoscaling-and-resource-analysis","sec":"kubernetes-s063","q":"What is wrong with comparing <code>cpu_usage</code> to <code>cpu_threshold=70</code> in this scaling loop?","a":"<p>The Metrics API returns CPU as a quantity string such as <code>250000n</code> (nanocores) or <code>15m</code> (millicores). The code sums nanocores and compares them to 70, so it is almost always above the threshold. It also fails with a <code>ValueError</code> for values in millicores, because <code>rstrip(\"n\")</code> does not strip <code>m</code>. Convert to one unit (for example millicores) and compare to a percentage of the requests, as the HPA does.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>cpu_usage += sum(int(c[&quot;usage&quot;][&quot;cpu&quot;].rstrip(&quot;n&quot;)) for c in item[&quot;containers&quot;])\n...\nif cpu_usage &gt; cpu_threshold and current_replicas &lt; max_replicas:</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0553","card":"q-kubernetes-062-python-autoscaling-and-resource-analysis","sec":"kubernetes-s063","q":"Why is a hand-written polling loop a poor replacement for the Kubernetes HPA?","a":"<p>It has to be run and kept alive as a single process (no high availability), uses a fixed polling interval, and has no stabilization window, tolerance or rate limits, so it can flap. It fights other controllers or GitOps over <code>replicas</code>, and needs custom RBAC and error handling. The HPA does all of this and supports custom metrics. Write your own only for logic the HPA cannot express.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0554","card":"q-kubernetes-062-python-autoscaling-and-resource-analysis","sec":"kubernetes-s063","q":"In the scaling loop, why does the script scale down only below half of the threshold?","a":"<p>A gap between the scale-up and scale-down thresholds is hysteresis. If both used the same value, a load hovering near it would trigger scale up and scale down repeatedly (flapping). The gap lets the system settle. The HPA gets the same effect from its tolerance and stabilization window.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>...\nelif cpu_usage &lt; cpu_threshold / 2 and current_replicas &gt; 1:</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0555","card":"q-kubernetes-062-python-autoscaling-and-resource-analysis","sec":"kubernetes-s063","q":"Why is matching Pods with <code>item[\"metadata\"][\"name\"].startswith(deployment_name)</code> fragile?","a":"<p>A name prefix also matches other workloads (<code>web</code> matches <code>web-admin-...</code>) and depends on naming. Select by the Deployment's label selector (<code>label_selector</code> on the Metrics API call, or on the Pod list) so you get exactly the Pods it owns.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0556","card":"q-kubernetes-062-python-autoscaling-and-resource-analysis","sec":"kubernetes-s063","q":"Which API groups does Metrics Server serve, and what does that mean for a Python client?","a":"<p>Pod and node metrics are served under <code>metrics.k8s.io/v1beta1</code> and there is no typed client class in the core client, so you call it through <code>CustomObjectsApi</code> with group, version and plural (<code>pods</code> or <code>nodes</code>). It requires metrics-server installed, and it gives only current values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0557","card":"q-kubernetes-062-python-autoscaling-and-resource-analysis","sec":"kubernetes-s063","q":"When would you use KEDA rather than the HPA or a custom script?","a":"<p>KEDA scales Deployments (and Jobs) from event systems such as queue length, Kafka lag, cron schedules or Prometheus queries, and can scale to zero and back from zero, which the plain HPA cannot do. It works with an HPA under the hood. Use it for event-driven and queue-driven workloads.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0558","card":"q-kubernetes-063-analyzing-resource-usage-with-python","sec":"kubernetes-s064","q":"A cluster is running out of resources. How do you analyze usage with Python and suggest optimizations?","a":"<p>Use the <code>kubernetes</code> client and Metrics API to compare usage with limits.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from kubernetes import client, config\n\ndef analyze_k8s_resources(namespace):\n    config.load_kube_config()\n    v1 = client.CoreV1Api()\n    metrics_api = client.CustomObjectsApi()\n    pods = v1.list_namespaced_pod(namespace)\n    for pod in pods.items:\n        metrics = metrics_api.get_namespaced_custom_object(\n            group=&quot;metrics.k8s.io&quot;, version=&quot;v1beta1&quot;, namespace=namespace, plural=&quot;pods&quot;, name=pod.metadata.name\n        )\n        for container in metrics[&quot;containers&quot;]:\n            cpu = container[&quot;usage&quot;][&quot;cpu&quot;].rstrip(&quot;n&quot;)\n            memory = container[&quot;usage&quot;][&quot;memory&quot;].rstrip(&quot;Ki&quot;)\n            print(f&quot;Pod: {pod.metadata.name}, Container: {container[&#x27;name&#x27;]}, CPU: {cpu}n, Memory: {memory}Ki&quot;)\n            if int(cpu) &gt; 500:  # Example threshold\n                print(f&quot;Recommendation: Reduce CPU limit for {container[&#x27;name&#x27;]}&quot;)\n\nanalyze_k8s_resources(&quot;default&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0559","card":"q-kubernetes-063-analyzing-resource-usage-with-python","sec":"kubernetes-s064","q":"A Python script calling the Kubernetes API is timing out on a large cluster. How do you optimize it?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Use pagination</strong>: limit results with <code>limit</code> and <code>continue</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Increase the timeout</strong>: adjust the HTTP client timeout.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cache results</strong>: store frequently accessed data locally.</li>\n  <li><strong>Parallelize requests</strong>: use <code>concurrent.futures</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from kubernetes import client, config\n\ndef list_pods_optimized(namespace):\n    config.load_kube_config()\n    v1 = client.CoreV1Api()\n    pods = []\n    limit = 100\n    continue_token = None\n    while True:\n        response = v1.list_namespaced_pod(namespace, limit=limit, _continue=continue_token)\n        pods.extend(response.items)\n        continue_token = response.metadata._continue\n        if not continue_token:\n            break\n    for pod in pods:\n        print(f&quot;Pod: {pod.metadata.name}&quot;)\n\nlist_pods_optimized(&quot;default&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"kubernetes-f0560","card":"q-kubernetes-063-analyzing-resource-usage-with-python","sec":"kubernetes-s064","q":"What bug does this memory parsing have?","a":"<p><code>rstrip(\"Ki\")</code> removes any trailing characters in the set <code>K</code> and <code>i</code>, not the suffix <code>\"Ki\"</code>. It also leaves the value unparsed when metrics-server reports <code>Mi</code> or plain bytes. Parse the quantity properly: check the suffix (<code>Ki</code>, <code>Mi</code>, <code>Gi</code>), multiply to bytes, or use a quantity parser such as <code>kubernetes.utils.parse_quantity</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>memory = container[&quot;usage&quot;][&quot;memory&quot;].rstrip(&quot;Ki&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0561","card":"q-kubernetes-063-analyzing-resource-usage-with-python","sec":"kubernetes-s064","q":"In Kubernetes resource units, how do <code>m</code> and <code>n</code> suffixes on CPU relate to cores?","a":"<p>1 core is 1000 millicores (<code>1000m</code>) and 1,000,000,000 nanocores (<code>n</code>). So <code>500m</code> is half a core. The Metrics API often reports nanocores, such as <code>250000000n</code>, which is <code>250m</code>. Memory uses <code>Ki</code>, <code>Mi</code>, <code>Gi</code> (binary) or <code>K</code>, <code>M</code>, <code>G</code> (decimal).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0562","card":"q-kubernetes-063-analyzing-resource-usage-with-python","sec":"kubernetes-s064","q":"Why is a single snapshot of current usage not enough to decide Pod requests and limits?","a":"<p>Usage varies with time of day, traffic and deploys. A snapshot may catch an idle moment or a peak. Look at percentiles over days (for example P95 for requests and the maximum plus headroom for memory) in Prometheus. The Vertical Pod Autoscaler in recommendation mode (and tools such as Goldilocks) does this and suggests values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0563","card":"q-kubernetes-063-analyzing-resource-usage-with-python","sec":"kubernetes-s064","q":"When you right-size a Kubernetes workload, why do you compare usage with both requests and limits?","a":"<p>Usage far below requests means wasted reserved capacity and cost. Usage above requests, but below limits, means noisy-neighbour risk and eviction under node pressure. Usage near the memory limit means OOM kills, and near the CPU limit means throttling. The comparison shows which one to change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"kubernetes-f0564","card":"q-kubernetes-063-analyzing-resource-usage-with-python","sec":"kubernetes-s064","q":"In the Python <code>kubernetes</code> client, how does <code>limit</code> with <code>_continue</code> paginate list calls, and why use it?","a":"<p>The server returns at most <code>limit</code> items and a continue token in the list metadata. You pass that token back to get the next chunk, until it is empty. This keeps each response and your memory small and avoids timeouts on big lists. A token expires after a few minutes (<code>410 Gone</code>), then restart the list. Chunking does not reduce total work, so also filter with selectors.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>response = v1.list_namespaced_pod(namespace, limit=limit, _continue=continue_token)\ncontinue_token = response.metadata._continue\nif not continue_token:\n    break</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"kubernetes-f0565","card":"q-kubernetes-063-analyzing-resource-usage-with-python","sec":"kubernetes-s064","q":"A Python script that lists the Kubernetes API times out on a large cluster. What are the options and what are the risks of each?","a":"<p>Filter on the server with namespace, label and field selectors. Paginate with <code>limit</code>. Raise the client timeout with <code>_request_timeout</code>. Use a watch or a cached informer instead of repeated full lists. Parallel requests with <code>concurrent.futures</code> speed things up, but too many can hit API Priority and Fairness limits and return 429, so cap the workers and back off.</p>","hand":true}];
